      \draw [->] (2) to node[auto,labelsize] {forgetful}  (3);
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
whose composite is denoted by $\MMat\colon \VDbl\to\VDbl$.
In other words, $\MMat$ is the polynomial $2$-functor $\mathcal{P}(P_\hc)$ (see \cref{subsec:poly}), induced by the polynomial \eqref{eqn:polynomial-for-Mat}. 

The construction in the proof of \cref{prop:disc-opfib-powerful} allows us to describe the $2$-functor $\MMat$ explicitly:
it maps each $\vdcat{A}\in\VDbl$ to $\enMMat{\vdcat{A}}\in\VDbl$, which is the virtual double category described as follows.
\begin{itemize}
    \item The vertical category of $\enMMat{\vdcat{A}}$ is the category $\Fam(\vdcat{A}_\vc)$ of families of the vertical category $\vdcat{A}_\vc$ of $\vdcat{A}$. Hence an object in $\enMMat{\vdcat{A}}$ is a pair $(I,\vect{A})$ of a small set $I$ and an $I$-indexed family $\vect{A}=(A_i)_{i\in I}$ of objects in $\vdcat{A}$, while a vertical morphism in $\enMMat{\vdcat{A}}$ from $(I,\vect{A})$ to $(I',\vect{A}')$ is a pair $(v,\vect{f})$ of a function $v\colon I\to I'$ and a family $\vect{f}=(f_i\colon A_i\to A'_{v(i)})_{i\in I}$ of vertical morphisms in $\vdcat{A}$. 
