\section{Introduction}\label{section:Introduction}

At the heart of modern theoretical physics lies the principle of local gauge invariance, a powerful idea that dictates the fundamental interactions of nature by elevating global symmetries to local, spacetime-dependent ones~\cite{Forger2012}. One of the most well-established gauge theories in physics is classical electromagnetism, formulated as a $U(1)$ gauge theory. Its mathematical foundation rests on the geometry of principal bundles and connections, offering a prototypical example of how gauge symmetry can encode physical interactions~\cite{Hamilton2017}. By extending this geometric framework to incorporate local scale transformations—homotheties—we aim to generalize the notion of gauge invariance itself. Such a generalization offers novel geometric and conceptual reinterpretations of gauge structure, aligning with recent insights into the foundations of gauge symmetries and their reformulations~\cite{Francois2023}.

The paper establishes a new \emph{homothetic gauge theory} where the gauge freedom corresponds to local changes of scale. The central innovation is the linear structure endowed upon scale transformations, which naturally integrates into the standard fiber bundle formalism. It occurs by embedding the tangent and cotangent bundles in the doubled bundles, anchored by a fixed but arbitrary reference vector field. It transforms the non-linear action of scaling into a linear matrix operation on the fibers of these extended bundles. 

This linearization permits the definition of an infinite-dimensional Lie group of fiber-wise homothetic transformations, which serves as the gauge group of the theory. We then proceed to build the essential machinery of a gauge theory upon this foundation: a compatible connection, a covariant derivative that respects the homothetic gauge symmetry, and an associated field-strength (curvature) tensor.

In the paper, the Weyl integrable geometry is obtained as a special case of the general formulation, and it is shown that a homothetic cohomology is definable consistent with this very conformal geometry, resulting in a generalized de Rham complex. When applied to the context of electromagnetism, the theory yields a generalization of Maxwell's equations. These "homothetic Maxwell equations" feature new interaction terms coupling the electromagnetic field to the gradient of the scalar gauge field that parameterizes the local scale.

Furthermore, the theory offers a novel and compelling resolution to one of the most persistent problems in classical field theory: the infinite self-energy of a point charge. By formulating the Laplace and Poisson equations within this homothetic framework, we demonstrate that a point source can be modeled as a non-singular object. The divergence is regularized not by altering the fundamental laws of electrodynamics at short distances, but by redefining the nature of a source as a boundary condition on an infinitesimal, penalized interface. This geometric regularization emerges naturally from the theory without ad hoc modifications.

While the use of doubled geometries and extended bundles is not new---notably appearing in string theory (e.g., double field theory)~\cite{Hull2009} and generalized geometry~\cite{Gualtieri2004}---our focus on homotheties as a gauge symmetry sets this work apart. Additionally, conformal gauge theories~\cite{Kaku1977} often emphasize angle-preserving transformations, whereas we highlight scale-preserving yet non-isometric maps. The proposed framework thus contributes a unique mathematical structure to the study of spacetime symmetries and potential extensions of general relativity and gauge theory.

The present work was initially began as a penalty method, a mathematical technique aiming to impose the solid boundary on the Navier–Stokes equations via controlling the dynamics of the equations \cite{Sabetghadam2015,Badri2020}. However, due to its applicability to Maxwell's equations— as an exact field equation — subsequent geometric interpretations of the method emerged \cite{Sabetghadam2020}. In fact, penalty methods have been widely used for decades in computational physics as mathematical tools, sometimes accompanied by some physical interpretations \cite{Mittal2008}. From this perspective, the present theory can be regarded as a geometric interpretation of a particular penalty method for Maxwell's equations and their subset equations.\\

{\bf An Operational Roadmap of the Paper}: We begin by interpreting conformal geometry as a special case of a broader homothetic framework---specifically, as a homothetic geometry with a zero homothety center. This perspective allows us to elevate local scale transformations to a general gauge symmetry. To facilitate this, we introduce a novel mathematical structure based on doubled tangent and cotangent bundles, which linearizes the action of the homothetic group. Upon this foundation, we construct a set of gauge-covariant differential operators, leading to a generalized de Rham complex and an associated Hodge decomposition theorem, which ensures the mathematical consistency of the resulting field theories. As a primary application, we apply this formalism to classical electromagnetism, yielding a set of homothetically covariant Maxwell equations. A distinctive feature of these equations is the emergence of interaction terms that couple the electromagnetic field to the scalar gauge field governing the local scale. These terms persist even under simplifying assumptions and function as geometric penalty terms. This structure allows for the imposition of boundary conditions directly within the field equations, thereby reformulating the theory in a sourceless, singularity-free manner. By imposing a generalized Lorenz gauge condition, we derive homothetic wave and Laplace equations that naturally encode boundary data. This framework is shown to be equivalent to solving classical Dirichlet or Neumann problems via a volumetric penalization method. Finally, we leverage this result to address the classical problem of the infinite self-energy of a point charge. By modeling the source as a penalized Dirichlet boundary condition on a shrinking interface, we construct a non-singular electrostatic potential, resolving the divergence in a manner that emerges naturally from the geometry.\\

This paper is structured as follows. Section~\ref{section:2-First setup} introduces the \emph{block–homothetic gauge group} and the doubled-bundle construction that renders local scale transformations linear. Section~\ref{section:3-A generalized} embeds this algebraic setup in a generalized Weyl-integrable geometry, deriving the compatible connection, curvature, and homothetic cohomology.  
In Section~\ref{section:4- Homothetic Hodge} we develop the homothetic de Rham complex and prove an extended Hodge decomposition that underpins the subsequent field theory.  Section~\ref{section:Homot_Elect} specializes the formalism to electromagnetism, obtaining homothetically covariant Maxwell equations and a modified Lorentz force. Section~\ref{section: Homot-Cohomo} then derives the associated conservation law together with the homothetic wave and Laplace equations, while Section~\ref{section:7} analyses their Cauchy, Dirichlet and Neumann boundary-value problems. These results are applied in Section~\ref{section:8} to construct a \emph{non-singular} electrostatic potential for a point charge. Section~\ref{sec:conclusions} summarises the main findings. For the sake of continuity, some merely technical materials are gathered in four appendices at the end of the paper. 
%--------------------------------------------
\section{First setup: Block-homothetic Transformations}\label{section:2-First setup}

From the mathematical viewpoint, we want to have homothetic transformations with linear operations; and it is achievable via embedding the tangent bundles in the doubled spaces. Let $(M,\eta)$ denote $(1+3)$-dimensional Minkowski spacetime, with metric $\eta := \mathrm{diag}(-,+,+,+)$.

We begin by defining a canonical embedding of tangent and cotangent bundles. For each $x \in M$, consider the tangent space $T_xM$. For a fixed, future-directed, timelike vector $X_d \in T_xM$, we consider a fiber-wise embedding:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:tangent-embed}
T_xM \hookrightarrow T_xM \oplus T_xM,
\qquad
X \mapsto \bar{X}
= \begin{pmatrix}
X \\ X_d
\end{pmatrix}, \qquad \forall X \in T_xM.
\end{equation}
This embedding is well-defined for each $x \in M$ and turns the homothetic transformations to the linear ones in the extended bundle $TM \oplus TM$. Note that since $X_d$ is fixed, $\bar{X}$ defines a section in the Whitney-sum bundle $TM \oplus TM$. 

Similarly, for the cotangent space, using the musical isomorphism $\flat: TM \to T^*M$ induced by $\eta$, given $X^\flat_d:=\eta(X_d)$, we define:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:cotangent-embed}
T_x^*M \hookrightarrow T_x^*M \oplus T_x^*M,
\qquad
X^{\flat} \mapsto \bar{X}^{\flat}
:= \begin{pmatrix}
X^{\flat} \\ X^{\flat}_d
\end{pmatrix}, \qquad X^{\flat}:=\eta(X) \in T_x^*M.
\end{equation}

We now define an infinite-dimensional group of pointwise homothetic transformations acting fiber-wise. Let:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H} := \left\{
\Lambda[\lambda] \mid \lambda \in C^\infty(M,\mathbb{R})
\right\}, \qquad
\Lambda[\lambda](x) :=
\begin{pmatrix}
e^{-\lambda(x)} & 1 - e^{-\lambda(x)} \\
0 & 1
\end{pmatrix},
\end{equation}
where the group operation is given by pointwise matrix multiplication:
\begin{align*}
(\Lambda[\lambda_1] \Lambda[\lambda_2])(x)
&= \Lambda[\lambda_1 + \lambda_2](x), \\
\Lambda[0](x) &= \mathbb{I}_2 =
\begin{pmatrix}
1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1
\end{pmatrix}.
\end{align*}
As one can see, $\mathcal{H}$ is isomorphic, as a Lie group, to the additive group $C^\infty(M,\mathbb{R})$ under pointwise addition.

The induced \emph{fiber-wise homothety} on tangent vectors is then:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:homothetic-u}
\hat{X}
:= \begin{pmatrix} \tilde{X} \\ X_d \end{pmatrix}
:= \Lambda[\lambda] \cdot \bar{X}
= \begin{pmatrix}
e^{-\lambda(x)} X + \left(1 - e^{-\lambda(x)}\right) X_d \\
X_d
\end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation}
This map is well-defined since all components live in the same fiber $T_xM$. The first component realizes a homothetic transformation centered at $X_d$ with scale $e^{-\lambda(x)}$, while the second component remains fixed; and as one can see, the transformation is linear on the extended space.

Analogously, we define the action of $\mathcal H$ on the dual space:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:homothetic-alpha}
\hat{X}^\flat
:= \begin{pmatrix} \tilde{X}^{\flat} \\ X_d^\flat \end{pmatrix}
:= \Lambda[\lambda] \cdot \bar{X}^\flat
= \begin{pmatrix}
e^{-\lambda(x)} X^\flat + \left(1 - e^{-\lambda(x)}\right) X_d^\flat \\
X_d^\flat
\end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation}

It should be noted that $\mathcal H$ has left actions on both extended fibers $TM \oplus TM$ and $T^*M \oplus T^*M$.

We then define a diagonal subgroup of $\mathcal{H} \oplus \mathcal{H}$ to capture simultaneous homothetic action on both vectors and covectors:
\begin{equation}
{\bf H} := \left\{
(\Lambda, \Lambda) \mid \Lambda \in \mathcal{H}
\right\} = \Delta(\mathcal{H}) \subset \mathcal{H} \oplus \mathcal{H}.
\end{equation}
Let $\bar{\bf X} := \begin{pmatrix} \bar{X} \\ \bar{X}^\flat \end{pmatrix}$ and $\hat{\bf X} := \begin{pmatrix} \hat{X} \\ \hat{X}^\flat \end{pmatrix}$. Then:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:combined-action}
\hat{\bf X} = 
\begin{pmatrix}
\Lambda & 0 \\
0 & \Lambda
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
\bar{X} \\
\bar{X}^\flat
\end{pmatrix}
= {\bf H} \cdot \bar{\bf X}.
\end{equation}

We now equip the extended bundle $TM \oplus TM$ with the metric $\bar{\eta} := \eta \oplus \eta$. This allows us to extend the musical isomorphism fiber-wise:
\begin{equation}
\bar{\flat}_x : T_xM \oplus T_xM \to T_x^*M \oplus T_x^*M, \qquad
\bar{X} \mapsto \bar{X}^{\bar{\flat}} := (\eta(X), \eta(X_d)) = (X^\flat, X_d^\flat).
\end{equation}
This will play a central role in the homothetic Hodge decomposition developed in section~\ref{section:4- Homothetic Hodge}.
