Finally, for general differential forms $\phi, \phi_d \in \Omega^k(M)$ with $0 \leq k \leq 4$, where $\phi_d$ is fixed, we define the embedding:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:k-forms-embed}
\Omega^k(M) \hookrightarrow \Omega^k(M) \oplus \Omega^k(M),
\qquad
\phi \mapsto \bar{\phi}
= \begin{pmatrix}
\phi \\ \phi_d
\end{pmatrix}, \qquad \forall \phi \in \Omega(M).
\end{equation}
And $\mathcal H$ has a well-defined pointwise action on $\bar{\phi}$:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:k-forms-embed1}
\hat{\phi} := \Lambda \cdot \bar{\phi}
= \begin{pmatrix}
\tilde{\phi} \\
\phi_d
\end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix}
e^{-\lambda(x)} \phi + \left(1 - e^{-\lambda(x)}\right) \phi_d \\
\phi_d
\end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation}
This construction extends naturally to bundles of differential forms, allowing us to analyze gauge-like homotheties and their induced effects on geometric structures such as the Hodge decomposition.

The above construction, while self-contained, is not arbitrary. As we demonstrate in Appendix \ref{app:cartan}, this entire framework arises naturally as a specific gauge fixing within the standard formulation of \emph{conformal Cartan geometry}, providing a deeper geometric justification for our approach. Moreover, in appendix \ref{app:principal_H_bundle} the connection and curvature of $\mathcal{H}$ bundle, as a principal bundle, are obtained.
