\section{A Generalized Weyl Integrable Geometry}\label{section:3-A generalized}

On the extended space $\bar{\bf X}$, a symmetry manifests itself: the transformation $\bar{\bf X} \to \hat{\bf X} = {\bf H}\bar{\bf X}$ preserves the musical isomorphism $\bar{\flat}$. In fact, one can verify that $\bar{\flat}$ and $\Lambda$ commute:
\begin{equation}\label{Commut_1}
\Lambda \bar{X}^{\bar{\flat}} = \Lambda \bar{\eta} \bar{X} = \bar{\eta}(\Lambda \bar{X}) = (\Lambda \bar{X})^{\bar{\flat}}.
\end{equation}
However, this does not imply that the lengths and angles of vectors are preserved under the transformation $\bar{\bf X} \to \hat{\bf X}$—only that they remain unchanged within a local Lorentz frame. To make this distinction precise, we define the effective (generalized Weyl integrable) metric $\tilde{\eta}$ for any $X, Y \in TM$ via:
\begin{equation}
\tilde{\eta}(X, Y) := \eta(\tilde{X}, \tilde{Y}),
\end{equation}
where $\tilde{X}$ and $\tilde{Y}$ are given by the homothetic action in Eq.~(\ref{eq:homothetic-u}). A direct computation yields:
\begin{equation}\label{GWI_metric_full}
\tilde{\eta}(X,Y) = e^{-2\lambda} \eta(X,Y) + (1 - e^{-\lambda})^2 \eta(X_d,X_d) + e^{-\lambda}(1 - e^{-\lambda})[\eta(X,X_d) + \eta(Y,X_d)].
\end{equation}

This metric generalizes the usual conformal structure of Weyl integrable geometry by incorporating an explicit \emph{offset vector} $X_d \in TM$. For $\lambda = 0$, the effective metric reduces to $\tilde{\eta} = \eta$, and for $\lambda \to \infty$, the metric degenerates:
\begin{equation}
\tilde{\eta}(X,Y) \to \eta(X_d, X_d),
\end{equation}
independently of $X$ and $Y$. The structure encoded in Eq.~(\ref{GWI_metric_full}) thus reflects a homothetic-invariant geometry, one that naturally combines a local scaling $\lambda(x)$ with a fiberwise translation defined by $X_d(x)$, consistent with the action of group $\mathcal H$.

While this offset structure is geometrically natural and consistent with our next cohomological framework, it is hardly interpretable physically. The general form of the metric in Eq.~(\ref{GWI_metric_full}), with $X_d \neq 0$, represents the full mathematical structure that arises from the linearization of the homothetic map. It is the most direct consequence of our initial setup. However, to ensure that this effective metric $\tilde{\eta}$ can be interpreted as a physical spacetime metric, it must satisfy certain fundamental axioms. In particular, the presence of $X_d$ in $\tilde{\eta}$ means that even the zero vector $X = 0$ can acquire a nontrivial norm—a violation of the identity of indiscernibles and an obstruction to interpreting $\tilde{\eta}$ as a standard spacetime metric. Therefore, to recover a physical spacetime geometry that supports unambiguous geodesic separation, we impose a physicality condition by restricting the general geometric structure to the subspace where the offset vector vanishes. This is achievable without altering the foundational structure by choosing:
\begin{equation}
X_d = 0,
\end{equation}
which leads to the conventional conformal geometry:
\begin{equation}\label{GWI_metric_reduced}
\boxed{
\tilde{\eta}(X,Y) = e^{-2\lambda(x)} \eta(X,Y).}
\end{equation}
That is, to this point, \emph {the conformal geometry is interpreted as a homothetic geometry with zero homothety center}.

However, we see this conformal geometry as a Weyl integrable geometry \cite{Scholz2011} because we want to use it in generalizing the cohomology. Recall that a Weyl integrable geometry is defined by the triple $(M, [\eta], \phi)$, where $[\eta]$ is the conformal class of Lorentzian metrics and $\phi := d\lambda$ is a globally defined, closed 1-form satisfying
\begin{equation}
\nabla_\rho \eta_{\mu\nu} = -2\phi_\rho \eta_{\mu\nu}.
\end{equation}
This non-metricity condition defines a Weyl-compatible connection that preserves null directions but allows local rescaling governed by $\phi$. In our formulation, both the conformal metric and the 1-form $\phi := d\lambda$ arise naturally from the homothetic structure, with $\lambda \in C^\infty(M)$ arbitrary and smooth. Thus, the effective geometry obtained from Eq.~(\ref{GWI_metric_reduced}) is not merely analogous to Weyl integrable geometry—it is precisely one, derived from a block-homothetic framework.

\subsection*{About the Physical Interpretation of \boldmath$\lambda$}

In the absence of any other valid physical interpretation, we posit that the physical interpretation of this conformal frame is that of a spacetime with a variable speed of light (VSL). Establishing a  relation between the conformal transformation and the variable speed of light is almost trivial, for example, one can see the following.

Consider the normalization of the four-velocity $u^\mu$ for a massive particle. In the physical frame described by $\tilde{\eta}_{\mu\nu}$, the squared norm is given by $\tilde{\eta}_{\mu\nu} u^\mu u^\nu$. To maintain a canonical structure where this norm is identified with the negative square of the local speed of light, we must define a position-dependent speed of light $c(x)$ such that:
\begin{equation}
    \tilde{\eta}_{\mu\nu} u^\mu u^\nu = -c(x)^2.
\end{equation}
Comparing with $\eta_{\mu\nu} u^\mu u^\nu = -1$, this naturally leads to the identification:
\begin{equation}
    c(x) = e^{-\lambda(x)}.
    \label{eq:vsl_relation}
\end{equation}
This VSL interpretation is further substantiated in the limit where $\lambda(x) \to \infty$. In this case, $c(x) \to 0$ and the metric components $\tilde{\eta}_{\mu\nu} \to 0$. The collapse of all spacetime intervals signifies a degeneration of the causal structure where propagation is impossible, consistent with a vanishing speed of light.

Crucially, the existence of a relativistic kinematic framework, regardless of the local value of $c(x)$, allows for the definition of its two fundamental degenerate limits. At any spacetime point $x$, the local physics is governed by a Lorentz algebra characterized by the finite, non-zero constant $c(x)$. The Galilean and Carrollian kinematics can be understood as distinct Inönü--Wigner contractions \cite{Inonu1953} of this local Poincaré algebra, corresponding to the limits where the structure constant $c(x)$ is taken to infinity or zero, respectively.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{The Galilean Limit ($c(x_0) \to \infty$):} This contraction yields the Galilean algebra $\mathfrak{g}(1,3)$. Physically, it corresponds to the causal cone opening up completely, allowing for absolute simultaneity and instantaneous propagation of signals.
    \item \textbf{The Carrollian Limit ($c(x_0) \to 0$):} This contraction yields the Carrollian algebra $\mathfrak{c}(1,3)$. Physically, it corresponds to the causal cone collapsing to a one-dimensional line, forbidding motion and rendering causality ultra-local.
\end{itemize}
This establishes that the Galilean and Carrollian frameworks are universally definable as conceptual limits from any local relativistic standpoint. The role of the field $\lambda(x)$ is then to dictate the \textit{physical character} of a given spacetime region. While an observer can always conceive of both limits, a region where $\lambda(x) \to \infty$ will see its physical dynamics---its particle propagation and causal structure---asymptotically approach the behavior described by the idealized Carrollian limit \cite{Levy1965}.
