\section{Homothetic Hodge Decomposition}\label{section:4- Homothetic Hodge}
In this section, we study the behavior of differential forms in the homothetically transformed geometry introduced previously. Rather than focusing on traditional invariance properties, we consider a class of \emph{homothetically transformed} differential forms, which lead naturally to a generalized de Rham complex and cohomology, and a generalized Hodge decomposition, adapted to the structure. We begin with the case of 1-forms, establishing both their physical interpretation and mathematical formulation. Other $k\neq 1$ ranks will be addressed in subsequent subsections.
%----------------------
\subsection{The Homothetically Transformed 1-Forms}\label{sub-section:4.1}

Finally, the present paper intends to analyze electromagnetism in which the (Ehresmann) connection, on which we can put physically meaningful constraints, is ${\mathfrak u}(1)$-valued 1-forms $A=A_\nu dx^\nu\in \Omega^1(M)$. Therefore, we begin here with 1-forms, albeit in a more general setting which will be suppressed to the potential gauge fields $A$ in $\S$ \ref{section:Homot_Elect}.   

In the conventional formulation of conformal geometry, the spacetime metric rescaling is a postulation occurring independently of the definition of other fields. But here, the conformal rescaling $\tilde{\eta} \to e^{-2\lambda}\eta$ is a \emph{consequence} of a more fundamental transformation acting on the tangent and cotangent bundles. Since a 1-form $\alpha$ is a section of this very cotangent bundle, it is not only legitimate but more consistent within our framework to posit that it transforms accordingly. We therefore define the action of the homothetic group $\mathcal{H}$ on the space of extended 1-forms. For a general $\alpha \in \Omega^1(M)$, its transformed counterpart $\tilde{\alpha}$ is given by the homothetic transformation:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:1-form-transform}
\tilde{\alpha} := e^{-\lambda(x)} \alpha + \left(1 - e^{-\lambda(x)}\right)\alpha_d,
\end{equation}
where $\alpha_d \in \Omega^1(M)$ is a fixed, globally defined 1-form that serves as the homothety center, in direct analogy to the covector $X^{\flat}_d$ in the transformation of cotangent spaces. Note that when $\alpha_d = 0$, this reduces to the familiar Weyl-type scaling $\tilde{\alpha} = e^{-W\lambda(x)}\alpha$, with the Weyl weight $W=1$. But in contrast to $X_d$, here we will keep $\alpha_d$ non-zero in general. 

Before continuing, it is important to note that in this formulation, a homothetically invariant 1-form is obtained by setting:
\begin{equation}
\alpha = \alpha_d,
\end{equation}
that is, $\tilde{\alpha}$ for $\lambda(x) = 0$ equals $\tilde{\alpha}$ for $\lambda(x) \to \infty$, which means that changing $\lambda(x)$ does not affect $\alpha$. This can also be derived from definition (\ref{eq:1-form-transform}):
\begin{equation}
\tilde{\alpha} = e^{-\lambda(x)}(\alpha - \alpha_d) + \alpha_d = e^{-\lambda(x)}(\alpha_d - \alpha_d) + \alpha_d = \alpha_d.
\end{equation}
As will be seen, even for homothetically invariant forms (such as electromagnetic gauge potentials, as discussed in §\ref{section:Homot_Elect}), the general homothetic formulation reveals new and interesting features, as it localizes the values of $\alpha$.

The physical motivation and mathematical justification for the homothetic transformation (\ref{eq:1-form-transform}) are now in order.

\medskip

\noindent \textbf{Physical motivation.} Consider the limiting case $\lambda(x) \to \infty$, where the conformal factor goes to zero, $e^{-2\lambda(x)} \to 0$, and hence the associated volume form goes to zero as well $(d\text{vol}_{\tilde{\eta}})_x \to 0$. Now, in order to have a theory, being valid in all scales, the functions on $(d\text{vol}_{\tilde{\eta}})_x$ must converges to a single well-defined value $(\alpha_d)_x$. Note that such a homothetic behavior may define a kind of homogenization, that is,  the transformation in Eq.~(\ref{eq:1-form-transform}) ensures that for any initial 1-form $\alpha$, its transform $\tilde{\alpha}$ approaches the fixed form $\alpha_d$:
$$
\lim_{\lambda \to \infty} \tilde{\alpha}(x) = \alpha_d(x).
$$

This principle thus endows the asymptotic field $\alpha_d$ with a clear physical interpretation: it is the universal 1-form to which all others are driven as the local spacetime structure degenerates. The existence of such a non-trivial asymptotic field is a profound consequence of the homothetic framework. 

%When specialized to electromagnetism, $\alpha_d$ becomes an offset potential $A_d$. This universal field might be interpreted as a reference background potential that sets a baseline for electromagnetic phenomena, becoming physically dominant in extreme regimes (i.e., as $\lambda(x) \to \infty$) where familiar spacetime intervals vanish.

\medskip

\noindent \textbf{Mathematical formalism.} This intuition can be rigorously framed using contraction on the cotangent bundle. Define, at each point $x \in M$, a fiberwise homothetic map:
\begin{equation}
{\mathbf T}_{\lambda(x)} : T_x^*M \to T_x^*M, \qquad \alpha \mapsto {\mathbf T}_{\lambda(x)}(\alpha) := e^{-\lambda(x)}\alpha + \left(1 - e^{-\lambda(x)}\right) \alpha_d.
\end{equation}
Endow each fiber $T_x^*M$ with the norm $\|\cdot\|_x$ induced by the original Minkowski metric $\eta$. Then for $\lambda(x) > 0$, ${\mathbf T}_{\lambda(x)}$ is a strict contraction with Lipschitz constant $e^{-\lambda(x)} < 1$. By the Banach fixed-point theorem, ${\mathbf T}_{\lambda(x)}$ has a unique fixed point in each fiber, which is precisely $\alpha_d$. Moreover, as $\lambda(x) \to \infty$, ${\mathbf T}_{\lambda(x)}$ converges to a projection map that sends every 1-form to $\alpha_d$:
\begin{equation}
\lim_{\lambda(x)\to\infty} {\mathbf T}_{\lambda(x)}(\alpha) = \alpha_d, \quad \forall \alpha \in T_x^*M.
\end{equation}

This construction defines a smooth family of fiberwise contractions across $M$, forming a pointwise contraction semigroup. In the limit $\lambda(x)\to\infty$, $\tilde{\alpha}$ thus enforces homogenization of all 1-forms to a single invariant form. 

Now, as we mentioned before (c.f., Eqns. (\ref{eq:k-forms-embed}) and (\ref{eq:k-forms-embed1})), the above homothetic transformation can be linearized via the embedding $\Omega^k(M) \hookrightarrow \Omega^k(M) \oplus \Omega^k(M)$, that is:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:embedd-forms-my-1}
\hat{\alpha} := \Lambda \cdot \bar{\alpha}
= \begin{pmatrix}
\tilde{\alpha} \\
\alpha_d
\end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix}
e^{-\lambda(x)} \alpha + \left(1 - e^{-\lambda(x)}\right) \alpha_d \\
\alpha_d
\end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation}
The implications of this contraction will become central in the upcoming discussion of generalized Hodge theory.

What happen to the other differential forms is the question that will be addressed in the next subsection, after developing some mathematical tools we need for their analyses.  
%------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Generalized de Rham complex and Hodge decomposition}\label{sub-section:4.2}

The key step in dealing with the homothetically transformed differential forms is the appropriate construction of the differential operators on this geometry. In this regard, we first define:
\begin{equation}
\bar{d} := 
\begin{pmatrix}
d & 0 \\
0 & d
\end{pmatrix}, \quad
\bar{\delta} :=
\begin{pmatrix}
\delta & 0 \\
0 & \delta
\end{pmatrix}, \quad
\bar{\Delta} :=
\begin{pmatrix}
\Delta & 0 \\
0 & \Delta
\end{pmatrix},\qquad\
\bar{\star} := 
\begin{pmatrix}
\star & 0 \\
0 & \star
\end{pmatrix}, \quad
\end{equation}
where $d$ is the exterior derivative, $\delta$ is the codifferential, $\Delta = d\delta + \delta d$ is the Hodge–de Rham Laplacian, and $\star$ is the Hodge star operator. Then, we define these operators on the homothetically transformed space of differential forms as:

\textbf{Generalized exterior derivative}:
\begin{equation}
\hat{d} :=
\begin{pmatrix}
d + (d\lambda) \wedge & -(d\lambda) \wedge \\
0 & d
\end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation}

\textbf{Generalized codifferential}:
\begin{equation}
\hat{\delta} :=
\begin{pmatrix}
\delta - * (d\lambda) \wedge * &  * (d\lambda) \wedge * \\
0 & \delta
\end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation}

\textbf{Generalized Laplacian}:
\begin{equation}
\hat{\Delta} := \hat{\delta} \hat{d} + \hat{d} \hat{\delta}.
\end{equation}

\textbf{Block Hodge star operator}:
\begin{equation}
\hat{\star}:=\bar{\star}.
\end{equation}

The key features of these operators are that (it can be verified directly):
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:commut-diff-forms}
\boxed{
\hat{d}\Lambda=\Lambda\bar{d}, \qquad \hat{\delta}\Lambda=\Lambda\bar{\delta}, \qquad \hat{\Delta}\Lambda=\Lambda\bar{\Delta}, \qquad \bar{\star}\Lambda=\Lambda\bar{\star},}
\end{equation}

and as the direct consequences of these features, one can verify that irrespective of the rank:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Both $\hat{d}$ and $\hat{\delta}$ are \emph{nilpotent}, that is, $\hat{d}^2=0$ and $\hat{\delta}^2=0$.
\item With the Weyl-rescaled metric $\tilde{\eta}=e^{-2\lambda(x)}\eta$, $\hat{\delta}$ is the \emph{adjoint} of $\hat{d}$, that is, $\langle \hat{d}\hat{\alpha},\hat{\beta}\rangle_{\tilde{\eta}}=\langle\hat{\alpha},\hat{\delta}\hat{\beta}\rangle_{\tilde{\eta}}$.
\item As a consequence of adjointness of $\hat{d}$ and $\hat{\delta}$, one can say that $\hat{\Delta}$ is \emph{elliptic}.
\item The block Hodge star operator $\bar{\star}$ and $\Lambda$ commute without any further modification. 
\end{enumerate}

\medskip

\noindent\textbf{Geometric and Physical Interpretations.} The generalized operators $\hat{d}, \hat{\delta}, \hat{\Delta}$, $\hat{\star}$ are not arbitrary: they arise naturally from the demand that the differential structure be compatible with the homothetic transformations introduced earlier. Specifically, the exterior derivative and codifferential must transform \emph{covariantly} under the homothetic action $\Lambda$, preserving the structure of the deformed forms $\hat{\phi} = \Lambda \bar{\phi}$. This compatibility is encoded in the identities (\ref{eq:commut-diff-forms}) which imply that homothetic transformations commute with the differential operators, effectively rendering the homothetic structure \emph{gauge covariant}.

Physically, this suggests that the transformation group $\mathcal{H}$ plays a role analogous to a gauge group, albeit non-compact and infinite-dimensional. The field $\lambda(x)$ behaves like a scalar gauge parameter—more precisely, a logarithmic scale factor—whose differential $d\lambda$ enters the transformation of both $d$ and $\delta$ in a manner reminiscent of a connection 1-form in Weyl geometry. The antisymmetric contributions involving $d\lambda \wedge$ and $* d\lambda \wedge *$ indicate that this is a kind of \emph{abelian connection with scale-coupled torsion}.

Moreover, the ellipticity of $\hat{\Delta}$ ensures that the generalized Laplacian still governs the dynamics of field propagation and harmonic analysis on the manifold. From a gauge-theoretic point of view, this confirms that no essential degrees of freedom are lost through the transformation: instead, they are reorganized in a manner compatible with the homothetic group action.

\medskip

