\noindent\textbf{Cohomological Consequences.} Importantly, the nilpotency of $\hat{d}$ allows us to define a generalized de Rham cohomology:
\[
\hat{H}^k(M) := \ker(\hat{d}^k)/\text{Im}(\hat{d}^{k-1}),
\]
which encodes the topological information of the manifold as filtered through the homothetic structure. While the underlying topology of $M$ remains unchanged, the representatives of each cohomology class are now deformed by the scalar field $\lambda(x)$ and the offset $\alpha_d$. When $\lambda = 0$, all modifications vanish and we recover standard de Rham theory. However, for $\lambda \neq 0$, the cohomology classes are parametrized by homothetically equivalent forms.

This leads us to the generalized Hodge decomposition:
\begin{equation}\label{Hom_Hodg_Dec}
\boxed{
\hat{\Omega}^k(M) = \text{Im}~\hat{d}^{k-1}~ \oplus~ \text{ker}~\hat{\Delta}^k~ \oplus ~\text{Im}~\hat{\delta}^{k+1},}
\end{equation}
in which the space of homothetically transformed $k$-forms splits orthogonally into exact, coexact, and harmonic components, all defined relative to the Weyl-rescaled metric $\tilde{\eta} = e^{-2\lambda(x)}\eta$. This decomposition underlies the forthcoming formulation of field equations—most notably, generalized Maxwell-type theories—in which the harmonic representatives encode gauge-invariant field configurations within the homothetic background. A functional analytic proof of decomposition (\ref{Hom_Hodg_Dec}) is provided in appendix \ref{app:hodge_proof}.
