\section{Conclusions}\label{sec:conclusions}
This work has introduced a novel gauge theory of scale, placing local homotheties on an equal footing with the familiar internal symmetries that underpin the Standard Model. By constructing a formalism where local scale transformations act linearly, we have developed a self-contained and consistent framework with significant physical implications. The principal results of this investigation demonstrate the theory's viability and its potential to resolve long-standing issues in classical physics:

\begin{itemize}
  \item A generalized Weyl-integrable geometry emerges naturally from our framework, where the spacetime metric dynamically responds to a scalar gauge field, \(\lambda\), that dictates the local scale.

  \item We established a homothetic de Rham complex and an associated Hodge decomposition. This mathematical backbone guarantees that the resulting field equations are well-posed and that their physical solutions are unique.

  \item Applying the theory to electromagnetism yields homothetically covariant sourceless Maxwell equations. These equations have some additional terms coupling the electromagnetic field with the gradient of the scale gauge field.

  \item A key achievement is a resolution to the century-old problem of the infinite self-energy of a point charge. Our model replaces the singularity with a regularized, penalized boundary condition on an infinitesimal interface---a result that emerges directly from the theory's geometric foundations, not from ad-hoc modifications.

  \item The entire gauge structure was shown to have a rigorous interpretation within the established framework of Cartan geometry, clarifying its deep connection to the conformal group and providing a basis for further geometric analysis.
\end{itemize}

Taken together, these findings present homothetic gauge theory as more than a mathematical curiosity; it is a workable extension of classical field theory. The future avenues for exploration are rich and varied. Integrating HGT with metric dynamics could offer a novel path toward modifying general relativity, where cosmological phenomena might be linked to gauge artifacts of the scale field. Furthermore, coupling the homothetic connection to non-Abelian gauge groups could unveil new scale-dependent physics relevant to the strong and weak interactions. The theoretical and computational tools developed here provide a solid foundation for pursuing these exciting possibilities.
