\section{Kummer function for small argument}
In this appendix we illustrate the expansion of the Kummer $U$ function (also known as confluent hypergeometric function or simply Tricomi's function) $U(a,b,z)$ is defined as \cite{ab64}
\begin{eqnarray}
    U(a,b,z)=\frac{\Gamma\left(1-b\right)}{\Gamma\left(1+a-b\right)}M(a,b,z)+\frac{\Gamma\left(b-1\right)}{\Gamma\left(a\right)}z^{1-b}M(a+1-b,2-b,z)
    \label{U}
\end{eqnarray}
where
\begin{eqnarray}
    M(a,b,z)=\frac{\Gamma(b)}{\Gamma(a)}\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{\Gamma\left(a+n\right)}{\Gamma\left(b+n\right)}\frac{z^{n}}{n!}
    \label{M}
\end{eqnarray}
is the Kummer $M$ function. We are interested specifically in the case $a=1$. Inserting the series expansion \eqref{M} into \eqref{U} we can obtain the series expansion for $U(a,b,z)$. Thus, depending on the values of $b$ the leading terms in the limit $z\ll 1$ are 
\begin{eqnarray}
U(1,b,z)=\left\{ \begin{array}{lll}
\frac{1}{1-b}+\frac{z}{b(1-b)}+\dots,\quad & b<0,\\
\frac{1}{1-b}-\frac{\Gamma\left(b\right)}{1-b}z^{1-b}+\dots,\quad & 0<b<1,\\
\frac{\Gamma\left(b-1\right)}{z^{b-1}}+\dots,\quad & b>1.
\end{array}\right.
\label{Us0}
\end{eqnarray}




