Table \ref{tab:cross} shows the SSL downstream task performances for a variation of a combination of SSL models that is used to calculate SSL-MSE loss and that is used for the inference of SSL downstream tasks. 
Overall, WavLM {\sc Base+} and WavLM {\sc Large} demonstrate superior performance compared to other SSL models as the SSL models for inference. This is likely because these models are pre-trained with noise-aware strategies, making them more effective on test sets with additive noise.
For the SSL pipeline with WavLM {\sc Base+}, SSL-MSE loss calculated with WavLM {\sc Large} (c2) most improves the performance, and SSL-MSE losses calculated with wav2vec 2.0 {\sc Base} (d1) and Hubert {\sc Base} (d2) do not improve the performance compared with vanilla SNR loss training (b). Moreover, SSL-MSE losses calculated with wav2vec 2.0 {\sc Base} and Hubert {\sc Base} do not improve matched condition where the same SSL model is used for inference, which is shown in (e5) and (f5). 
% This seems to be because wav2vec and Hubert are not trained to be robust to noise; their pre-training procedure does not include manual addition of noise unlike wavLM {\sc Base+} and WavLM {\sc Large}~\citep{chen2022wavlm}. Thus, SSL-MSE training with wav2vec 2.0 and Hubert can impose SE model to eliminate all types of noise that may not affect the downstream tasks, and causes over-suppression and distortion.
