\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}

Modern investment management operates in an arena of high‑dimensional, tightly coupled, and rapidly evolving data.  Conventional quantitative techniques—most notably the seminal mean–variance framework of Markowitz~\cite{markowitz1952portfolio}—encapsulate the trade‑off between expected return and risk through a \emph{quadratic} objective that minimizes portfolio variance for a target mean return.  While mathematically elegant, this quadratic form becomes fragile when confronted with today’s realities: estimation error in large covariance matrices, non‑Gaussian return distributions, time‑varying correlations, and a proliferation of real‑world constraints such as turnover limits, liquidity frictions, and regulatory capital charges~\cite{fabozzi2007robust,black1992global,mantegna1999introduction,jegadeesh1993returns,fletcher2013practical}.

\paragraph{From quadratic programming to graph dynamics.}
Early econophysics research showed that a covariance matrix can be \emph{filtered} into a sparse network via minimum‑spanning trees or planar maximally filtered graphs whose topology reflects hidden economic sectors and contagion channels~\cite{mantegna1999hierarchical,onnela2003dynamic,TUMMINELLO201040}. Subsequent work demonstrated that risk propagation and systemic fragility travel along these edges much like a diffusion process~\cite{pozzi2013spread,kenett2012networks}. We therefore reinterpret the Markowitz objective as assigning a probability measure over such a graph: edge flows penalize high covariance, while self‑loops reward each asset’s risk‑adjusted return.  Capturing this diffusion explicitly motivates the textbf{dual-channel} approach pursued in this work.

\paragraph{Quantum stochastic walks as a dual‑channel engine}
Recent progress in quantum information science—including decoherence‑controlled \emph{quantum stochastic walks} (QSWs)~\cite{whitfield2010quantum,attal2012open}—and quantum network‑ranking algorithms~\cite{Eduardo2012quantum, paparo2013quantum, wang2022continuous, chruscinski2017gkls} makes it possible to merge coherent quantum evolution with classical random walks on the same graph.  The \textbf{coherent channel}, governed by a Hermitian Hamiltonian,  explores the network through superposition and interference, while a \textbf{stochastic channel} guarantees ergodicity and convergence.  By tuning the quantum–classical mixing parameter, QSWs interpolate smoothly between fully quantum exploration and purely classical diffusion, providing an adaptive mechanism well suited to financial systems that exhibit both structured dependencies and stochastic shocks.

\paragraph{Contributions of this work}
Building on these insights, we develop a \emph{quantum graph‐theoretic} framework for portfolio optimization that

\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
  \item maps mean returns, volatilities, and covariances to a weighted, directed graph whose self‑loops retain the asset‑specific Sharpe ratio and whose inter‑node edges penalize correlation;
  \item deploys a dual‑channel QSW to evolve a density matrix whose diagonal converges to steady‑state asset weights, thereby realizing the quadratic mean–risk balance \emph{via} graph dynamics;
  \item implements the algorithm on GPU hardware, enabling real‑time optimization over universes of 100 assets; and
  \item empirically benchmarks the resulting portfolios against maximum‑Sharpe mean–variance solutions and the capitalization‑weighted S\&P 500 index.
\end{enumerate}

Comprehensive back‑testing demonstrates that incorporating quantum coherence yields higher Sharpe ratios and lower turnover than classical optimization without sacrificing diversification.  Moreover, a large‑scale grid search across 625 parameter combinations and 50 randomly selected sub‑universes establishes the robustness of these gains.


This paper builds understanding systematically from classical foundations to quantum innovations. Section~\ref{sec:classical} identifies the five fundamental limitations of mean-variance optimization that motivate our quantum approach, providing the conceptual anchor readers need to appreciate why quantum methods offer genuine advantages. Section~\ref{sec:theory} bridges classical and quantum domains by showing how financial networks naturally support QSW dynamics. Section~\ref{sec:methods} translates theory into practice through GPU-accelerated algorithms and comprehensive experimental design. Section~\ref{sec:results} demonstrates that quantum-enhanced portfolios consistently outperform classical alternatives across multiple metrics and market conditions. Section~\ref{sec:discussion} synthesizes the findings and explores practical implementation pathways for quantum portfolio management.

%======================================================
% Classical Portfolio Optimization: Foundations and Limitations
%======================================================


\section{Classical Portfolio Optimization: Foundations and Limitations}\label{sec:classical}

The classical approach to portfolio optimization has served as the bedrock of quantitative finance for over seven decades. In this section, we review the fundamental principles underlying traditional portfolio theory and critically examine its limitations in addressing the complexities of modern financial markets. This analysis provides the necessary context for understanding how quantum methods can transcend these classical boundaries.

\subsection{Mean-Variance Framework}\label{sec2.1}

The seminal work of Harry Markowitz \cite{markowitz1952portfolio} established the mean-variance optimization framework, which remains the cornerstone of modern portfolio theory. This framework elegantly balances the trade-off between expected returns and risk, measured as variance of returns. The theoretical foundations were further developed by Tobin \cite{tobin1958liquidity}, who introduced the separation theorem, Sharpe \cite{sharpe1964capital}, who developed the Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM), and Merton \cite{merton1972analytic}, who provided analytical solutions for dynamic portfolio problems.

Consider a universe of $n$ risky assets with random returns $\mathbf{r} = (r_1, r_2, \ldots, r_n)^T$. The expected return vector is denoted as $\boldsymbol{\mu} = \mathbb{E}[\mathbf{r}] = (\mu_1, \mu_2, \ldots, \mu_n)^T$, and the covariance matrix is $\boldsymbol{\Sigma} = \text{Cov}(\mathbf{r}) \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}$, where element $\Sigma_{ij} = \text{Cov}(r_i, r_j)$.

For a portfolio with weight vector $\mathbf{w} = (w_1, w_2, \ldots, w_n)^T$, where $w_i$ represents the fraction of wealth invested in asset $i$, the portfolio return is:
\begin{equation}
r_p = \mathbf{w}^T \mathbf{r} = \sum_{i=1}^n w_i r_i
\end{equation}

The expected portfolio return and variance are given by:
\begin{align}
\mu_p &= \mathbb{E}[r_p] = \mathbf{w}^T \boldsymbol{\mu} \\
\sigma_p^2 &= \text{Var}(r_p) = \mathbf{w}^T \boldsymbol{\Sigma} \mathbf{w}
\end{align}

The classical mean-variance optimization problem can be formulated in multiple equivalent forms:

\textbf{Minimum Variance Problem:}
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\min_{\mathbf{w}} \quad & \frac{1}{2}\mathbf{w}^T \boldsymbol{\Sigma} \mathbf{w} \\
\text{subject to} \quad & \mathbf{w}^T \boldsymbol{\mu} \geq \mu_{\text{target}} \\
& \mathbf{w}^T \mathbf{1} = 1 \\
& \mathbf{w} \geq \mathbf{0}
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:minvar}
\end{equation}

where $\mu_{\text{target}}$ is the desired minimum expected return, $\mathbf{1}$ is a vector of ones, and the inequality $\mathbf{w} \geq \mathbf{0}$ enforces no short-selling constraints.

\textbf{Maximum Sharpe Ratio Problem:}
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\max_{\mathbf{w}} \quad & \frac{\mathbf{w}^T \boldsymbol{\mu} - r_f}{\sqrt{\mathbf{w}^T \boldsymbol{\Sigma} \mathbf{w}}} \\
\text{subject to} \quad & \mathbf{w}^T \mathbf{1} = 1 \\
& \mathbf{w} \geq \mathbf{0}
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:sharpe}
\end{equation}

where $r_f$ denotes the risk-free rate.

The solution to the unconstrained problem yields the efficient frontier, characterized by:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{w}^* = \frac{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1}(\boldsymbol{\mu} - \lambda \mathbf{1})}{\mathbf{1}^T \boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1}(\boldsymbol{\mu} - \lambda \mathbf{1})}
\label{eq:optimal_weights}
\end{equation}

where $\lambda$ is a Lagrange multiplier that parameterizes different points along the efficient frontier.

\subsection{Extensions and Refinements}\label{sec2.2}

Several extensions to the basic mean-variance framework have been proposed to address specific practical considerations:

\textbf{Black-Litterman Model:} Black and Litterman \cite{black1992global} developed a Bayesian approach that combines market equilibrium with investor views:
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\text{BL}} = [(\tau \boldsymbol{\Sigma})^{-1} + \mathbf{P}^T \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1} \mathbf{P}]^{-1}[(\tau \boldsymbol{\Sigma})^{-1} \boldsymbol{\Pi} + \mathbf{P}^T \boldsymbol{\Omega}^{-1} \mathbf{q}]
\end{equation}

where $\boldsymbol{\Pi}$ represents equilibrium returns, $\mathbf{P}$ encodes views, $\mathbf{q}$ represents view magnitudes, $\boldsymbol{\Omega}$ is the uncertainty in views, and $\tau$ is a scaling parameter.

\textbf{Robust Optimization:} To address parameter uncertainty, robust formulations have been developed \cite{fabozzi2007robust,goldfarb2003robust,garlappi2007portfolio}:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\min_{\mathbf{w}} \max_{\boldsymbol{\mu} \in \mathcal{U}_{\mu}, \boldsymbol{\Sigma} \in \mathcal{U}_{\Sigma}} \quad & \mathbf{w}^T \boldsymbol{\Sigma} \mathbf{w} \\
\text{subject to} \quad & \mathbf{w}^T \boldsymbol{\mu} \geq \mu_{\text{target}} \\
& \mathbf{w}^T \mathbf{1} = 1
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

where $\mathcal{U}_{\mu}$ and $\mathcal{U}_{\Sigma}$ represent uncertainty sets for parameters.

\textbf{Resampled Efficiency:} Michaud \cite{michaud1989markowitz} introduced resampling techniques to address estimation error, while coherent risk measures \cite{artzner1999coherent} and conditional value-at-risk (CVaR) optimization \cite{rockafellar2000optimization} provide alternatives to variance as a risk metric.

\subsection{Limitations}\label{sec2.3}

Despite its theoretical elegance and widespread adoption, the classical mean-variance framework faces numerous limitations when applied to real-world investment problems:

\paragraph{Curse of Dimensionality and Estimation Error}

The covariance matrix $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}$ contains $\frac{n(n+1)}{2}$ unique parameters that must be estimated from historical data. For a modest universe of $n = 500$ assets, this requires estimating 125,250 parameters. Given finite sample sizes, estimation errors compound dramatically, leading to unstable and suboptimal portfolios \cite{mantegna1999introduction}. Indeed, DeMiguel et al. \cite{demiguel2009optimal} demonstrated that a naive $1/n$ equal-weighted portfolio often outperforms sophisticated optimization techniques out-of-sample, highlighting the severity of estimation error.

The condition number of the covariance matrix, defined as:
\begin{equation}
\kappa(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}) = \frac{\lambda_{\max}(\boldsymbol{\Sigma})}{\lambda_{\min}(\boldsymbol{\Sigma})}
\end{equation}

often exceeds $10^6$ for large asset universes, indicating severe ill-conditioning that amplifies small estimation errors into large portfolio weight distortions.

\paragraph{Non-Stationarity and Regime Changes}

Financial markets exhibit time-varying statistical properties, violating the stationarity assumption underlying classical optimization. The dynamic nature of correlations can be characterized by:
\begin{equation}
\rho_{ij,t} = \frac{\text{Cov}_t(r_i, r_j)}{\sigma_{i,t} \sigma_{j,t}}
\end{equation}

where subscript $t$ denotes time-dependence. During market crises, correlations often converge toward unity, precisely when diversification is most needed \cite{mantegna1999hierarchical}. Longin and Solnik \cite{longin2001extreme} documented that correlations increase significantly during market downturns, while Ang and Chen \cite{ang2002asymmetric} revealed asymmetric correlations between upside and downside movements.

\paragraph{Non-Gaussian Return Distributions}

Empirical asset returns exhibit significant departures from normality, including:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Heavy tails:} Excess kurtosis $\kappa = \mathbb{E}[(r - \mu)^4]/\sigma^4 - 3 > 0$
\item \textbf{Asymmetry:} Non-zero skewness $\gamma = \mathbb{E}[(r - \mu)^3]/\sigma^3$
\item \textbf{Volatility clustering:} Time-varying conditional variance
\end{itemize}

Cont \cite{cont2001empirical} provides a comprehensive review of these stylized facts, showing that return distributions exhibit power-law tails with tail indices typically between 2 and 5. These features render variance an inadequate risk measure, as it fails to capture tail risks and higher-order moments that significantly impact portfolio performance.

\paragraph{Computational Complexity with Realistic Constraints}

Practical portfolio optimization often involves constraints beyond the simple linear constraints in Equation \eqref{eq:minvar}:

\textbf{Cardinality Constraints:}
\begin{equation}
\sum_{i=1}^n \mathbb{I}(w_i > 0) \leq K
\end{equation}

where $\mathbb{I}(\cdot)$ is the indicator function and $K$ limits the number of holdings. Chang et al. \cite{chang2000heuristics} and Bienstock \cite{bienstock1996computational} demonstrated that portfolio optimization with cardinality constraints is NP-hard.

\textbf{Transaction Costs:}
\begin{equation}
TC = \sum_{i=1}^n c_i |w_i - w_{i,0}|
\end{equation}

where $c_i$ represents the transaction cost rate and $w_{i,0}$ is the initial weight.

\textbf{Market Impact:}
\begin{equation}
\text{Impact}_i = \alpha_i \cdot \text{sgn}(w_i - w_{i,0}) \cdot |w_i - w_{i,0}|^{\beta_i}
\end{equation}

Almgren and Chriss \cite{almgren2001optimal} developed optimal execution strategies that account for both permanent and temporary market impact, while Grinold and Kahn \cite{grinold1999active} integrated transaction costs into the portfolio construction process.

The inclusion of such constraints transforms the problem from a convex quadratic program to a mixed-integer nonlinear program (MINLP), which is NP-hard \cite{fletcher2013practical}.

\paragraph{Sensitivity to Input Parameters}

Small perturbations in expected returns can lead to dramatic changes in optimal portfolios. Best and Grauer \cite{best1991sensitivity} showed that mean-variance portfolios are extremely sensitive to estimates of expected returns, while Chopra and Ziemba \cite{chopra1993effect} quantified that errors in means are roughly eleven times as important as errors in variances, and errors in variances are twice as important as errors in covariances. The sensitivity can be quantified by:
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial \mathbf{w}^*}{\partial \boldsymbol{\mu}} = \frac{1}{\mathbf{1}^T \boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1} \mathbf{1}} \left[ \boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1} - \frac{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1} \mathbf{1} \mathbf{1}^T \boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1}}{\mathbf{1}^T \boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1} \mathbf{1}} \right]
\end{equation}

This extreme sensitivity often results in concentrated, unintuitive portfolios that perform poorly out of sample.

\paragraph{Failure to Capture Complex Dependencies}

Classical correlation measures only capture linear dependencies:
\begin{equation}
\rho_{ij} = \frac{\text{Cov}(r_i, r_j)}{\sigma_i \sigma_j}
\end{equation}

However, financial markets exhibit complex nonlinear dependencies, tail dependencies, and higher-order interactions that are invisible to linear correlation analysis. Copula-based measures reveal that:
\begin{equation}
\tau_{\text{tail}} = \lim_{u \to 1} P(F_j(r_j) > u | F_i(r_i) > u)
\end{equation}

often differs significantly from what linear correlation would predict.
\subsection{Practical Limitations of Classical Optimization}\label{sec:classical:limits}

Despite seven decades of refinement, mean-variance optimization faces fundamental challenges when applied to real-world portfolio construction:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,label=(\alph*)]
\item \textbf{Estimation error and dimensionality.}  
      The covariance matrix $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}$ contains $\tfrac{1}{2}n(n+1)$ parameters; with $n=500$ assets this exceeds $1.25 \times 10^{5}$ parameters. Finite samples routinely yield condition numbers $\kappa(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}) > 10^{6}$, causing extreme sensitivity to estimation errors and unstable portfolio weights~\cite{demiguel2009optimal}.

\item \textbf{Market non-stationarity.}  
      Correlations spike toward unity during market crises~\cite{longin2001extreme}, precisely when diversification is most crucial. This violates the stationarity assumption underlying Equation~\eqref{eq:minvar} and renders historical covariance estimates unreliable for forward-looking optimization.

\item \textbf{Non-Gaussian return distributions.}  
      Empirical returns exhibit heavy tails, asymmetric skewness, and volatility clustering~\cite{cont2001empirical}. Variance captures only second-moment risk, ignoring tail dependencies and higher-order interactions that dominate crisis periods.

\item \textbf{Real-world implementation constraints.}  
      Practical considerations—cardinality limits, transaction costs, market impact, and liquidity constraints—transform the convex quadratic program into an NP-hard mixed-integer nonlinear program~\cite{chang2000heuristics,fletcher2013practical}, often requiring heuristic approximations.

\item \textbf{Extreme parameter sensitivity.}  
      Small perturbations in expected returns $\boldsymbol{\mu}$ cause dramatic portfolio rebalancing. Estimation errors in means dominate those in covariances by roughly an order of magnitude~\cite{best1991sensitivity,chopra1993effect}, leading to unstable and counterintuitive allocations.
\end{enumerate}

