\subsection{Why a Quantum--Graph Perspective?}
\label{sec:classical:motivation}
%------------------------------------------------------

The classical mean--variance framework treats portfolio choice as a \emph{static} quadratic programme, yet the risk term $\mathbf{w}^{\top}\!\boldsymbol{\Sigma}\,\mathbf{w}$ can be viewed more intuitively as a \textbf{diffusion of capital} on a weighted network: assets form the vertices, covariances set the edge weights, and self--loops reward each asset’s own risk--adjusted return.  
Network studies based on minimum–spanning trees (MST) and planar maximally filtered graphs (PMFG) first revealed that diversification and contagion propagate along these edges \cite{mantegna1999hierarchical,onnela2003dynamic,TUMMINELLO201040}.  
Later work traced systemic‐risk spill-overs across such graphs \cite{pozzi2013spread,kenett2012networks}.  
Classical solvers glimpse this structure only indirectly through dense linear algebra, which neither adapts on-line to time-varying edges nor embeds diversification \emph{in the dynamics} of the flow.

\paragraph{Quantum Stochastic Walks (QSWs) as a dual-channel engine.}
A QSW blends coherent quantum evolution with a stochastic Google-type walk \cite{whitfield2010quantum,attal2012open, Eduardo2012quantum, paparo2013quantum}.  
The coherent channel, driven by a Hamiltonian, explores correlated asset clusters through superposition and interference; the stochastic channel, parameterized by the Google matrix \cite{brin1998anatomy}, guarantees ergodicity and convergence.  
Tuning the quantum--classical mixing parameter $\omega$ turns this into an adaptive mechanism ideally suited to markets that exhibit both structured dependencies and stochastic shocks.

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Correlated clusters explored coherently.}  
      Superposition surveys multiple highly-covariant groups in parallel, uncovering hidden diversification routes.
\item \textbf{Diversification via decoherence.}  
      The stochastic channel damps cyclic probability within tight clusters, nudging capital towards under-represented sectors.
\item \textbf{Non-linear dependencies captured.}  
      Interference patterns encode higher-order effects that linear correlation omits.
\item \textbf{Guaranteed convergence.}  
      For any $\omega>0$, the GKLS generator with Google matrix 
      formulation is relaxing; the walk converges exponentially 
      fast to a unique stationary state \cite{spohn1977algebraic}.
\item \textbf{Scalable like PageRank.}  
      Sparse matrix--vector kernels identical to web ranking allow GPU or distributed implementations on universes of $10^{3}$--$10^{4}$ assets.
\end{itemize}

Thus the quantum--graph view transforms portfolio optimization from a brittle quadratic fit into a living, ergodic process that balances exploration and exploitation on the financial network itself.  
Sections~\ref{sec:theory}--\ref{sec:methods} formalize this construction and show how it subsumes Eqs.~\eqref{eq:minvar}--\eqref{eq:sharpe}, addressing the five classical limitations identified in Section~\ref{sec:classical:limits} while delivering empirically superior risk-adjusted returns.





