\subsection{Dual-Channel Quantum Encoding}\label{sec3.3}

Our framework encodes financial information through two complementary channels that capture different aspects of market dynamics.
\paragraph{Coherent Channel: Hamiltonian Construction}
\textbf{Coherent channel} explores highly correlated clusters via quantum tunneling and interference.
The Hamiltonian governs the quantum coherent evolution and encodes both individual asset quality and correlation structure:
\begin{equation}
H_{ij} = \begin{cases}
-\gamma_1 \cdot SR_i & \text{if } i = j \\
\gamma_2 \cdot \hat{\Sigma}_{ij} & \text{if } i \neq j
\end{cases}
\label{eq:hamiltonian_impl}
\end{equation}

where $\gamma_1 = \gamma_2 = 100$ are scaling factors and $\hat{\Sigma}_{ij}$ is the normalized covariance:
\begin{equation}
\hat{\Sigma}_{ij} = \frac{\Sigma_{ij} - \Sigma_{\min}}{\Sigma_{\max} - \Sigma_{\min}}
\label{eq:normalized_cov}
\end{equation}

This normalization ensures $\hat{\Sigma}_{ij} \in [0,1]$ and provides numerical stability. The scaling factor of 100 ensures the Hamiltonian elements have appropriate magnitude for numerical evolution.

The Hamiltonian design achieves several objectives:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Energy landscape}: Diagonal terms create an energy gradient favoring high-Sharpe assets (lower energy)
\item \textbf{Quantum tunneling}: Off-diagonal terms enable coherent transitions based on the covariance structure
\item \textbf{Hermiticity}: The construction ensures $H_{ij} = H_{ji}$, preserving unitarity
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Stochastic Channel: Google Matrix Rates}
\textbf{Stochastic channel} enforces diversification through teleportation and correlation-weighted penalties.
The weight matrix is row-normalized to create a stochastic transition matrix:
\begin{equation}
P_{ij} = \frac{W_{ij}}{\sum_k W_{ik}}
\label{eq:transition_matrix}
\end{equation}
Then implemented in the Google matrix\eqref{eq:google_impl}

The stochastic dynamics are implemented through transition rates in Eq.~\eqref{eq:qsw_master_final} derived from the Google matrix:
\begin{equation}
c_{ij} = \mathcal{G}_{ij}
\label{eq:google_rates}
\end{equation}



When set proportional to Google matrix elements, the rates $c_{ij}$ intuitively represent:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Capital flow propensity}: Risk- and return-weighted tendency to reallocate from asset $j$ to asset $i$
\item \textbf{Liquidity constraints}: Small $c_{ij}$ for illiquid or high-friction transitions
\item \textbf{Market microstructure}: Can be calibrated to empirical trade flow intensities
\end{itemize}


The QSW framework provides several quantum advantages over classical approaches:

\paragraph{Simultaneous Exploration of Portfolio Space}

The quantum superposition enables exploration of multiple portfolio configurations simultaneously. Starting from the maximally mixed state:
\begin{equation}
\rho_0 = \frac{I}{n}
\label{eq:initial_state}
\end{equation}

the system evolves through a superposition of paths weighted by both:
\begin{itemize}
\item Quantum amplitudes (from Hamiltonian evolution)
\item Classical probabilities (from Google matrix transitions)
\end{itemize}

This dual exploration mechanism can discover portfolio allocations that satisfy multiple criteria simultaneously.

\paragraph{Correlation-Aware Portfolio Construction}

The interplay between the two channels creates sophisticated portfolio dynamics:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Hamiltonian channel}: The normalized covariance in off-diagonal terms enables quantum tunneling between correlated assets, allowing the system to explore correlation clusters coherently

\item \textbf{Google channel}: The exponential penalty $\exp(-\beta \Sigma_{ij})$ in transition rates suppresses classical jumps between correlated assets, encouraging diversification
\end{enumerate}

This dual treatment of correlations—quantum tunneling within clusters but classical suppression between them—naturally identifies diversified portfolios.

\paragraph{Adaptive Risk-Return Balance}

The QSW framework provides control over optimization behavior through multiple parameters:

\paragraph{Mixing Parameter $\omega$}
Controls the quantum-classical balance:
\begin{itemize}
\item Low $\omega$ (e.g., 0.2): Quantum effects dominate, enabling broad exploration
\item High $\omega$ (e.g., 0.8-1.0): Classical dynamics dominate, ensuring convergence
\item Medium $\omega$ (e.g., 0.4-0.6): Balanced regime for most market conditions
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Return Preference $\alpha$}
Controls the strength of preference for high-Sharpe assets in the weight matrix:
\begin{itemize}
\item Low $\alpha$ (e.g., 0.1): Weak preference, allowing broader asset selection
\item High $\alpha$ (e.g., 100-500): Strong preference for high-Sharpe assets
\item Affects the exponential weight $W_{ij} \propto \exp(\alpha SR_j)$
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Diversification Strength $\beta$}
Controls the penalty for correlated assets:
\begin{itemize}
\item Low $\beta$ (e.g., 0.1): Weak correlation penalty, allowing concentrated portfolios
\item High $\beta$ (e.g., 100-500): Strong penalty, enforcing diversification
\item Affects the exponential weight $W_{ij} \propto \exp(-\beta \Sigma_{ij})$
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Holding Coefficient $\lambda$}
Controls the incentive to maintain positions:
\begin{itemize}
\item Low $\lambda$ (e.g., 0.1): Weak holding incentive, allowing frequent rebalancing
\item High $\lambda$ (e.g., 100-500): Strong incentive to maintain current positions
\item Affects diagonal weights $W_{ii} = \exp(\lambda SR_i)$
\end{itemize}

These parameters offer flexibility in tailoring the optimization to different investment objectives and market conditions. The optimal parameter values depend on the specific application and can be determined through a systematic grid search or optimization.


