        \item \textit{HHI:} \(\rho_{1\mathrm{Y}}\!=\!+0.36,\;
              \rho_{2\mathrm{Y}}\!=\!+0.40\).  
              The same shift re-concentrates the book.
      \end{itemize}
      The negative trade-off confirms the earlier scenario study:
      staying in the \(\omega\!=\!0.2{-}0.4\) band is essential for both
      alpha and diversification.

\end{enumerate}

Consequently, $\omega$ is the first-order lever (quality and breadth), $\lambda$ the transaction cost lever (lower values reduce turnover), $\alpha$ an opportunistic lever that should be eased back when a more extended history is available, and $\omega$ a second-order "risk-polisher". The hierarchy is identical for 1- and 2-year look-backs—only the strength of the coefficients changes, providing a robust roadmap for parameter setting in live deployments.



