\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
%======================================================

This study set out to test whether a \emph{QSW} engine can produce practically relevant gains once realistic
trading frictions and diversification constraints are taken into account.  Three progressively broader experiments were run on
\textbf{large-cap U.S.\ equities}—first on the \textit{Top 100} names by
market-capitalization (Sec.~\ref{sec5.2}–\ref{sec5.3}), then on
\textit{fifty} randomly redrawn 100-stock universes
(Sec.~\ref{sec5.4}).  We distil the results into four take-aways,
followed by limitations and avenues for further work.

%------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{What did we learn?}
%------------------------------------------------------

\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*),leftmargin=*]

\item \textbf{Quantum coherence is the dominant lever.}  
      In every experiment the mixing parameter
      $\omega$ explains the largest share of variance in Sharpe
      (Pearson $\rho=-0.26$ to $-0.58$), turnover
      ($\rho=+0.40$) and concentration ($\rho=+0.36/+0.40$).
      A \emph{low} classical weight
      ($\omega\simeq0.2$–$0.4$) emerges as a near-universal
      sweet-spot.\medskip

\item \textbf{Cost efficiency—not headline Sharpe—is where QSW shines.}  
      With \textbf{one year} of training data QSW beats
      a maximum-Sharpe MPT optimizer by $+24$–$27\%$
      \emph{and} cuts trading by 90–99\,\%, lifting
      Sharpe/turnover ratios by up to $312\times$.
      With \textbf{two years} of history, the classical portfolio
      regains the raw-Sharpe crown, yet QSW still
      trades one to two orders of magnitude less,
      so its \emph{net-of-cost} alpha remains comparable.\medskip

\item \textbf{Broad, automatic diversification is “free’’ in QSW.}  
      HHI sits in $[0.01,0.016]$ for \emph{all} parameter sets,
      two orders of magnitude below the UCITS 5/10/40 ceiling
      (\raisebox{.5pt}{\texttildelow}0.25).\medskip

\item \textbf{Results generalize across random universes.}  
      In 50 independent 100-stock draws, the best QSW
      configuration (re-optimized each time) beats the
      MPT reference on Sharpe in 54\,\% of samples and
      on \emph{trading efficiency} in 100\,\%.
      QSW turnover averages 48\%, with 90 \% of cases below 92 \%, vs. 493 \% average for MPT.
\end{enumerate}

%------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Practical recipe}
%------------------------------------------------------



\begin{center}
\fbox{\parbox{0.96\linewidth}{%

\textbf{Step 1}: Choose $\lambda$ to set turnover band and $\alpha$ provides fine-tuning:\\
$\lambda=0.1$ (low, $\sim10\%$), $\lambda=100$ (medium, $\sim45\%$), $\lambda=500$ (high, $\sim70\%$)

\textbf{Step 2}: Use $\omega\!\in[0.2,0.6]$ to trade performance vs.\ efficiency.\\
\textbf{Supporting}: $\alpha\!\ge50$, $\beta\!\ge500$.\\[2pt]
\emph{Outcome}: Sharpe $0.74{-}0.85$,
HHI $\approx0.010$,
efficiency $5{-}40\times$ MPT.}}
\end{center}

The rule is robust to look-back length and to the precise stock sample.  Implementation costs of $\sim$20 bp per
100\,\% turnover—typical for large caps—therefore reduce QSW’s “paper’’ alpha by just 1–8 bp, versus 65–100 bp for MPT.

