Now considering a sample $\doe = \{X_1, \dots, X_n \}$ drawn from the uniform distribution on $[0, 1]$, we can propose two estimators of $\HoneNorm{f}^2$, obtained by replacing $x_{(i)}$ by $X_{(i)}$ either directly in \eqref{eq:SqNormGram} or in its explicit expression \eqref{eq:SqNormGramExplicit}. Notice that the estimator \eqref{eq:SqNormMCEwithDer} may appear as a limit case of \eqref{eq:SqNormGramExplicit}: 
if $x_{(i)} \approx \frac{i-1/2}{n}$ then, using that $\th(x) \underset{x \to 0}{\sim} x$ and $\sh(x) \underset{x \to 0}{\sim} x$, we obtain that 
\begin{equation*}     \Vert \Pi_X(f) \Vert_{\cH} ^2 
\approx \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} 
f(x_i)^2 
+ n \sum_{i=2}^{n} 
\left[f(x_{(i)}) - f(x_{(i-1)}) \right]^2. 
\end{equation*}

