 \begin{abstract}
 In this paper we provide an explicit tableaux realization for all simple subquotients of a relation Gelfand-Tsetlin $\mathfrak{gl}(n)$-module.



 \end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
 In the context of the theory of representations of the Lie algebra $\mathfrak{gl}(n)$ of all $n\times n$ matrices over $\mathbb{C}$, a remarkable construction, due to I. Gelfand and M. Tsetlin \cite{GT50} provides a presentation for every simple finite-dimensional module in terms of certain combinatorial objects called Gelfand-Tsetlin tableaux, and explicit action of the generators of the algebra -- the Gelfand-Tsetlin formulas. In \cite{Zhe73} it is shown that Gelfand-Tsetlin basis are eigenbasis for the action of a certain maximal commutative subalgebra $\Gamma$ of $U(\mathfrak{gl}(n))$ -- the Gelfand-Tsetlin subalgebra. 
 \noindent
 In \cite{DFO94} the authors introduced and studied modules that can be decomposed into a direct sum of generalized eigenspaces with respect of the action of $\Gamma$-- the Gelfand-Tsetlin modules. Moreover, the class of generic Gelfand-Tsetlin modules is constructed. This infinite--dimensional  modules have a  tableaux basis as the simple finite-dimensional modules, and the action of the algebra is also given by the Gelfand-Tsetlin formulas.\\
\noindent
Relation modules (see \cite{FRZ19}) depend of a directed graph and a  Gelfand-Tsetlin tableau related with the graph, and were constructed with the aim of unify several known constructions of Gelfand-Tsetlin modules (see \cite{GT50}, \cite{LP79}, \cite{DFO94}, \cite{Maz98}, \cite{Maz03} among others). 
 In this paper we provide the explicit basis for any simple subquotient of a relation module, generalizing the results obtained in \cite{FGR15} for generic modules.

  This paper is divided as follows: in Section~\ref{sec:GTmod} we define the Gelfand-Tsetlin modules, and recall the construction of all simple finite--dimensional $\mathfrak{gl}(n)$-modules. In Section \ref{sec:RelGTmodules} we consider relation modules and recall their main properties. In Section~\ref{sec:simplesubquotients}, we establish the main results of the paper. Finally, Section~\ref{sec:examples} is devoted to present examples illustrating the main results discussed in Section~\ref{sec:simplesubquotients}.




