In~\cite{Gaifullin19}, Gaifullin proved that certain trinomial hypersurfaces are flexible; he call them hypersurfaces of types $H_1$ -- $H_5$. In fact, our trinomial varieties of types $V_1$, $V_2$ and $V_3$ generalize hypersurfaces of types $H_1$, $H_3$ and $H_4$. On the other hand, the cases of hypersurfaces of types $H_2$ and $H_5$ can not be generalized in such a way. Namely, by definition, a hypersurface of type $H_2$ is given by the equation of the form 
$$T_0^2+T_1^2+T_2^{l_2}=0,$$
where $T_i^2=T_{i1}^2T_{i2}^2\ldots T_{in_i}^2$
while a hypersurface of type $H_5$ is defined as follows:
