\section{Introduction}
The concept of action---or of external operation---is a pervasive algebraic notion, as it enables algebraic structures to interact beyond their intrinsic environment of definition. 

In categorical terms, such actions can be defined as functors. 
For instance, an action of a group $G$ on a set $X$ can be described as a functor
\[
\alpha\colon G(*) \rightarrow \Set,
\]
where $G(*)$ is the group $G$ viewed as a one-object groupoid, and the unique object~$*$ is sent to $X$ by $\alpha$.

However, it is sometimes necessary to consider \emph{internal actions},  where the acting and the acted objects belong to the same category. These kinds of actions are useful tools for  studying the algebraic structures themselves, as it happens systematically in cohomological algebra. In the case of groups, a functor $\alpha\colon G(*) \rightarrow \Gp$ corresponds to a group homomorphism $G \rightarrow \Aut(X)$, where $X=\alpha(*)$ and $\Aut(X)$ denotes the group of automorphisms of $X$.  

On the one hand, this approach offers certain advantages, as it enables the expression of the notion of action within the internal language of the category of groups. On the other hand, it also presents several drawbacks. Firstly, many algebraic categories, otherwise well-behaved, do not admit an internal construction that plays the role of $\Aut(-)$. Secondly, even in cases where such a construction exists, it is typically not functorial.

The right take on this subject was developed by D.~Bourn and G.~Janelidze in~\cite{Bourn-Janelidze:Semidirect}, and later systematized by F.~Borceux, G.~Janelidze and G.~M.~Kelly in~\cite{IntAct}. In these two papers, the authors make a strong point on what an internal action should be from a \emph{category-theoretic perspective}. The first paper centers on the algebraic (monadic) description of split epimorphisms, i.e., of the category of \emph{points} (see \Cref{sec_prel}). In a Barr-exact pointed protomodular category $\bC$ with pushouts of split monomorphisms, the split epimorphisms with a fixed codomain $B$ are given by algebras on the kernel of the epimorphism, for a specified monad $B\flat(-) \colon \bC \rightarrow \bC$. When the category is not pointed, the general theory introduced in~\cite{Bourn-Janelidze:Semidirect} still applies, albeit with a caveat: it only allows us to express split epimorphisms over $B$ in terms of split epimorphisms over another object $E$, whenever there is a morphism $E \rightarrow B$.

However, the ``working mathematician'' knows that fair notions of (external) actions have been defined in many non-pointed algebraic contexts, as for example for unital algebras over a field $\bF$ or for unital rings~\cite{maclane_homology}, and they are often obtained by imposing additional axioms to a corresponding notion of action in a pointed context (see, for instance,~\cite{Orzech}, where external actions are described in the context of groups with operations). 

The present paper aims to partially bridge this gap, by proposing the new notions of \emph{coherent action} and of \emph{ideal action}. From an algebraic perspective, the former generalizes unital actions of rings and algebras, where a multiplicative unit is required to \emph{act} like a unit, while the latter stems from the classical situation where a unitary algebra acts on an ideal.

\smallskip
The manuscript is structured as follows. After this introduction, Section 2 presents some necessary background. {\em Ideally exact categories} are recalled in Section 3, while in Section 4 we propose the notions of coherent and ideal actions and  morphisms. In \Cref{thmunital},  we show that all ideal actions are coherent; the problem of whether the converse of the theorem holds in general remains open. We call {\em BAT} the ideally exact contexts with a \emph{good theory of actions}, i.e., where the converse of \Cref{thmunital} holds for objects and morphisms. A connection with semidirect products in the ideally exact context is analyzed in Section 5, and equivalent conditions for an ideally exact context to be BAT are given (\Cref{cor:main}). 
The final section presents case studies of BAT contexts, namely: unitary non-associative $\bF$-algebras or rings, MV-algebras, product algebras, and $\Set^{\op}$, the dual of the category of sets.

\medskip
\textbf{Notation.} We often identify algebraic varieties with their corresponding categories; therefore, we shall adopt the same notation for both.


\section{Preliminaries}\label{sec_prel}
Let us briefly recall that, for an object $B$ of a category $\bC$, one can define the slice category $(\bC\downarrow B)$, having for objects the arrows with codomain $B$, and for morphisms  the obvious commutative triangles. 
Let $\bC$ be a category with pullbacks, with initial object $0$ and terminal object $1$, and let $f \colon E \rightarrow B$ be a morphism in $\bC$. We recall that the \textit{adjunction associated} with $f$ is
\[
\begin{tikzcd}
{(\bC\downarrow B)} & {(\bC \downarrow E),}
\arrow[""{name=0, anchor=center, inner sep=0}, "{f^{*}}"', from=1-1, to=1-2]
\arrow[""{name=1, anchor=center, inner sep=0}, "{f \circ -}"', curve={height=12pt}, from=1-2, to=1-1]
\arrow["\dashv"{anchor=center, rotate=-89}, draw=none, from=1, to=0]
\end{tikzcd}
\]
where $f^{*}$ is the functor defined by pulling back along $f$ the object $p\colon A\rightarrow B$ of $(\bC\downarrow B)$, as it is shown in the following diagram:
\[
\begin{tikzcd}
{E\times_{B} A} & A \\
E & B.
\arrow["{p_2}", from=1-1, to=1-2]
\arrow["{f^*(p)=p_1}"', from=1-1, to=2-1]
\arrow["p", from=1-2, to=2-2]
\arrow["f"', from=2-1, to=2-2]
\end{tikzcd}
\]
Notice that the unit and the counit of the adjunction $(f \circ -)\dashv f^{*}$ are \emph{cartesian}.
Denote by $\iota$ the initial maps and with $\tau$ the terminal ones, and consider the adjunction associated with the unique morphism $\iota=\tau\colon 0\rightarrow 1$:
\[
\begin{tikzcd}
{\bC \cong (\bC \downarrow 1)} & {(\bC\downarrow 0).}
\arrow[""{name=0, anchor=center, inner sep=0}, "{R}"', from=1-1, to=1-2]
\arrow[""{name=1, anchor=center, inner sep=0}, "{L}"', curve={height=15pt}, from=1-2, to=1-1]
\arrow["\dashv"{anchor=center, rotate=-89}, draw=none, from=1, to=0]
\end{tikzcd}
\]
Since the unit $\eta$ of the adjunction is cartesian,  for any object $C$ in $\bU$, the map $\eta_{C}\colon C \rightarrow RL(C)$ defines a kernel of $RL(\iota)$, i.e., the following diagram is a pullback:
\[\begin{tikzcd}
C & {RL(C)} \\
0 & {RL(0).}
\arrow["{\eta_{C}}", from=1-1, to=1-2]
\arrow["\tau"', from=1-1, to=2-1]
\arrow["{RL(\tau)}", from=1-2, to=2-2]
\arrow["{\iota=\eta_{0}}"', from=2-1, to=2-2]
\end{tikzcd}\]

Another relevant construction on $\bC$ is the category $\Pt_{\bC}(B)$ of \emph{points over $B$}, i.e., the category of \emph{pointed objects} of $(\bC\downarrow B)$. Explicitly, objects are split epimorphisms over $B$ with a chosen splitting, and morphisms are arrows between the domains of such split epimorphisms, commuting with both the retraction and the section.

Let $f\colon E\to B$ be a morphism of $\bC$. If $\bC$ admits pullbacks along $f$, we can define the functor
\[
f^*\colon \Pt_{\bC}(B) \to \Pt_{\bC}(E)
\]
(notice that we are adopting the same notation here, as for slice categories, since the construction is essentially the same). Furthermore, if $\bC$ admits pushouts along~$f$, the functor $f^*$ has a right adjoint 
\[ f_!\colon \Pt_{\bC}(E) \to \Pt_{\bC}(B),\]
defined by
\[
\begin{aligned}
\xymatrix{
D\ar@<+.5ex>[d]^{d}
\\
E\ar@<+.5ex>[u]^{e}
}    
\end{aligned}
\quad\mapsto\quad
\begin{aligned}
\xymatrix{
D\ar@<+.5ex>@{.>}[d]^{d}\ar@{.>}[r]
&D+_EB\ar@<+.5ex>[d]^{[f\circ d, id_B]}
\\
E\ar@<+.5ex>@{.>}[u]^{e}\ar@{.>}[r]_-{f}
&B\ar@<+.5ex>[u]^{f_!(e)}
}    
\end{aligned}
\]

From now on, let us tacitly suppose that $\bC$ has pullbacks and pushouts, so that~$f^*$ and $f_!$ are defined for all morphisms $f \colon E \to B$ of $\bC$. Let us fix some standard terminology. 

The category $\bC$ is \emph{Bourn protomodular}~\cite{protomodular} when, for every morphism $f \colon B \to E$ of it, the functor $f^*$ on points reflects isomorphisms. Notice that if $\bC$ is \emph{pointed}, i.e., when the unique arrow $0 \to 1$ is an isomorphism, protomodularity is equivalent to the \emph{split short five-lemma}~\cite{reg_prot}. $\bC$ is \emph{regular}~\cite{Barr} if it is a finitely complete category and all effective equivalence relations have pullback stable coequalizers. A regular category $\bC$ is \emph{Barr exact}~\cite{Barr} if all equivalence relations are effective, i.e., kernel pairs. Finally, a category $\bC$ is \emph{semi-abelian}~\cite{Semi-Ab} when it is pointed, with finite coproducts, Bourn protomodular and Barr exact. 

Let $\bC$ be a protomodular category. If the functors $f^*$ are not only conservative, but monadic, $\bC$ is said to be a category with \emph{semidirect products}~\cite{Bourn-Janelidze:Semidirect}. Indeed, if we denote by $T^f$ the monad determined by $f^*$, the $T^f$-algebras are called \emph{internal actions}. One defines the semidirect product $(X,\xi) \rtimes (B,f)$ of $(B,f)$ with a $T^f$-algebra $(X,\xi)$ as a pre-image of $(X,\xi)$ along the comparison equivalence $K$:
\[
\xymatrix@!C=14ex{
&(X,\xi)\ \ \in \ar@{|-->}[dl]
&\Pt_{\bC}(E)^{T^f}\ar[d]^{\text{forgetful}}
\\(X,\xi)\rtimes(B,f)\in 
&\ar@<+1ex>[ur]^K\Pt_{\bC}(B)\ar[r]_-{f^*}&\Pt_{\bC}(E). \ar@/_2.5ex/[l]^{\bot}_(.35){f_!}}
\]
When $\bC$ is pointed, one may take $E=0$, so that $\Pt_{\bC}(E)\cong \bC$. In this case, $f^*=\iota_B^*$ is nothing but the kernel functor, so that we reproduce a more familiar notion of semidirect product. Notice that in this case we slightly modify our notation: $B\flat \coloneqq T^{\iota_B}$, where $B\flat X=\ker ([1,0]\colon B+X\to B)$. Hence, internal $B$-actions are morphisms of the form
\[
\xi \colon B \flat X \to X
\]
and monadicity establishes the equivalence of categories between internal $B$-actions and points over $B$.

\section{Ideally exact categories}\label{ideallyexactcat}
Ideally exact categories have been introduced by G.~Janelidze in~\cite{IdeallyExact} as a non-pointed counterpart of semi-abelian categories. In fact, ideally exact categories are closely related to a categorical setting introduced by S.~Lapenta, L.~Spada and the second named author in~\cite{rel} in order to define a \emph{categorical notion of ideal} relative to a given adjunction $U\vdash F$ (a \emph{basic setting for relative $U$-ideal}, see~\cite[Definition~3.3]{IdeallyExact}). In the present article, we stick to G.~Janelidze's definition, since, among many of its features, it clarifies how the notion of relative $U$-ideal can be defined intrinsically.

\begin{definition}\cite[Definition 3.2 and Theorem 3.1]{IdeallyExact}
A category $\bU$ is \textit{ideally exact} if it is Barr exact, Bourn protomodular, has finite coproducts, and the unique morphism $\iota \colon 0\rightarrow 1$ in $\bU$ is a regular epimorphism.
\end{definition}

In addition to all semi-abelian categories, examples of ideally exact categories include the categories $\Ring$ and $\CRing$ of  unital and commutative unital rings respectively, every category of unital algebras over a field $\bF$ (see \Cref{Var}), the categories of \emph{MV-algebras}, \emph{product algebras} (see~\cite{rel} and \Cref{MV}), and any cotopos (see \Cref{sec_set}).

Since the pullback functor $\bU \rightarrow  (\bU \downarrow 0)$ along the regular epimorphism $0 \rightarrow 1$ is monadic, ideal exactness can be related to adjoint pairs.

\begin{theorem}\cite[Theorem 3.1]{IdeallyExact}\label{carid}
Let $\bU$ be a category with pullbacks. The following conditions are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\bU$ is ideally exact;
\item $\bU$ is Barr-exact, has finite coproducts and there exists a monadic functor $\bU\rightarrow \bV$, where $\bV$ is a semi-abelian category;
\item There exists a monadic functor $\bU\rightarrow \bV$, where $\bV$ is a semi-abelian category, such that the underlying functor of the corresponding monad preserves regular epimorphisms and kernel pairs. \noproof
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}\cite[Theorem 3.3]{IdeallyExact}\label{cartesian}
Let $\bU$ be an ideally exact category. It follows from \Cref{carid} that there exists a monadic adjunction 
\begin{equation}\label{monadic}
\begin{tikzcd}
{\bU} & {\bV}
\arrow[""{name=0, anchor=center, inner sep=0}, "U"', from=1-1, to=1-2]
\arrow[""{name=1, anchor=center, inner sep=0}, "F"', curve={height=12pt}, from=1-2, to=1-1]
\arrow["\dashv"{anchor=center, rotate=-90}, draw=none, from=1, to=0]
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
with $\bV$ semi-abelian. This adjunction is associated with the unique morphism $0 \rightarrow 1$ (up to an equivalence) if and only if the unit of the adjunction is cartesian. One may always choose $\bV=(\bU \downarrow 0)$ with $U$ and $F$ defined in the obvious way, but this may not be the most convenient choice. For instance, if $\bU$ is already semi-abelian, it might be most convenient to take $\bV=\bU$.
\end{remark}

\begin{example}\label{monadic_ring}
Let $\bU=\Ring$. Then a monadic adjunction as in \Cref{cartesian} is given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:situation_rng}
\begin{tikzcd}
{\Ring} & {\Rng,}
\arrow[""{name=0, anchor=center, inner sep=0}, "U"', from=1-1, to=1-2]
\arrow[""{name=1, anchor=center, inner sep=0}, "F"', curve={height=12pt}, from=1-2, to=1-1]
\arrow["\dashv"{anchor=center, rotate=-90}, draw=none, from=1, to=0]
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
where $\Rng$ is the semi-abelian category of non-necessarily unital rings, $U$ is the forgetful functor and $F$ maps every ring $X$ to the semidirect product $\bZ \ltimes X$ with multiplication
\[
(\alpha,x)\cdot(\alpha',x')=(\alpha \alpha', xx' + \alpha x' + \alpha' x)
\] 
and unit $(1,0)$. The unit $\eta \colon 1_{\Rng} \Rightarrow UF$ of the adjunction is cartesian since the map $\eta_X \colon X \rightarrow U(\bF \ltimes X) \colon x \mapsto (0,x)$ is the kernel of
\[
U(\pi_1) \colon U(\bF \ltimes X) \rightarrow U(\bF) \colon (\alpha,x) \mapsto \alpha.
\]
Thus, $F \dashv U$ is, up to an equivalence, the adjunction associated with the unique morphism $\bZ \rightarrow \lbrace 0 \rbrace$ in $\Ring$.
\end{example}

\section{Coherent and ideal actions}\label{unitalactions}
Let $\bU$ be ideally exact, $\bV$ semi-abelian and let 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:situation}
\begin{tikzcd}
{\bU} & {\bV}
\arrow[""{name=0, anchor=center, inner sep=0}, "U"', from=1-1, to=1-2]
\arrow[""{name=1, anchor=center, inner sep=0}, "F"', curve={height=12pt}, from=1-2, to=1-1]
\arrow["\dashv"{anchor=center, rotate=-90}, draw=none, from=1, to=0]
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
be a monadic adjunction with cartesian unit; we shall refer to such a situation as an \emph{ideally exact context}. 
Since $\bV$ is semi-abelian, internal actions can clearly be defined for objects of $\bV$.  In this section we extend this possibility to objects of $\bU$ acting via the functor $U$.

\begin{definition}
Let $B$ be an object of $\bU$ and let $X$ be an object of $\bV$. A \emph{$B$-action}, or a \emph{relative $U$-action}, is an internal action $\xi \colon U(B)\flat X\rightarrow X$ in $\bV$.
%An internal action $\xi \colon U(B)\flat X\rightarrow X$ be an internal action in $\bV$. Such actions are called $B$-actions in $\bV$, or relative  $U$-actions, when $B$ is not specified.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
Let $\xi \colon U(B)\flat X\rightarrow X$ be a relative $\bU$-action. We say that $\xi$ is a \emph{coherent action} if $\xi \circ (U(\iota )\flat 1_{X})=\xi_{0}$, where $\xi_{0}\colon UF(0)\flat X \rightarrow X$ is the relative $U$-action associated with the canonical split epimorphism
\[\begin{tikzcd}
{UF(X)} & {UF(0).}
\arrow["{UF(\tau)}", shift left=2, from=1-1, to=1-2]
\arrow["{UF(\iota)}", shift left, from=1-2, to=1-1]
\end{tikzcd}\]
In other words, the following diagram in $\bV$
\[\begin{tikzcd}
{U(B)\flat X} & X \\
{UF(0)\flat X}
\arrow["\xi", from=1-1, to=1-2]
\arrow["{U(\iota)\flat \id_{X}}", from=2-1, to=1-1]
\arrow["{\xi_{0}}"', from=2-1, to=1-2]
\end{tikzcd}\]
is commutative.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}
The action $\xi_0$ is coherent since $\xi_0=\xi_0 \circ (U(\id_{F(0)})\flat \id_{X})$.
\end{example}

\begin{remark}
Let us motivate our terminology choices.
In the case of \Cref{monadic_ring}, the action of a unitary ring $B$ is coherent on a non-unitary ring X, when the multiplicative unit acts \emph{coherently} as in $F(X)=\mathbb{Z} \ltimes X$ (see \Cref{BAT_Rng}).
%the free unitary ring over $X$.
More generally, in the varietal case, when $U$ is  a forgetful functor into a semi-abelian variety that forgets all the constants but one, coherent actions are those for which the constants behave coherently as in $F(X)$.
\end{remark}

Next lemma, whose proof is immediate,  allows us to relate coherent actions in~$\bV$ with split epimorphisms in $\bU$.

\begin{lemma}\label{unit}
Let $\xi\colon U(B)\flat X \rightarrow X$ be a relative $U$-action, and let
\[\begin{tikzcd}
{A} & {U(B)}
\arrow["{p}", shift left, from=1-1, to=1-2]
\arrow["{s}", shift left, from=1-2, to=1-1]
\end{tikzcd}\] be the split epimorphism associated with $\xi$. Then, $\xi$ is coherent if and only if there exists a morphism $f\colon UF(X)\rightarrow A$ in $\bV$ such that the following diagram  
\[\begin{tikzcd}
X & {UF(X)} & {UF(0)} \\
X & A & {U(B).}
\arrow["{\eta_{X}}", from=1-1, to=1-2]
\arrow[equal, from=1-1, to=2-1]
\arrow["{UF(\tau)}", shift left, from=1-2, to=1-3]
\arrow["f"', dashed, from=1-2, to=2-2]
\arrow["{UF(\iota)}", shift left, from=1-3, to=1-2]
\arrow["{U(\iota)}", from=1-3, to=2-3]
\arrow["k"', from=2-1, to=2-2]
\arrow["p", shift left, from=2-2, to=2-3]
\arrow["s", shift left, from=2-3, to=2-2]
\end{tikzcd}\]
is a morphism of split extensions in $\bV$ (see~\cite[Lemma 2.3]{cross}). Notice that, in this case, the square on the right is a split pullback. \noproof
\end{lemma}

Observe that, since the category $\bV$ is protomodular, then by~\cite[Lemma 3.1.22]{malcev} the pair $(\eta_{X},UF(\iota))$ is jointly strongly epimorphic. Thus, if it exists, $f$ is uniquely determined by $\id_X$ and $U(\iota)$.

%In other words, the action $\xi\colon U(B)\flat X \rightarrow X$ is coherent if and only if the following diagram is a split pullback diagram
%\[\begin{tikzcd}
%	{UF(X)} & {UF(0)} \\
%	A & {U(B).}
%	\arrow["{UF(\tau)}", shift left, from=1-1, to=1-2]
%	\arrow["f"', from=1-1, to=2-1]
%	\arrow["{UF(\iota)}", shift left, from=1-2, to=1-1]
%	\arrow["{U(\iota)}", from=1-2, to=2-2]
%	\arrow["p", shift left, from=2-1, to=2-2]
%	\arrow["s", shift left, from=2-2, to=2-1]
%\end{tikzcd}\]

Recall from~\cite{rel} that a \emph{relative $U$-ideal} is a morphism $k\colon X\rightarrow U(A)$ in $\bV$ such that there exists a morphism $p'\colon A'\rightarrow B$ in $\bU$ with $k=\ker(U(p'))$. 

\begin{definition}\label{def_ideal}
Given an ideally exact context (\ref{eq:situation}), we say that a split epimorphism
\begin{equation}\label{ideal_split}
\begin{tikzcd}
{A} & {U(B)}
\arrow["{p}", shift left, from=1-1, to=1-2]
\arrow["{s}", shift left, from=1-2, to=1-1]
\end{tikzcd}    
\end{equation}
in $\bV$ is \emph{ideal} (relatively to $U$), if there exists a split epimorphism
\begin{equation}\label{ideal_split_prime}\begin{tikzcd}
{A'} & {B}
\arrow["{p'}", shift left, from=1-1, to=1-2]
\arrow["{s'}", shift left, from=1-2, to=1-1]
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
in $\bU$ and an isomorphism $\sigma\colon U(A')\rightarrow A$ such that the following diagram in $\bV$ 
\[\begin{tikzcd}
{U(A')} & {} & A \\
& {U(B)}
\arrow["\sigma", from=1-1, to=1-3]
\arrow["{U(p')}"', shift right, from=1-1, to=2-2]
\arrow["p"', from=1-3, to=2-2]
\arrow["{U(s')}"'{pos=0.3}, shift right, from=2-2, to=1-1]
\arrow["s"', shift right=2, from=2-2, to=1-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
is commutative.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
A motivation for our choices of terminology: the relative $B$-actions just described are called \emph{ideal} because the object $B$ acts on the relative $U$-ideal $X$. 
\end{remark}

Recall that a functor $U\colon \bU\to \bV$ is 
\emph{full on isomorphisms} if, given an isomorphism $\alpha\colon U(A)\to U(B)$ in $\bV$, there exists in  $\bU$ an isomorphism $\beta\colon A\to B$  such that $U(\beta)=\alpha$. Typically, the forgetful functors between algebraic varieties are full on isomorphisms. An example of a non-full on isomorphisms functor is the forgetful functor $U\colon \mathbf{Top} \to \Set$, where $\mathbf{Top}$ is the category of topological spaces.

\begin{lemma}\label{lm:unique_xi}
Given an ideally exact context (\ref{eq:situation}), suppose that the functor $U$ is faithful and full on isomorphism. If the split epimorphism (\ref{ideal_split}) of \Cref{def_ideal} is ideal, then the split epimorphism (\ref{ideal_split_prime}) is essentially unique.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Suppose there exist two split epimorphisms
\[\begin{tikzcd}
{A'} & {B}
\arrow["{p'}", shift left, from=1-1, to=1-2]
\arrow["{s'}", shift left, from=1-2, to=1-1]
\end{tikzcd}\quad \text{ and } \quad
\begin{tikzcd}
{A''} & {B}
\arrow["{p''}", shift left, from=1-1, to=1-2]
\arrow["{s''}", shift left, from=1-2, to=1-1]
\end{tikzcd}\]
in $\bU$, and two isomorphisms $\sigma_1 \colon U(A') \rightarrow A$, $\sigma_2 \colon U(A'') \rightarrow A$ such that the following diagrams in $\bV$
\[\begin{tikzcd}
{U(A')} & {} & A \\
& {U(B)}
\arrow["\sigma_1", from=1-1, to=1-3]
\arrow["{U(p')}"', shift right, from=1-1, to=2-2]
\arrow["p"', from=1-3, to=2-2]
\arrow["{U(s')}"'{pos=0.3}, shift right, from=2-2, to=1-1]
\arrow["s"', shift right=2, from=2-2, to=1-3]
\end{tikzcd}
\qquad
\begin{tikzcd}
{U(A'')} & {} & A \\
& {U(B)}
\arrow["\sigma_2", from=1-1, to=1-3]
\arrow["{U(p'')}"', shift right, from=1-1, to=2-2]
\arrow["p"', from=1-3, to=2-2]
\arrow["{U(s'')}"'{pos=0.3}, shift right, from=2-2, to=1-1]
\arrow["s"', shift right=2, from=2-2, to=1-3]
\end{tikzcd}
\]
commute. Thus, if $\sigma=\sigma_2^{-1} \circ \sigma_1$, the diagram in $\bV$
\[
\begin{tikzcd}
{U(A')} & {} &  {U(A'')}\\
& {U(B)}
\arrow["\sigma", from=1-1, to=1-3]
\arrow["{U(p')}"', shift right, from=1-1, to=2-2]
\arrow["U(p'')"', from=1-3, to=2-2]
\arrow["{U(s')}"'{pos=0.3}, shift right, from=2-2, to=1-1]
\arrow["U(s'')"', shift right=2, from=2-2, to=1-3]
\end{tikzcd}
\]
is commutative. Since $U$ is faithful and full on isomorphisms, there exists a morphism $\sigma \colon A' \rightarrow A''$ in $\bU$, such that $U(\sigma')=\sigma$. By faithfulness of the functor $U$, $\sigma$ is a morphism in the category $\Pt_\bU(B)$, i.e., the split epimorphisms
\[\begin{tikzcd}
{A'} & {B}
\arrow["{p'}", shift left, from=1-1, to=1-2]
\arrow["{s'}", shift left, from=1-2, to=1-1]
\end{tikzcd}\quad \text{ and } \quad
\begin{tikzcd}
{A''} & {B}
\arrow["{p''}", shift left, from=1-1, to=1-2]
\arrow["{s''}", shift left, from=1-2, to=1-1]
\end{tikzcd}\]
are isomorphic.
\end{proof}
\begin{definition}
A morphism 
%$h\colon (A_1,p_1,s_1)\to (A_2,p_2,s_2)$ 
\[
\begin{tikzcd}
{A_1} & {} & {A_2} \\
& {U(B)}
\arrow["h", from=1-1, to=1-3]
\arrow["{p_1}"', shift right, from=1-1, to=2-2]
\arrow["p_2"', from=1-3, to=2-2]
\arrow["{s_1}"'{pos=0.3}, shift right, from=2-2, to=1-1]
\arrow["s_2"', shift right=2, from=2-2, to=1-3]
\end{tikzcd}
\]
between ideal split epimorphisms over $U(B)$ is an \emph{ideal morphism} if there exists a morphism
%\[h'\colon (A'_1,p'_1,s'_1)\to (A'_2,p'_2,s'_2)\] 
\[
\begin{tikzcd}
{A_1'} & {} & {A_2'} \\
& {B}
\arrow["h'", from=1-1, to=1-3]
\arrow["{p_1'}"', shift right, from=1-1, to=2-2]
\arrow["p_2'"', from=1-3, to=2-2]
\arrow["{s_1'}"'{pos=0.3}, shift right, from=2-2, to=1-1]
\arrow["s_2'"', shift right=2, from=2-2, to=1-3]
\end{tikzcd}
\]
between split epimorphisms over $B$ such that $U(h')=h$.
\end{definition}
In the language of actions, we call \emph{ideal} the relative $U$-action $\xi \colon U(B) \flat X \rightarrow X$ corresponding to the ideal split epimorphism (\ref{ideal_split}). Similarly, an ideal morphism of such actions comes from an ideal morphism of the corresponding split epimorphisms.

\smallskip
The following theorem shows a connection between coherent actions and ideal actions in any ideally exact context.
%states that if a split epimorphism in $\bV$ is ideal, then the corresponding internal action is coherent.

\begin{theorem}\label{thmunital} 
Consider an ideally exact context (\ref{eq:situation}) and let $\xi\colon U(B)\flat X\rightarrow X$ be a relative $U$-action. If $\xi$ is ideal, then $\xi$ is coherent.
%and let 
%\begin{equation}\label{ideal_split}
%\begin{tikzcd}
%{A} & {U(B)}
%\arrow["{p}", shift left, from=1-1, to=1-2]
%\arrow["{s}", shift left, from=1-2, %to=1-1]
%\end{tikzcd}
%\end{equation}
%be the split epimorphism associated with $\xi$. If \eqref{ideal_split} is ideal, then $\xi$ is coherent.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let (\ref{ideal_split}) be the split epimorphism associated with $\xi$. Since $\xi$ is ideal, then there exists in $\bU$ a split epimorphism
\[\begin{tikzcd}
{A'} & {B}
\arrow["{p'}", shift left, from=1-1, to=1-2]
\arrow["{s'}", shift left, from=1-2, to=1-1]
\end{tikzcd}\] 
and an isomorphism $\sigma \colon U(A') \rightarrow A$ such that $U(p')=p\circ \sigma $ and $\sigma \circ U(s')=s$.

Let $k'=\sigma\circ k$, so that $(X,k')$ is a kernel of $U(p')$. Since the unit $\eta$ of the adjunction is cartesian, then $\eta_{X}\colon X \rightarrow UF(X)$ is a kernel of $UF(\tau)\colon UF(X)\rightarrow UF(0)$. Moreover, if $f\colon F(X) \rightarrow A'$ is the morphism in $\bU$ given by the universal property of the unit $\eta$ with respect to the kernel $k'\colon X \rightarrow U(A')$ of $U(p')$, that is
\[\begin{tikzcd}
X & {UF(X)} & {F(X)} \\
{U(A')} && {A'}
\arrow["{\eta_{X}}", from=1-1, to=1-2]
\arrow["k'"', from=1-1, to=2-1]
\arrow["U(f)", dashed, from=1-2, to=2-1]
\arrow["f", dashed, from=1-3, to=2-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
then the diagram
\[\begin{tikzcd}
X & {UF(X)} & {UF(0)} \\
X & {U(A')} & {U(B)}
\arrow["{\eta_{X}}", from=1-1, to=1-2]
\arrow[equal, from=1-1, to=2-1]
\arrow["{UF(\tau)}", shift left, from=1-2, to=1-3]
\arrow["U(f)"', dashed, from=1-2, to=2-2]
\arrow["{UF(\iota)}", shift left, from=1-3, to=1-2]
\arrow["{U(\iota)}", from=1-3, to=2-3]
\arrow["k'"', from=2-1, to=2-2]
\arrow["{U(p')}", shift left, from=2-2, to=2-3]
\arrow["{U(s')}", shift left, from=2-3, to=2-2]
\end{tikzcd}\]
is commutative, since $U(f)\circ \eta_{X}=k'$. 

By protomodularity, the pair $(\eta_{X}, UF(\iota))$ is jointly strongly epimorphic. Thus, we may prove that the morphisms $U(\iota)\circ UF(\tau)$ and $U(p')\circ U(f)$ are equal by composing them with the pair $(\eta_{X}, UF(\iota))$. Indeed, one has
\[U(\iota)\circ UF(\tau)\circ \eta_{X}=0=U(p')\circ k'=	U(p')\circ k'=U(p')\circ U(f)\circ \eta_{X}.\]
Moreover, since $F(0)$ is initial in $\bU$, one has
\[
s' \circ \iota=f \circ F(\iota),
\]
and therefore
\[U(\iota)\circ UF(\tau)\circ UF(\iota)=U(\iota)=U(p')\circ U(s')\circ U(\iota)= U(p')\circ U(f)\circ UF(\iota).
\]
Hence $U(\iota)\circ UF(\tau)=U(p')\circ U(f)$. As a consequence, by composing the diagrams below
\[\begin{tikzcd}
X & {UF(X)} & {UF(0)} \\
X & {U(A')} & {U(B)} \\
X & A & {U(B)}
\arrow["{\eta_{X}}", from=1-1, to=1-2]
\arrow[equal, from=1-1, to=2-1]
\arrow["{UF(\tau)}", shift left, from=1-2, to=1-3]
\arrow["{U(f)}"', from=1-2, to=2-2]
\arrow["{UF(\iota)}", shift left, from=1-3, to=1-2]
\arrow["{U(\iota)}", from=1-3, to=2-3]
\arrow["{k'}"', from=2-1, to=2-2]
\arrow[equal, from=2-1, to=3-1]
\arrow["{U(p')}", shift left, from=2-2, to=2-3]
\arrow["\sigma"', from=2-2, to=3-2]
\arrow["{U(s')}", shift left, from=2-3, to=2-2]
\arrow[equal, from=2-3, to=3-3]
\arrow["k"', from=3-1, to=3-2]
\arrow["p", shift left, from=3-2, to=3-3]
\arrow["s", shift left, from=3-3, to=3-2]
\end{tikzcd}\]
we have that the action $\xi$ is coherent.
\end{proof}

As of now, the authors do not know whether the converse of \Cref{thmunital} holds in any ideally exact context. This is currently under investigation. However, when it holds,  it establishes a convenient setting to study a well-behaved notion of action. This motivates the following definition.
\begin{definition}
An ideally exact context (\ref{eq:situation}) admits a \emph{good theory of actions} (or, it is BAT\footnote{The acronym BAT is inspired by the notion of BIT-variety, where BIT stands for {\bf B}uona (good, in Italian) {\bf I}deal {\bf T}heory, introduced by A.~Ursini in \cite{BIT}. Analogously, BAT stands for {\bf B}uona {\bf A}ction {\bf T}heory.}, for short) if all coherent actions are ideal, and all morphisms of such actions are ideal.
\end{definition}

In \Cref{Var} we present some case studies which have a good theory of actions.

