Then, by \Cref{unit} there exists a map $f \colon (A,*_{A}) \rightarrow (1+X, 1)$ in $\Set_{*}$ such that the following diagram
\[
\begin{tikzcd}
{(1+{*}, 1)} & {(1+X, 1)} & {(X,*_{X})} \\
{(1+B,1)} & {(A,*_{A})} & {(X,*_{X})}
\arrow["{1+*_{X}}", shift left, from=1-1, to=1-2]
\arrow["{1+!_{X}}", shift left, from=1-2, to=1-1]
\arrow["{[*_{X}, \id_{X}]}", from=1-2, to=1-3]
\arrow["{1+!_{B}}", from=2-1, to=1-1]
\arrow["p", shift left, from=2-1, to=2-2]
\arrow["f"', from=2-2, to=1-2]
\arrow["s", shift left, from=2-2, to=2-1]
\arrow["k", from=2-2, to=2-3]
\arrow["{\operatorname{id}_{(X,*_{X})}}"', from=2-3, to=1-3]
\end{tikzcd}
\]
is commutative. We observe that for any $a \in A$, $s(a)=1$ if and only if $a=p(1)=*_A$. Indeed, if $a=p(1)$, then $s(a)=s(p(1))=1$. Conversely, if $s(a)=1$, it follows from 
\[1=((1+!_{B})\circ s)(a)=((1+!_{X})\circ f)(a)\]
that $f(a)=1$. Hence
\[
([*_{X}, \id_{X}]\circ f)(a)=k(a)=*_{X}.
\]
As a consequence $a \in \ker(k)$, i.e., there exists $x\in 1+B$ such that $p(x)=a$. If $x \in B$, then 
\[
f(a)=f(p(x))= ((1+*_{X})\circ (1+!_{B}))(x)=*_{X} \in X,
\]
which is a contradiction since $f(a)=1 \notin X$. Thus, $a=p(1)$.

To conclude the proof, we take $A'=A \setminus \lbrace *_A \rbrace$, $p'=p_{\downharpoonright_{B}}$ and $s'= s_{\downharpoonright_{A'}}$. One may easily check that $U(p')=p$, $U(s')=s$ and the map
