\section{Theoretical Background}

\label{sec:ap2}

\subsection{GD Flow Derivation}
\label{sec:ap21}

\paragraph{Supervised Learning Context}

%Fundamentally, all gradient-based optimization methods (GD) applied to a task consisting of many sub-tasks attempt to train a parameterized model to minimize a loss function over an ensemble of trials by evolving the model parameters down a consensus loss gradient. 
We let $s$ denote the Gradient Descent (GD) iteration. In the simplest form of GD, the parameters $\theta(s)$ evolve as $s$ progresses according to 
\begin{align}
    \theta_{s+1} = -\eta \nabla_{\theta(s)} L(\theta(s)).
\end{align}
Once the trial input $x(t) \sim X$ is chosen, there is assumed to be a corresponding target for this particular trial input, $y^*(t|x)$. Then, the loss (which implicitly depends on $\theta$ through $z$) is assumed to have the form
\begin{align}
    L(\theta) &:= \E_{x \sim X} [\int_0^{t_{end}} \ell(z(t | x,\theta), y^*(t|x)) \d t],
\end{align}
where the term $\ell$ defines the instantaneous ``running loss'' on each individual timestep. Throughout our work, $\ell$ is an MSE loss with linear readout with dynamically trained $W_{out}, b_{out}$ and normalization constant $k$ defined by the batch size, timesteps and number of hidden units,
\begin{align}
    \ell(z(t|x), y^*(t)) = \frac{1}{k} \| W_{out} z(t|x) + b_{out} - y^*(t)\|. 
\end{align}

\paragraph{Adjoint Method Preliminaries}

Fundamentally, GD necessitates the ability to compute efficient and accurate per-trial loss gradients, $\nabla_\theta  L(\theta |d)$. The most common means of computing such gradients in deep-learning contexts is backwards-mode automatic differentiation (also known as backpropagation through time, BPTT), which is provably identical to the \textit{adjoint method} in both continuous and discrete cases \citep{lecun1988theoretical,pontryagin1962mathematical}. The latter is distinguished as a more general approach to solving ODE- and PDE-constrained optimization problems primarily in the context of optimal control. We describe the general detials of this in the main text, but here we go into more depth.
%In this broader context, richer theoretical tools and analyses are often applied that are not as well explored in the specific case mentioned here \tdc \textbf{todo clarify}.  \\

% The adjoint method provides a formalism for understanding backpropagation on sequential parameterized models in both continuous and discrete cases \textbf{cite}.  \Ecomment{Let's make sure our cites include opt control literature ...} We consider a supervised learning context in which inputs, $x(t)$, are drawn from some distribution $X$. Corresponding to each input is a target, $y^*(t)$ that is deterministically defined when the input is known. The target indicates what the desired output of the network should be at a particular time, quantified by a loss function below. We ``train'' a parameterized model, as follows. With a particular input, $x(t),$ that drives the dynamics, a continuous model has dynamics 
% \begin{align}
%     \dot z(t | x)= f(z(t |  x) | x, \theta),
% \end{align}
% where $|$ means ``given'' and $\theta$ are the model parameters that are changed through gradient descent. The state exists in an ambient space $\R^n$ and the parameters from some space $\R^m$, typically with $n << m$. 

% The goal is to minimize a loss function on all input trial pairs. The loss typically has the form 
% \begin{align}
%      L &= \underset{(x,y^*)}{\E} \Big[ \int_0^{t_{end}} \ell(z(t | x),  y^*(t)) \d t + \psi(z(t_{end} | x), y^*(t_{end})) \Big],
% \end{align}
% where the $\E_{(x,y^*)}$ averages over all trials. Note $t_{end}$ may also depend on the particular, trial, i.e. $t_{end}(x)$ is a function, to support variable length trials. The first term $\ell$ is a running loss and the second function $\psi$ is a terminal loss. The former loss appears with ``many to many'' or ``one to many'' RNNs and the latter appears in the ``many to one'' and ``one to one'' contexts. 

% Computing the gradient sensitivity $\nabla_\theta L$ is required to perform batch gradient descent on the loss. 

As in Supplementary Figure \ref{fig:adjointschematic}, the adjoint method computes per-trial gradients by solving three initial value problems \citep{chen2018neural}: 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textit{Forward propagation}, where the state $z(t|x)$ is simulated from time $0$ to time $t_{end}$. 
    \item \textit{Adjoint backpropagation}, where an adjoint variable is simulated backwards from time $t_{end}$ to time $0$.
    \item \textit{Gradient backpropagation}, where a running gradient is simulated backwards from time $t_{end}$ to time $0$ effectively ``extrapolating'' the adjoint in state space to parameter space sensitivities. 
\end{enumerate}

Step 1 is as detailed above. Step 2 computes the per-trial adjoint, $a(t | d)$, defined as
\begin{align}
    \label{eqn:adjoint}
    a(t | d) := \nabla_{z(t|d)} L(\theta | d).
\end{align}
Empirically, the adjoint measures how small perturbations tangential to the hidden state trajectory at a an instant in time $t$ (i.e. in the direction of $f$) affect the loss. The adjoint is computed incrementally backwards as below. 

First, we define 
\begin{align}
    \Err(t |d) := \nabla_{z(t|x)} \ell (z(t|x), y^*(t)),
\end{align}
which is an instantaneous sensitivity of the running loss with respect to state. The notation $\Err(t | d)$ is motivated by the special case of a regression task, where 
\begin{align}
    \label{eqn:mse}
    \ell(z(t|x), y^*(t)) = \| W_{out} z(t|x) - y^*(t)\| \,\, \Rightarrow \, \, \Err(t | d) = 2 W_{out}^T (W_{out} z(t|x) - y^*(t)),
\end{align}
i.e. when using a squared-error loss $\Err(t | d)$ is simply an extrapolated residual. 

The dynamics of the adjoint are given by the following initial value problem \citep{chen2018neural}:
\begin{align}
    \frac{\d}{\d t} a(t | d) &= - D_{z(t | x)} f(t|d)^T \cdot a(t | d) - \Err(t | d); \, \,  a(t_{end} | d) = 0. 
\end{align}
The term $D_{z(t|x)} f$ denotes the Jacobian of $f$ at $z(t|x)$. The forcing term,

% Unlike the state, the adjoint dynamics involve the task target, $y^*$, so we use the notation $a(t | y^*)$ to denote it throughout, analogous to the hidden state $z(t | x)$, which is driven by the input $x(t)$. 

Step (3) defines a running gradient sensitivity $g_\theta(t | d)$ in the parameter space, which is typically higher dimensional than the hidden state space. Explicitly, it is given by 
\begin{align}
    \label{eqn:subgrad}
    g_\theta(t | d) &= \nabla_{z(t|d)} \int_t^{t_{end}} \ell(z(t_0|x, \theta), y^*(t_0)) \d t_0. 
\end{align}
The gradient $g_\theta(t |d)$ is given by solving backwards the initial value problem \citep{chen2018neural}:
\begin{align}
    \label{eqn:runninggrad}
    \frac{\d}{\d t} g_\theta(t | d) = - D_\theta f(t|d)^T \cdot a(t|d); \, \, g_\theta(t_{end} | d) = 0.  
\end{align} 
Finally, from Equation \ref{eqn:subgrad}, the per-trial loss gradient is defined by
\begin{align}
    \nabla_\theta  L(\theta |d) = g_\theta(0 | d).
\end{align}
% Thus, the adjoint attempts to push the state $z(t|d)$ tangentially in the negative gradient term at each instant $t$ in time by adjusting the function $f$ defining the dynamics. However, these changes are filtered through the parameters, $\theta$. 
Finally, given all trials, $d$, the proposed parameter update is a mean of these parameter proposals: 
\begin{align}
     \delta  \theta  = -\eta \underset{d \sim D}{\E} [ g_{\theta(s)}(0 | d) ]. 
\end{align}

% 


% To compute this, first an intermediate adjoint $a(t | y^*)$ is computed corresponding to trial $z(t | x)$. This adjoint can be defined by
% \begin{align}
%     a(t | y^*) = \nabla_{z(t | x)} L,
