In this paper we consider the sphere $(S^d, \fa)$ with the antipodal involution $\fa = \tau^{0,d+1}$. Let
\[ \fS^d = C(S^d , \fa) = \{ f \in C(S^d, \C) \mid f(-x) = \overline{f(x)} \} \]
denote the associated real $C \sp *$-algebra. 
We note that $\fS^0 \cong \C$ and that the $K$-theory of $\fS^1$ has been previously studied as
$$\fS^1 \cong \{ f \in C([0,1], \C) \mid f(0) = \overline{f(1)} \} \; $$ 
in \cite{Boer2002}. The groups of $KO_*(\fS^1)$
have period 4 and are shown to be $KO_*(\fS^1)= (\Z, \Z_2, 0, \Z)$  (in degrees $0, 1, 2, 3$) in Corollary 1.6 of \cite{Boer2002}. This algebra $\fS^1$ and its $K$-theory also play a key role in the development of united $K$-theory in \cite{bousfield90} and \cite{Boer2002}. However, in those papers the particular non-trivial $K$-theory classes were not identified explicitly in terms of unitaries. We will do that here in Section~\ref{section-lowdim}.

 We are not aware of any other study of the real $C \sp *$-algebras $\fS^d$ and their $K$-theory, or of $KR(S^d, \fa)$ for $n \geq 2$.
 In this article, we will compute the real $K$-theory of $\fS^d$ for all $d$ (in fact, we will compute the united $K$-theory, which includes the real, complex, and self-conjugate $K$-theory). We find that the critical non-trivial class generating the global part of $K$-theory (the part not associated with constant functions) lies in $\widetilde{KO}_{d+2}(\fS^d)$. We note that since the involution $\fa$ has no fixed points on the sphere, we cannot carry out the earlier analysis for $\tau^{a,b}$ when $a \neq 0$. However, it turns out that the same formula (\ref{KOformula}) still holds for $\fa = \tau^{a,d+1}$ where $a = 0$ as long as $d \geq 2$. This is Theorem~\ref{fS^d}.

Beyond the calculation of the abstract algebraic structure of $KO_i(\fS^d)$, we will identify specific unitary classes representing the non-trivial $K$-theory elements for all $i$ in the low-dimensional cases (for $d \leq 4$); and following that we will describe a recipe for unitary elements which represent the key generator classes of $KO_{d+1}(\fS^d)$ in any dimension $d \geq 3$. To explain what we mean by this, recall that
for a real unital $C \sp *$-algebra $A$, the group $KO_0(A)$ is generated by projection classes $[p]$ where $p \in M_n(A)$. By considering $u = 2p-1$ we can instead formulate $KO_0(A)$ in terms of classes of self-conjugate unitaries $[u]$ where $u \in M_n(A)$.
The group $KO_1(A)$ is generated by unitary classes $[u]$ where $u \in M_n(A)$ (these statement hold in the real case exactly the same as in the complex case).  For $i \neq 0,1$ in the real case, we tend to work with $KO_i(A)$ abstractly either in terms of suspension, writing $KO_i(A) = KO_0(S^d A)$ (such as in Definition~1.4.4 of \cite{schroderbook}); or in terms of graded Clifford algebras, writing $KO_i(A) = KO_0(A \otimes \mathcal{C}_{i, 0})$ (such as in Corollary 2.5.2 of \cite{schroderbook}). However, these abstract approaches, while useful for studying the functorial properties of real $K$-theory and for calculating the algebraic structure of in examples, make it difficult to represent particular elements of $KO_i(A)$ in a concrete and meaningful way. The papers \cite{BL} and \cite{Boer2020} develop a framework for describing elements in all of the groups $KO_i(A)$, as well as $KU_i(A)$, in very concrete terms, using unitaries in $M_n(A \otimes \C)$ that satisfy certain symmetries. The unitary picture of $KO_i(A)$ from \cite{BL} is summarized in Table~\ref{unitaryTable}, which we describe in more detail now.

