 \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1_2 \\ 1_2 & 0 \end{pmatrix} & i = 4 \\ ~ \\
 \begin{pmatrix}  1_2 & 0  \\ 0 & -1_2  \end{pmatrix} & i = 5 \; .
\end{cases}$$
Then we check that $\Up_i^* = \Up_i$ and $\Up_i \Up_j + \Up_j \Up_i = 2 \delta_{i,j}$ for all $i,j$. Therefore, by Proposition~1 of \cite{SBS}, it follows that
$$y_0 = x \Up_1 + y \Up_2 + z \Up_3 + w \Up_4 + v \Up_5$$
represents a generator of $\widetilde{KU}_0(S^4)$. Note that this is a slightly set of Clifford elements $\Up_i \in M_4(\C)$ to that described in \cite{SBS}. The representatives here are chosen to satisfy $\Up_i^{\sharp \otimes {\rm{Tr}}} = \Up_i$ for all $i$. 
 Note that any representation of $M_4(\C)$ is unitarily equivalent to any other, so the resulting class $[y_0]$ will be the same up to sign.
