\begin{abstract}
A p-circuit comprising a network of $N$ p-bits can generate samples from a probability distribution with $2^N$ possibilities just like a network of q-bits, though there are interesting fundamental differences. A p-bit is essentially a binary stochastic neuron used in Boltzmann machines which involve the repeated application of a specific elementary operation, whereby each p-bit looks at the state of 'n' other p-bits, computes the probability for being $0$ or $1$, and chooses its next state accordingly. We argue that the energy cost, $\epsilon(n)$, of this elementary operation is an important metric and we use our ASIC to extract it for our implementation, to serve as model and benchmark for other implementations. We further argue that the same elementary operation can be used in generative AI replacing the standard continuous or multi-bit digital neurons with p-bits. It might appear that this would introduce significant errors, but we show that by averaging a small number (say $1 \ to \ 100$) samples, the image quality can be restored.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
p-bits, p-circuits, probabilistic computing, machine learning, binary stochastic neuron, Markov Chain Monte Carlo, Boltzmann machine, Deep Neural Networks.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
\IEEEPARstart{T}{his} paper is intended as a companion to the introduction to p-bits provided in \cite{li_122_2025}, where a digital ASIC implementing a p-circuit was presented (see Fig.\ref{fig:chip}). We use the word p-bit to contrast it with q-bits that are coherently interconnected to construct q-circuits.
%A p-bit has a probability $p$ of being $0$ and a probability $q$ of being $1$, with $p+q=1$, while a q-bit has complex probability amplitudes $u$ and $v$ for $0$ and $1$ whose squared magnitudes give the corresponding probabilities, with $\vert u \vert^2 + \vert v \vert^2 = 1$.
A q-circuit of $N$ q-bits can be used to generate samples from a probability distribution with $2^N$ possibilities (Fig.\ref{F1}). $2^N$ grows exponentially with $N$ reaching a trillion with just $N=40$ and the power of q-circuits comes from this ability to store such an enormous distribution in a small network. This is not possible with a deterministic circuit, but a classical p-circuit can address at least a subset of the probability distributions accessible to q-circuits \cite{chowdhury_accelerated_2023}.
%where similar benefits in speed and energy over standard implementations can be observed  Here, we focus on exploring that reduction in energy and increase in speed that p-circuits can provide over standard implementations on digital hardware. 



\begin{figure}[!t]
