$\textbf{\textit{How do we build a p-circuit?}}$ The basic building block of a p-circuit consists of a p-bit which generates a random binary variable $b$ whose probability P($b=1$) of being 1 is given by a continuous function W of its binary inputs $\{b_1, b_2, \ \cdot \cdot \ b_n\} $ (Fig.\ref{fig:building_block}). This building block has an intuitive correspondence with Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) algorithms in general, where a system performs a random walk, with the probability of each step governed by its current state. % \cite{ghantasala_efficient_2022, kaiser_benchmarking_2021}.
Boltzmann machines (BM), well-known in machine learning (ML), represent a subset of MCMC and are typically implemented in software on a CPU or a GPU. There are $N$  p-bits, each of which is updated based on the states of the other p-bits using the building block in Fig.\ref{fig:building_block}. This elementary operation is applied to each of $N$ p-bits and the entire cycle is repeated $T$ times resulting in $N \times T$ applications of the same $\textit{elementary operation}$.

$\textbf{\textit{Energy per elementary operation: }}$ We can write the total energy $E$ needed to implement a particular algorithm % as $E=NT \epsilon_p$
%\begin{equation}
%\label{eq:overall_qmc}
%E=NT \epsilon_p
%\end{equation}
in terms of the energy $\epsilon_p$ per probabilistic elementary operation which can be written as (see Fig.\Ref{fig:building_block}b):
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:1}
E=NT \big( \ \underbrace{nb_w \ \epsilon_{\text{wM}} + (n+1) \ \epsilon_{\text{pM}} +  \epsilon_{\text{C}}^p(n,b_a) + \epsilon_{\text{SAMP}}}_{\epsilon_p} \ \big)
\end{equation}
\noindent where $\epsilon_{wM}$ and $\epsilon_{pM}$ represent the energy to access one bit from the weight memory and the p-bit memory respectively, $\epsilon_{C}^p$ is the energy needed to compute the function W over $n$ weights, $b_w$ and $b_a$ are the bits used to represent weights and activations respectively, and $\epsilon_{\text{samp}}$ is the energy needed to generate a binary output based on the output of W. The energy to write the result to p-bit memory is assumed equal to $\epsilon_{pM}$.
