\section{Boltzmann machine: Energy Analysis}
Ref.\cite{li_122_2025} described a 65 nm ASIC which was used to solve a diverse set of problems having very different values of $N$ and $T$, but in each case the energy per elementary operation was about the same: $\epsilon_p \sim 0.5 pJ$. The energy split between the components of Eq.\ref{eq:1} is depicted in Fig.\ref{fig:energy_qmc} for a specific configuration of hyper-parameters pertaining to the QMC problem solved in \cite{li_122_2025}.  

We are focusing this discussion on the energy per elementary operation because we believe it can provide a basis for comparing different hardware implementations. Indeed the inverse of $\epsilon_p$ could be identified as $ELOPS/W$ (elementary operations per second per Watt) comparable to the $FLOPS/W$ used for ordinary computing. For example $\epsilon_p = 0.5 pJ$ corresponds to $2 \times 10^{12} ELOPS/W$. With a power budget of $400 \mu W$, $800M$ operations can be carried out per second. The ASIC in \cite{li_122_2025} operated at this power level to solve a QMC problem with $N=1440$ and $T=2.2 \times 10^4$ taking $NT/800M \sim 40 \ msec$.

