The solution could be reached faster at the same power level if the energy per elementary operation $\epsilon_p$ could be lowered, for example through the use of in-memory computing and/or analog addition followed by a sampler that uses the analog sum to generate a binary output. These are part of the hardware design while the quantities $N$, $n$ and $T$ are set by algorithmic considerations. Let us elaborate a little on the different energy components of the building block: \\
\indent \textbf{\textit{Memory access energy}}, $\epsilon_{WR}, \epsilon_{pR}$: 
In \cite{li_122_2025} both weights and pbit values were stored in local registers, making their read energies identical and relatively low in the tens of fJ range. With large problems it may not be possible to store all weights in registers, requiring SRAM or even DRAM with much larger read energies. Separating weight and p-bit memory is useful in that p-bits may be stored in closer-to-compute memory than weights, which may be orders of magnitude larger overall. In that case, in-memory computing options can be employed to minimize read energies, which can otherwise dominate the other components (Fig.\ref{fig:energy_dnn}). 

\textbf{\textit{Compute energy}}, $\epsilon_{C}(n, b_w, b_a)$: In [1], as with most neural networks, the function W is linear: $w_1b_1 + w_2b_2+ \cdot \cdot + w_nb_n$ for multi-bit $w_i$ quantities. Usually a multiply-accumulate unit (MAC) is used to evaluate W but since ${b_1, \cdot \cdot b_n}$ are binary variables, the function $W$ only requires an accumulate (AC) function that selectively sums a subset of the weights ${w_1, \cdot \cdot w_n}$. For the problems in \cite{li_122_2025} the number $n=6$ is relatively small so that the function W can be read off a look-up table (LUT) with $2^n = 64$ entries. For building blocks where $n$ is too  large for the compute to be implemented with an LUT, $\epsilon_C^p \sim n~\epsilon_{add}$, where $\epsilon_{add}$ is the energy for adding two $b_w$ bit numbers via an adder.  %The energy is halved assuming on average across the system, half the p-bit building block outputs are 1. The remaining -1s result in an additional 2 adds and 1 memory read per building block, which for larger n becomes negligible. As such, it is useful to think of $\epsilon_C$ as having two cases, small $n$ and large $n$. 

\textbf{\textit{Sampling energy}}, $\epsilon_{SAMP}$:
In \cite{li_122_2025} the sampling was done following the function
\begin{equation}
b = sign \big(tanh(W)-rand\{-1,+1\} \big)
\label{EQ2}
\end{equation}
\noindent used for p-bits (or BSN's) where rand\{-1,+1\} is a random number distributed uniformly between $-1$ and $+1$. The sampling energy then has three components: (1) compute $tanh(W)$, (2) generate $rand(-1,+1)$, and (3) compare the two to generate a binary output. Note that unlike the memory read and compute energies, the sampling energy is independent of $n$ and $b_a$ and becomes relatively unimportant for large values of $n$ or $b_a$. 

\textcolor{black}{We include a fourth energy component in Fig.\ref{fig:energy_qmc} as a design-specific catch-all term to include any control logic, I/O, clock distribution, routing, or other overheads. For the ASIC in \cite{li_122_2025}, this design-specific overhead amounted to 25\% of the overall energy consumption, but we have not included this component in Eq.\ref{eq:1}, since it is often not fundamental to the underlying algorithm or architecture.}

\section{p-bits for Feedforward Neural Networks}


    Much work has been reported at the ISSCC on Ising computers which are essentially Boltzmann machines requiring the repeated application of the p-circuit building block we have been discussing (Fig.\Ref{fig:building_block}.a). By contrast, feedforward deep neural networks (DNN), widely used in generative AI, use continuous valued neurons or activations given by $A=f(W)$, $f$ being a non-linear function like $tanh$ or $ReLu$. Instead can we use p-bits with binary activations following Eq.\Ref{EQ2}?

