\textbf{\textit{Results for image generation:}}  \textcolor{black}{As the baseline for our proof-of-concept demonstration, we use a 12-layer Variational Autoencoder DNN using 32-bit weights and (tanh) activations trained to generate celebrity images from a random input (Fig.\Ref{fig:image_gen}a). Replacing the 32-bit activations with pbits we get very noisy images, but averaging over 100 samples creates a hint of recognizable facial images (Fig.\Ref{fig:image_gen}b). Retraining the weights using the actual nonlinear element (Eq.\Ref{EQ2}) instead of the continuous version, the quality of images increases significantly (Fig.\Ref{fig:image_gen}c).}

\textcolor{black}{Replacing the p-bit activations in the final layer with continuous tanh activations marks an improvement, even at 1 sample (Fig.\Ref{fig:image_gen}d, top row). High quality images comparable to the original baseline are obtained (see final row of Fig.\ref{fig:image_gen}d) with \textit{sample-aware} training, which uses the loss value computed from the average of $s$ samples to perform the training by backpropagation \cite{Raiko2015}. Fig.~\ref{fig:fid} compares the image quality for different schemes quantitatively using the Fr\'echet-Inception Distance (FID). A key point to note is that better performance is obtained when the number of samples used in testing matches that used in re-training. Furthermore, with sample-aware retraining, images generated with p-bits can reach very close to 32b FID scores.}

\textit{\textbf{Contrast with stochastic bitstreaming:}} \textcolor{black}{It should be noted that using p-bits in place of continuous neurons as described above is \textit{very} different from the well-known use of stochastic bitstreams to represent continuous signals. With stochastic bitstreams the aim is to generate streams of single bits whose mean value matches the original 32-bit value. Since the mean of a non-linear function is not equal to the function of the mean, sample averaging should be performed individually for \textit{each linear operation at each layer} which gives the top curve in Fig.\Ref{fig:fid} showing an improvement in FID score from $400$ to $170$ with only $s=100$ samples. This may seem surprising since the standard error goes down as $ 1/\sqrt{s}$, and $2^{64}$ samples would be needed to achieve $\textit{32-bit accuracy}$ in the activation, making $100$ samples look like a 'drop in the bucket.'}

\textcolor{black}{However, in problems of this type, our aim is not to recreate the activations $A$ precisely but rather to recreate the loss function $\mathcal{L}$ characterizing the mapping of probability distributions. Consequently we are helped by a small value of the factor $\partial \mathcal{L}/\partial A$. We can get better results by re-training the weights to recover any loss of $\mathcal{L}$ due to the binarization of $A$ as seen from the lower curves in Fig.\Ref{fig:fid}. Unlike stochastic bitstreaming, our approach requires averaging \textit{only at the end layer where $\mathcal{L}$ is computed, and not at intermediate layers.}}
