\textit{\textbf{Energy cost:}} Unlike the BSN function (see Eq.\Ref{EQ2}) the output for a standard DNN elementary operation is deterministic. Its value is continuous and is usually digitized with a bit accuracy $b_a$, which in modern versions is often reduced to $16$ or $8$ rather than the customary $32$ that we show in Fig.\ref{fig:fid}.  The energy of the components of a deterministic, $b_a$ bit building block can be mapped onto Eq.\ref{eq:1} as follows: 
\begin{itemize}
    \item  The compute energy $\epsilon_C$ now includes both a multiply and an add (MAC), rather than just the accumulation (AC), if $b_a>1$. We'll call this $\epsilon_C^d$, as opposed to the probabilistic compute operation $\epsilon_C^p$.
    \item The sampling energy $\epsilon_{\text{samp}}$ becomes a traditional activation energy, $\epsilon_\text{samp} \rightarrow \epsilon_\text{act}$ where $\epsilon_\text{act}$ describes the energy of $b = tanh(W)$.     
    \item If $b_a > 1$, energy pertaining to memory access for the activation values increases. 
\end{itemize}

