\noindent The energy $E_d$ for a deterministic DNN is given by an expression similar to Eq.~\ref{eq:1}:
\begin{equation}
\label{EQ4}
E_d = N \big( \ \underbrace{ nb_w \ \epsilon_{\text{wM}} + (n+1)b_a \ \epsilon_{\text{aM}} +  \epsilon_{\text{C}}^d(n,b_a) + \epsilon_{\text{act}}}_{\epsilon_d} \ \big)
\end{equation}

The energies for deterministic and p-bit based building blocks for DNNs are compared in Fig.\ref{fig:energy_dnn}. The $n$ used for Fig.\ref{fig:energy_dnn} is the average input to each neuron over all layers of the DNN. The largest benefit offered by p-bits is in the simplification of the compute step. But for this to make a difference to the overall energy $\epsilon_p$ per elementary operation it is important to minimize the memory access energy, using alternatives like in-memory computing.  

Assuming the memory access energy can be minimized, p-bits can offer an advantage through the reduced compute energy. Ordinarily the building block multiplies $n$ weights (each $b_w$ bits) with $n$ activations (each $b_a$ bits) while the pbit implementation only sums up a selected subset of $n$ weights (each $b_w$ bits), corresponding to activations with b=1. An additional factor to consider is that unlike a deterministic DNN, a pbit implementation could average over T samples ($T > 1$) to improve image quality. However, multiple samples can be generated without reloading the weights, so that
\begin{equation}
\label{EQ5}
E_p = N \big( nb_w \ \epsilon_{\text{wM}} + T(n+1) \ \epsilon_{\text{pM}} +  T\epsilon_{\text{C}}^p(n) + T \epsilon_{\text{SAMP}} \big)
\end{equation}



