\section{Conclusion}
In summary, Ref.\cite{li_122_2025} described an ASIC implementation of a Boltzmann machine based on repeated applications of the same elementary operation (Fig.~\ref{fig:building_block}) whose energy cost is a key metric for comparing different hardware implementations. With $n=5$ we estimate $\epsilon \sim 0.5 pJ$ corresponding to $1/\epsilon \sim 2 \times 10^{12} ELOPS/W$. This is about six orders of magnitude higher than a straightforward CPU implementation. For large values of $n$, the ELOPS/W will be lower by a factor $\sim 1/n$, but the relative values for different implementations with a given $n$ should be instructive.  The same elementary operation with larger values of $n$ can be used to implement DNN's for generating images whose quality can be improved by averaging $O(10-100)$ samples. The flexibility provided by adjusting the number of samples is a useful feature of the pbit implementation, but whether it will provide an energy advantage over standard implementations will depend on other factors as we have discussed.

%rather than the $O(2^{64})$ that standard error suggests for emulating a 32b deterministic model accurately. The overall energy going from a deterministic DNN to a probabilistic DNN would be reduced so long as the number of samples required is less than the ratio of elementary operation energies. As training strategies reduce the number of required samples, we posit that probabilistic computing approaches for machine learning will become more and more competitive. 

