\section{Conclusion}

% In this work, we developed a distributed data-driven framework for analyzing large-scale GPU performance logs by partitioning SQLite3 tables into smaller shards. These shards are processed concurrently across multiple MPI ranks, significantly reducing per-node memory usage and computational latency. Our approach enables efficient and scalable analysis of massive GPU datasets without memory overflow issues. In the future, we want to investigate more to improve our pipeline by removing the I/O bottlenecks. By analyzing around 93M samples, we find potential memory stall occurring timestamps and further investigation reveals which entities are responsible for the memory spike of that certain period of time. 
We developed a distributed framework that concurrently analyzes GPU performance logs by partitioning SQLite3 tables into shards, reducing memory usage and latency. Analyzing 93M samples, we identified timestamps and entities responsible for memory stalls. Future work will focus on improving scalability by eliminating intermediate I/O bottlenecks.

