\begin{abstract}
    Property elicitation studies which attributes of a probability distribution can be determined by minimising a risk. We investigate a generalisation of property elicitation to imprecise probabilities (IP). This investigation is motivated by multi-distribution learning, which takes the classical machine learning paradigm of minimising a single risk over a (precise) probability and replaces it with $\Gamma$-maximin risk minimization over an IP. We provide necessary conditions for elicitability of a IP-property. Furthermore, we explain what an elicitable IP-property actually elicits through Bayes pairs---the elicited IP-property is the corresponding standard property of the maximum Bayes risk distribution.

    \textbf{Keywords:} Property elicitation; Gamma-maximin; Multi-distribution learning; Distributionally robust optimization; $M$-estimation.
\end{abstract}

\section{Motivation: What can multi-distribution learning \emph{learn}?}

Machine learning in many of its forms is, at its core, minimizing an expected loss (\ie a risk). 
Interestingly, expected loss minimization is also central to another strand of literature: \emph{property elicitation}. 
Starting with the seminal work of \citeauthor{osband1985providing} \cite{osband1985providing}, this field takes a distinct view of loss minimization: It asks two closely related questions. Firstly, for a specific loss function, what property---i.e. function of the data generating distribution---is elicited by this loss---i.e. is given by minimizing the expectation of this loss. Secondly, given a specific property, does there exist a loss function which elicits this property? 

A classical example of an elicitable property is the mean. Minimizing the expected squared error elicits the mean of the data generating distribution. Furthermore, all quantiles of a distribution are elicitable through appropriate parametrizations of the pinball loss. On the other hand, the conditional value at risk (sometimes called expected shortfall) of a distribution is not elicitable \citep{gneiting2011making, ziegel2016coherence}.

To put the questions described above more succinctly: the field of property elictation aims to answer what \emph{is}, and what \emph{can be}, elicited through minimizing an expected loss. Those with a statistical background will immediately see that this is equivalent to the questions of what property of the data generating distribution a given $M$-estimator learns (in the asymptotic regime), and, more broadly, what properties are even possible to learn by $M$-estimation. In recent years, these questions have largely been answered by the field, culminating in the characterization of elicitable properties (see \cite[Theorem 1]{lambert2019elicitation} and \cite{steinwart2014elicitation}).  

However, over the last decades the risk minimization framework has grown into an entire family of related learning paradigms, many of which deviate from the classical setup considered in the property elicitation literature. As motivation for the present paper, we highlight one such paradigm: \emph{multi-distribution learning} (MDL) \citep{haghtalab2022demand}. Instead of minimizing an expected loss, MDL replaces the expectation operator by the supremum over a set of expectations, following the rationale that during training the actual distribution of interest---from which data will be drawn in deployment---is unknown. Rather, good prediction performance should be guaranteed across the class of distributions over which the supremum is taken.
In other words, MDL can be described as expected loss minimization over an imprecise probability (IP) \citep{augustin2014introduction}, rather than over a single (precise) probability. 

A closely related framework is \emph{distributionally robust optimization} (DRO) \citep{rahimianFrameworksResultsDistributionally2022}, which is an IP generalization of classical stochastic programming. It addresses scenarios where the data generating distribution is not known precisely, but rather, may be adversarially chosen from a family of possible distributions---the ``ambiguity set''---which are consistent with the existing knowledge about the data. As such, DRO optimizes the worst-case risk taken over this ambiguity set.

In this work, we address two fundamental questions motivated by the MDL and DRO paradigms:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{What \emph{can} a model learn when trained via MDL?}
    \item \textbf{What \emph{does} a model learn when trained via MDL?}
\end{itemize}
Essentially, we reiterate the central questions of the field of property elicitation---but we are now concerned with loss minimization over IP-expectations, rather than standard expectations. 

Note that we do \emph{not} neglect the conditional relationship between inputs $X$ and outputs $Y$ which is central to machine learning. However, we abstract away this distinction by using an encompassing random variable $Z$ which can be defined as (but is not restricted to) $Z = (X,Y)$. We introduce IP-properties and a definition of elicitability related to the $\Gamma$-minimax decision rule (\cf \citep{troffaes2007decision}). Then, we provide a series of necessary conditions for IP-elicitability. We clarify what an elicitable IP-property actually elicits. We end with a set of open questions such as sufficient conditions for IP-elicitability and IP-identifiability.


\section{Related work}
As noted in the introduction, property elicitation was initiated in \cite{osband1985providing}, although it has its roots in the classical statistical theory of $M$-estimation. More recent work in this area include \cite{lambert2008eliciting} and \cite{steinwart2014elicitation}. Our work extends the concept of property elicitation to imprecise probabilities.

The term `elicitation' often refers to the problem of recovering the true data generating distribution, for example by minimizing the expected value of a proper scoring rule in the context of forecasting \citep{brierVerificationForecastsExpressed1950}. Property elicitation can be viewed as a generalization of this idea, as it considers whether a property of the data generating distribution %---rather than the entire distribution---
can be ascertained. This property can be the distribution itself (this would recover the classical elicitation problem), or only an attribute of the distribution, such as its mean or variance. 

Recently, the study of proper scoring rules for imprecise probabilities has gained new interest \citep{konek2023evaluating, frohlich2024scoring, singh2025truthful, schervish2025elicitation}. Although this paper is not explicitly concerned with proper scoring rules, it is connected to this new work---most closely with \cite{frohlich2024scoring} in particular, from which we borrow some technical machinery. \cite{frohlich2024scoring}, by leaning upon the fundamental link between the maximum entropy distribution and the $\Gamma$-maximin decision rule established in \cite{grunwald2004game}, introduce the concept of an IP scoring rule, which (non-uniquely) elicits an imprecise probability distribution from an IP analogue of the data generating distribution (thereby, fully generalizing proper scoring rules for precise probabilities which elicit precise probability distributions from a precise data generating distribution). \cite{schervish2025elicitation} also generalizes elicitation to IPs, but they do this using (one of three different) IP definitions of admissibility, rather than use IP scoring rules: Given a (precise) strictly proper scoring rule, \cite{schervish2025elicitation} elicits the set of precise forecasts which are $M$-, $E$- or $\Gamma$-maximin admissible according to an IP (see Remark~\ref{remarkGammaMinimax}). We, in contrast, focus on the elicitation of property values (not just forecasts) from an imprecise probability using $\Gamma$-maximin. In this way, our work also differs from \cite{gneiting2011making, fissler2021forecast, brehmer2021scoring} who consider the elicitation of ``imprecise'' interval valued (or set-valued) properties on precise probability distributions.

Properties of an IP can also be understood as structural statements about the IP, \eg, whether the IP is 2-monotone or whether it has a set of desirable gambles which is invariant to certain transformations \citep{mirandaStructuralJudgements2014}. Clearly, these binary statements are representable as properties following our definition. However, it is not clear whether these properties are elicitable in the sense we present in this paper.

\section{Background: Elicitable properties of probabilities}
Let us first recapitulate some background on property elicitation. We follow the presentation of \citep{gneiting2011making}. Fix some measurable space $(\Z, \Sigma)$. Let $\Delta(\Z)$ denote the set of probabilities on $\Z$ (the ``probability simplex'' of $\Z$). Given a set $A$, we denote its power set by $2^A$.

\begin{definition}[Elicitable property]
\label{def:elicitable property}
    Let $\R \subseteq \mathbb R^p$ be some set of property values.
    A \emph{property} is a function $f : \mathbb P \to 2^\R$ for some $\mathbb P \subseteq \Delta(\Z)$. For $\theta \in \R$, the \emph{level set} $\L_\theta$ of a property is defined as
    \begin{align*}
        \L_\theta = \{P \in \mathbb P \colon \theta \in f(P) \},
    \end{align*}
    (Without loss of generality we assume throughout that $\R = \cup_{P \in \mathbb{P}} f(P)$.)

    A property $f$ is \emph{elicitable} if there exists a loss function $\ell : \R \times \Z \to \mathbb R$ such that for all $P \in \mathbb P$,
    \[f(P) = \argmin_{\theta \in \R} \mathbb E_{Z \sim P} [ \ell(\theta, Z) ].\]
\end{definition}

It will typically be the case that $f(P)$ is a singleton set---\ie usually a property $f$ can be considered as a map $\mathbb P \to \R$. However, restricting ourselves to point-valued properties poses a complication: For $f : \mathbb P \to \R$ to be elicitable by the loss $\ell$, we would have to assume that the minimizer of the risk $\mathbb E_{Z \sim P} [ \ell(\theta, Z)$ is unique. To avoid making this assumption, we instead allow properties to be set-valued. Intuitively, this is akin to bundling together all the point-valued properties which are elicited by a non-uniquely-minimized loss. %When $\R$ is in a natural correspondence with $\mathbb P$ (and $f : \mathbb P \to \R$ gives this correspondence) and the minimizer of $\ell$ is unique, the loss $\ell$ can be considered as a strictly proper scoring rule for the property elicited by $\ell$.

We also highlight that a property may be defined only on a subset $\mathbb P$ of the probability simplex $\Delta(\Z)$. This is useful because there are many properties (\eg the mean) that are not defined for all distributions.

We further introduce a special type of property pair, which will be of use in later discussion.
\begin{definition}[Bayes pair \cite{embrechts2021bayes}]
    Let $\ell \colon \R \times \Z \to \mathbb R$ be a loss function. The property pair $(\Theta, L_\Theta)$ is called a \emph{Bayes pair}, if, $\Theta$ is the property elicited by $\ell$ and, for all $P \in \mathbb P$,
    \begin{align*}
        L_\Theta(P) = \min_{\theta \in \R} \mathbb{E}_{Z \sim P}[\ell(\theta, Z)].
    \end{align*}
\end{definition}
A Bayes pair $(\Theta, L_{\Theta})$ has special structure: $L_\Theta$ is the minimum risk and $\Theta$ the set of minimizers. Note that $L_\theta$ is elicitable on level sets of $\Theta$ \citep[Theorem 3]{frongillo2021elicitation}. While the minimizer is central to forecasting \citep{gneiting2011making}, the minimum risk is of interest in mathematical finance \citep{embrechts2021bayes}.
\begin{example}
\label{Bayes pair for squared loss}
    Let $\Z \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ and $\R \subseteq \Z$. Suppose that $\mathbb P$ is the set of all distributions with finite second moments. Let $\ell(\theta, z) = (\theta - z)^2$ be the loss function. For $P \in \mathbb P$, the property $\Theta(P)$ of the corresponding Bayes Pair is the mean of $P$,
    because
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb{E}_{Z \sim P}[(\theta-Z)^2] = \theta^2 -2 \theta \mathbb{E}_{Z \sim P}[Z] + \mathbb{E}_{Z \sim P}[Z^2]\,,
    \end{align*}
    is minimized by $\theta = \mathbb{E}_{Z \sim P}[Z]$. The corresponding $L_\Theta(P)$ is the variance of $P$ since
    \begin{align*}
        \min_{\theta \in \R} \mathbb{E}_{Z \sim P}[(\theta, Z)^2] = \mathbb{E}_{Z \sim P}[(\mathbb{E}_{Z \sim P}[Z]-Z)^2]\,.
    \end{align*}
\end{example}

\section{Elicitable IP-properties via minimax}
Given the introductory remarks in the previous section, we propose the following definition of an IP-property. Note that we call a set of probability distributions---\ie, a subset of $\Delta(\Z)$---an \emph{imprecise probability (IP)}.
\begin{definition}[Elicitable IP-property]
\label{def:elicitable IP-property}
    Let $\R \subseteq \mathbb R^p$ be a set of property values.
    An \emph{IP-property} is a function $f : \scP \to 2^\R$ for some $\scP \subseteq \TwoP$ with $\emptyset \notin \scP$. For $\theta \in \R$, the \emph{level set} $\LIP_{\theta}$ of a property is defined as
    \begin{align*}
        \LIP_{\theta} = \{\P \in \scP :  \theta \in f(\P) \}\,,
    \end{align*}
    (As for precise probabilities, we assume without loss of generality that $\R = \cup_{\P \in \scP} f(\P)$.)

    An IP-property $f$ is \emph{elicitable} if there exists a loss function $\ell : \R \times \Z \to \mathbb R$ such that, for all $\P \in \scP$, % and all $\theta^* \in f(\P)$,
    \begin{align*}
        %\sup_{P \in \P} \mathbb{E}_{Z \sim P}[\ell(\theta^*, Z)] \le \sup_{P \in \P} \mathbb{E}_{Z \sim P}[\ell(\theta, Z)].
        f(\P) = \argmin_{\theta \in \R} \sup_{P \in \P} \mathbb{E}_{Z \sim P}[\ell(\theta, Z)]\,.
    \end{align*}
\end{definition}

As with precise properties, we allow an IP-property to be defined only on a subset $\scP$ of the set $\TwoP$ of all IPs. Nevertheless, as we show in the following lemma, elicitable IP-properties can be defined on $\TwoP$ if we make assumptions that ensure a minimizer of the relevant loss function exists. Under additional assumptions, we can also guarantee that this minimizer is unique. (These conditions are satisfied when, for instance, $\Z \subseteq \mathbb R^p$; $\R$ is a closed box in $\mathbb R^p$ and $\ell(\theta, z) = \|\theta-z\|_2^2$.)
\begin{lemma}[Existence and uniqueness of elicitation values]
\label{lemma:Existence and Uniqueness of Elicitation Value}
    If $\R$ is compact, and $\ell$ lower semi-continuous in $\theta$, then a (potentially non-unique) minimum of $\theta \mapsto \sup_{P \in \mathcal P} \mathbb E_{Z \sim P} [ \ell (\theta, Z) ]$ is attained for some $\theta^* \in \R$.
    If furthermore $\R$ is convex and $\ell \colon \R \times \Z \to \mathbb R$ strictly convex in $\R$ for all $z \in \Z$, then the minimizer $\theta^* \in \R$ is unique.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    The results follows from the fact that a point-wise supremum over a lower semi-continuous function is lower-semi-continuous \cite[15.23.d]{schechter1997handbook}, and that a minimum is then attained if this function ranges over a compact set  \cite[17.7.i]{schechter1997handbook}.
    We show the second statement by contradiction. Suppose that $\R$ is convex, $\ell$ strictly convex and $\theta^* \in \R$ is a non-unique minimizer. Then, there exists another minimizer $\theta' \in \R$ such that $\theta' \neq \theta^*$. Let $\alpha \in (0,1)$, and define $\theta_\alpha = \alpha \theta^* + (1-\alpha)\theta' \in \R$. But then,
    \begin{align*}
        \sup_{P \in \mathcal P} \mathbb E_{Z \sim P} [ \ell (\theta_\alpha, Z) ] &< \sup_{P \in \mathcal P} \mathbb E_{Z \sim P} [  \alpha \ell (\theta^*, Z) + (1-\alpha) \ell(\theta',Z)]\\
        &= \sup_{P \in \mathcal P}  \alpha \mathbb E_{Z \sim P} [  \ell (\theta^*, Z)] +  (1-\alpha) \mathbb E_{Z \sim P} [  \ell(\theta',Z)]\\
        &\le \sup_{P \in \mathcal P}  \alpha \mathbb E_{Z \sim P} [  \ell (\theta^*, Z)] +  \sup_{P \in \mathcal P} (1-\alpha) \mathbb E_{Z \sim P} [  \ell(\theta',Z)] \\
        &= \min_{\theta \in \R} \sup_{P \in \mathcal P} \mathbb E_{Z \sim P} [  \ell(\theta',Z)].\qedhere
    \end{align*}
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[$\Gamma$-minimax and DRO]\label{remarkGammaMinimax}
    A special case of an elicitable IP-property $f$ is given by the loss function $\ell$ of a decision maker, with $\R$ a finite set of possible decisions. In such a setting, $f$ is the corresponding $\Gamma$-minimax---or equivalently DRO---decision rule (\cf \cite{troffaes2007decision, rahimianFrameworksResultsDistributionally2022}). Given an imprecise probability $\P$, \eg a credence about nature's state, $f(\P)$ is the decision which minimizes the worst-case risk. % best expected worst-case decision to take.

	When $\ell$ is a strictly proper scoring rule for forecasts, $f(\mathcal P)$ can be interpreted as the set of forecasts which are $\Gamma$-minimax admissible under $\mathcal P$ (as first introduced in \cite[Definition~2.1]{schervish2025elicitation}).
\end{remark}
Naturally, IP-properties strictly extend standard (precise) properties. Every standard property can be written as an IP-property using singleton IP sets. In the other direction, every IP-property implies a certain standard property, called the \emph{precise restriction} $\hat{f} : \mathbb P \to 2^\R$, which is defined as
\begin{align*}
    \hat{f}(P) &= f(\{ P\}),
\end{align*}
where $\mathbb P = \{ P \in \Delta (\Z) \mid \{ P \} \in \scP \}$.


\subsection{Necessary conditions for elicitability}

Equipped with these definitions we can approach the first question posed in the introduction: What can a model learn when trained via MDL? To this end, we provide necessary conditions that an IP-property has to satisfy in order to be elicitable---i.e. in order to be learnable by MDL. We begin by restricting our attention to so-called \emph{full} IP-properties. However, we will show later (see Remark~\ref{remarkExtend}) that this restriction is without loss of generality for elicitable properties.

\begin{definition}[Full IP-property]
\label{def:full IP-property}
    We say that an IP-property $f : \scP \to 2^\R$ is \emph{full} if all of the following conditions are satisfied:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item If $\P \in \scP$, then $\co \P \in \scP$, where $\co$ denotes the closed, convex hull.
        \item The convex combination of IPs in a level set of $f$ forms an element in the domain $\scP$. That is, for all $\P, \Q \in \LIP_\theta$ and $\alpha \in [0,1]$, the IP 
    \[\alpha \P + (1-\alpha) \Q = \{ \alpha P + (1-\alpha) Q : P \in \P, Q \in \Q\} \in \scP.\]
        \item The union of two IPs in a level set of $f$ also forms an element in the domain $\scP$. That is, if $\P, \Q \in \LIP_\theta$, then $\P \cup \Q \in \scP$.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{prop}[Necessary conditions for elicitability]
\label{prop:Necessary Conditions for Elicitability}
    Let $f\colon \scP \to 2^\R$ be a full, elicitable IP-property. Then, the following four statements hold:
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\Roman*)]
        \item \label{necessaryCondition1} For all $\P \in \scP$, $f(\P) = f(\co \P)$.
        %\jb{How do we know that $\co \P$ is in $\scP$? Do we need to add this as a condition to a full IP property?}\rd{Yes, I add now.}
        \item \label{necessaryCondition2} The level sets of $f$ are convex; \ie for all $\P, \Q \in \LIP_\theta$ and $\alpha \in [0,1]$, the IP 
    \[\alpha \P + (1-\alpha) \Q = \{ \alpha P + (1-\alpha) Q : P \in \P, Q \in \Q\}\]
    is also in $\LIP_\theta$.
        \item \label{necessaryCondition3} The level sets of $f$ are closed under arbitrary unions. That is, 
        %if $\P, \Q \in \LIP_\theta$, then $\P \cup \Q \in \LIP_\theta$.
        if $\{\P_i\}_{i \in I} \subset \LIP_\theta$, then $\bigcup_{i \in I} \P_i \in \LIP_\theta$.
        \item \label{necessaryCondition4} The precise restriction $\hat{f}$ of $f$ is elicitable.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{prop}
Note that in Condition~\ref{necessaryCondition1} (and throughout this paper), the topology is taken to be the weak topology for an appropriate choice of topological vector space which includes $\Delta(\Z)$.
\begin{proof}
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\Roman*)]
        \item By linearity and continuity of $P \mapsto \mathbb{E}_{Z \sim P}[\ell(\theta, Z)]$ we can write (\cf \citep[Theorem 7.51]{aliprantis2006infinite}),
        \begin{align*}
            \argmin_{\theta \in \R} \sup_{P \in \P} \mathbb{E}_{Z \sim P}[\ell(\theta, Z)] = \argmin_{\theta \in \R} \sup_{P \in \co \P} \mathbb{E}_{Z \sim P}[\ell(\theta, Z)].
        \end{align*}
        Hence, $\theta^* \in f(\P)$ if and only if $\theta^* \in f(\co \P)$.
        \item Let $\P, \Q \in \scP$ be such that %for an elicitable IP-property 
        $\theta^* \in f(\P)$ and $\theta^* \in f(\Q)$. Fixing some $\alpha \in [0,1]$, for all $\theta \in \R$, we have %Writing $P$ for a representative of $\P$ and $Q$ for a representative of $\Q$, 
    \begin{align*}
    \sup_{D \in \alpha \Q + (1-\alpha)\P}\mathbb{E}_{Z \sim D}[\ell(\theta^*, Z)] &= \alpha \sup_{Q \in \Q} \mathbb{E}_{Z \sim Q}[\ell(\theta^*, Z)] +(1- \alpha) \sup_{P \in \P}\mathbb{E}_{Z \sim P}[\ell(\theta^*, Z)]\\
    &\le \alpha \sup_{Q \in \Q} \mathbb{E}_{Z \sim Q}[\ell(\theta, Z)] +(1- \alpha) \sup_{P \in \P}\mathbb{E}_{Z \sim P}[\ell(\theta, Z)]\\
    &= \sup_{D \in \alpha \Q + (1-\alpha)\P}\mathbb{E}_{Z \sim D}[\ell(\theta, Z)].
    \end{align*}

    \item 
	Let $\{\P_{i}\}_{i \in I} \subset \scP$ be such that %for an elicitable IP-property 
	$\theta^* \in f(\P_i)$ for all $i \in I$, and write $\P_{\cup}$ for $\bigcup_{i \in I} \P_i$. Then Condition~\ref{necessaryCondition3} follows because, for all $\theta \in \R$,
	\begin{align*}
		\sup_{P \in \P_\cup} \mathbb E_{Z \sim P} [ \ell (\theta^*, Z) ] &= \sup_{i \in I} \sup_{P \in \P_i} \mathbb E_{Z \sim P} [ \ell (\theta^*, Z) ] \\
		&\le \sup_{i \in I}  \sup_{P \in \P_i} \mathbb E_{Z \sim P} [ \ell (\theta, Z) ] \\
		&= \sup_{P \in \P_\cup} \mathbb E_{Z \sim P} [ \ell (\theta, Z) ].
	\end{align*}

    \item This statement follows immediately from the elicitability of $f$.\qedhere % because the requirements of IP-elicitability reduce to those of standard elicitability for singleton sets.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{proof}
Note that the last condition means that the necessary and sufficient conditions for standard elicitable properties have to hold for the restriction $\hat{f}$, \ie \cite[Theorem 1]{lambert2019elicitation} and \cite[Theorem 5]{steinwart2014elicitation}.
\begin{remark}\label{remarkExtend}
    If an IP-property is elicitable, then one can, without loss of generality, extend the domain $\scP$ by taking the closed convex hull of elements in $\scP$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:Necessary Conditions for Elicitability}, Condition~\ref{necessaryCondition1}), and closure with respect to convex combinations (Proposition~\ref{prop:Necessary Conditions for Elicitability}, Condition~\ref{necessaryCondition2}) and set unions (Proposition~\ref{prop:Necessary Conditions for Elicitability}, Condition~\ref{necessaryCondition3}) of elements in each level set.
    %\jb{Possibly need to add Condition~\ref{necessaryCondition1} if we update the definition of a full IP-property include the closed convex hull}\rd{Done.}
    Hence, an elicitable IP-property can always be extended to a full elicitable property.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
    The level sets of an elicitable IP-property $f$ are also closed under taking the full convex combination of elements. That is, if $\P, \Q \in \LIP_\theta$ then 
        \[[\P, \Q] = \{ \alpha P + (1-\alpha) Q : P \in \P, Q \in \Q, \alpha \in [0,1]\},\]
        is also in $\LIP_\theta$. This follows by Conditions~\ref{necessaryCondition1} and~\ref{necessaryCondition3} of Proposition~\ref{prop:Necessary Conditions for Elicitability}, and the observation that $\co (\P \cup \Q) = \co [\P, \Q]$, because $\P \cup \Q \subseteq [\P, \Q] \subseteq \overline{\operatorname{co}} \P \cup \Q$.
\end{remark}

\begin{example}
\label{ex:level sets are not closed under intersection}
    As the level sets of an elicitable IP-property are closed under arbitrary union, a natural question is whether the level sets are also closed under intersection. Let us provide a counterexample. As this example requires the computation of the IP-properties the reader might first go through Example~\ref{ex:squared loss elicits the mean of maximum variance distribution} to get a better understanding why the argument holds.
    Consider the outcome set $\Z = \{ 0,1,2\}$. We denote probabilities $p \in \Delta(\Z)$ as $(p_0, p_1, p_2) \in [0,1]^3$. Let $\ell(\theta, z) = (\theta - z)^2$ be the loss function which elicits $f$ (\cf Example~\ref{ex:squared loss elicits the mean of maximum variance distribution}). Then, for $\P = \{ (1,0,0), (0.5, 0, 0.5)\}$,
    \begin{align*}
        f(\P) &= 1,
    \end{align*}
    the mean of $p = (0.5, 0, 0.5)$ which is the maximum variance distribution in $\co \P$ (\cf Proposition~\ref{prop:bayes pair gives ip-property}). For $\Q = \{ (1,0,0), (0.25, 0.5, 0.25)\}$, we obtain,
    \begin{align*}
        f(\Q) &= 1,
    \end{align*}
    the mean of $p = (0.25, 0.5, 0.25)$ which is the maximum variance distribution in $\co \Q$ (\cf Proposition~\ref{prop:bayes pair gives ip-property}). However, it is easy to see that,
    \begin{align*}
        f(\P \cap \Q) = f( \{(1,0,0)\} ) = 0.
    \end{align*}
    %\rd{todo make plot ? }
\end{example}

Proposition~\ref{prop:Necessary Conditions for Elicitability} provides a short list of necessary conditions for a property to be elicitable. In other words, only if a property fulfills these conditions it can be learned through MLD. A central open question is now: are these conditions also sufficient? (For comparison, see the sufficient and necessary conditions for standard (precise) properties given in \cite[Theorem 1]{lambert2019elicitation} and \cite[Theorem 5]{steinwart2014elicitation}).

\subsection{What does an elicitable IP-property elicit?}

This section is devoted to showing that the value of an IP-property elicited by the loss $\ell$ can be defined through the corresponding (precise) Bayes pair $(\Theta, L_\Theta)$ elicited by $\ell$. The motivation for this result is the second question posed in the introduction: What does a model learn when trained via MDL? We show that (asymptotically) MDL learns values of the minimizers $\Theta(P^*)$ of the probability with maximin risk $P^* \in \argmax_{P \in \P} L_\Theta(P)$. We first guide the reader through a simple example which suggests a conclusive answer to the main question of this section.
\begin{example}
\label{ex:squared loss elicits the mean of maximum variance distribution}
    Consider the binary outcome set $\Z = \{ 0,1\}$. We denote probabilities $p \in \Delta(\Z)$ as $p \in [0,1]$. Let $\ell(\theta, z) = (\theta - z)^2$ be the loss function which elicits $f$ (\cf Example~\ref{ex:squared loss elicits the mean of maximum variance distribution}). Then, for $\P = [0,0.5]$,
    \begin{align*}
        f(\P) &=\argmin_{\theta \in \mathbb R} \sup_{P \in \P} \mathbb{E}_{Z \sim P}[\ell(\theta, Z)]\\
        &=\argmin_{\theta \in \mathbb R} \sup_{p \in [0,0.5]} (1-p) \theta^2 + p (\theta - 1)^2\\
        &=\argmin_{\theta \in \mathbb R} \sup_{p \in [0,0.5]} \theta^2 - p (2\theta - 1)\\
        &=0.5.
    \end{align*}
    Note that, for $\Q = \{ 0\}$, $f(\Q) =\argmin_{\theta \in \mathbb R} \theta^2 =0$, while for $\Q' = \{ 0.5\}$, $f(\Q') =\argmin_{\theta \in \mathbb R} \theta^2 =0.5$. Hence, there exist $Q, Q' \in \P$ such that $f(\{Q\}) \neq f(\P) = f(\{ Q'\})$. In particular, observe that $f(\P) = f(\{ Q'\})$ for $Q'$ being the maximal variance distribution in the set $\P$.
\end{example}

The last observation in the above example suggests the following informal claim.

\begin{claim}
    Let $(\Theta, L_\Theta)$ be the Bayes pair corresponding to the loss function $\ell$. Then, the IP-property $f \colon \scP \to 2^\R$ elicited by $\ell$ satisfies $f(\P) \subseteq \Theta(P^*)$ for all $P^* \in \argmax_{P \in \P} L_\Theta(P)$.
\end{claim}
This claim is not new and can be made formal under several technical restrictions (\cf \citep{grunwald2004game, frohlich2024scoring, schervish2025elicitation}).
\begin{prop}
\label{prop:bayes pair gives ip-property}
    Suppose the loss function $\ell$ is lower semi-continuous and convex in $\theta$ for every $z \in \Z$, and 
    %Let $\ell$ be
    upper semi-continuous in $z \in \Z$ for every $\theta \in \R$.
    Suppose the set of property values $\R$ be convex.
    Let $(\Theta, L_\Theta)$ be the Bayes pair corresponding to the loss function $\ell$. 
    Let $\P \in \scP$ be closed and convex, and suppose $\Theta$ is defined for every element in $\P$.
    Then the IP-property $f \colon \scP \to 2^\R$ elicited by $\ell$ satisfies $f(\P) \subseteq \Theta(P^*)$ for all $P^* \in \argmax_{P \in \P} L_\Theta(P)$.
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
    Let $\P \in \scP$ with $\theta^* \in f(\mathcal P)$. Hence,
    \begin{align*}
        \inf_{\theta \in \R} \sup_{P \in \P} \mathbb E_{Z \sim P} [ \ell (\theta, Z) ] = \sup_{P \in \P} \mathbb E_{Z \sim P} [ \ell (\theta^*, Z) ].
    \end{align*}
    Sion's maxmin-theorem  \cite[Corollary 3.3]{sion1958general} guarantees that
    \begin{align*}
        \inf_{\theta \in \R} \sup_{P \in \P} \mathbb E_{Z \sim P} [ \ell (\theta, Z) ] = \sup_{P \in \P} \inf_{\theta \in \R} \mathbb E_{Z \sim P} [ \ell (\theta, Z) ].
    \end{align*}
    Note that all conditions for the theorem are fulfilled: (a) $\P$ is convex and compact because it is the closed subset of the compact set $\Delta(\Z)$ \citep[5.105 Alaoglu’s Compactness Theorem]{aliprantis2006infinite}. %\footnote{All topological statements are with respect to the weak$^*$ topology for an appropriate choice of topological vector space which includes $\Delta(\Z)$.} 
    (b) $\R$ is convex. (c) $\ell(\theta, P) = \mathbb E_{Z \sim P} [ \ell (\theta, Z) ]$ is lower semi-continuous and convex in $\theta$ for every $P \in \P$. Furthermore, it is upper semi-continuous \cite[Theorem 9.1.5(v)]{stroock2010probability} and linear in $P$ for every $\theta \in \R$.

    Since $\ell(\theta, P) = \mathbb E_{Z \sim P} [ \ell (\theta, Z) ]$ is upper semi-continuous in $P$,  it follows that $\inf_{\theta \in \R} \mathbb E_{Z \sim P} [ \ell (\theta, Z) ]$ is also upper semi-continuous in $P$ \cite[15.23.d]{schechter1997handbook}. Moreover, because $\P$ is compact, there exists $P^* \in \P$ \cite[17.7.i]{schechter1997handbook} such that,
    \begin{align*}
        \sup_{P \in \P} \inf_{\theta \in \R} \mathbb E_{Z \sim P} [ \ell (\theta, Z) ] = \inf_{\theta \in \R} \mathbb E_{Z \sim P^*} [ \ell (\theta, Z) ].
    \end{align*}
    Hence, $P^* \in \argmax_{P \in  \P}L_\Theta(P)$. Having established that such a $P^*$ exists, we let $P^*$ be an arbitrary element of $\argmax_{P \in  \P}L_\Theta(P)$ in the remainder of the proof.

    By definition of $\Theta$, for all $\Bar{\theta} \in \Theta(P^*)$,
    \begin{align*}
        \inf_{\theta \in \R} \mathbb E_{Z \sim P^*} [ \ell (\theta, Z) ] = \mathbb E_{Z \sim P^*} [ \ell (\Bar{\theta}, Z) ].
    \end{align*}
    We can plug the obtained equalities together to give,
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb E_{Z \sim P^*} [ \ell (\theta^*, Z) ] \le \sup_{P \in \P} \mathbb E_{Z \sim P} [ \ell (\theta^*, Z) ] = \mathbb E_{Z \sim P^*} [ \ell (\Bar{\theta}, Z) ],
    \end{align*}
    from which it follows that $f(\P) \subseteq \Theta(P^*)$. %Furthermore, $P^* \in \argmax_{P \in  \P}L_\Theta(P)$ was arbitrary, which concludes the statement.
\end{proof}
Note that $\ell$ is upper semi-continuous in $z \in \Z$ for every $\theta \in \R$ if $\Z$ is discrete (\ie finite or countable) and equipped with the discrete topology.
Furthermore, if $\R$ is a finite-dimensional simplex, then $\ell$ simply needs to be bounded above to ensure the conclusions in Proposition~\ref{prop:bayes pair gives ip-property} hold. To prove this, Sion's maxmin-theorem has to be replaced by the minimax-theorem \cite[p. 82]{ferguson1967mathematical}. The rest of the proof follows analogously. For related proofs, see \cite[Theorem 5.1 and 6.1]{grunwald2004game}. The following corollary follows immediately from Proposition~\ref{prop:bayes pair gives ip-property}.
\begin{corollary}
\label{corollary:level sets and intersection with IP}
    Let $f$ be a full, elicitable IP-property and suppose all of the conditions of Proposition~\ref{prop:bayes pair gives ip-property} are fulfilled. 
    Then, 
    for all closed and convex, $\P \in \scP$, if $\L_\theta \neq \emptyset$ and $\L_{\theta} \cap \P = \emptyset$, then $\theta \notin f(\P)$, where $\L_\theta$ denotes the level set of the precise restriction $\hat{f}$ of $f$.
\end{corollary}

To support the reader's intuition we provide a short list of exemplary loss functions and the corresponding IP-properties they elicit.
\begin{example}
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item If $\ell(z, \theta) = (z- \theta)^2$ and $\Z = [-C, C]$ for some constant $C > 0$, 
        %then the property $f$ elicited by $\ell$ assigns to $\P \in \scP$ 
        then, for all $\P \in \TwoP$, $\ell$ elicits
        the mean of maximum variance distribution in $\P$ \citep[Example 1.(iii)]{embrechts2021bayes}.
        \item If $\ell(z, \theta) = |z- \theta|$ and $\Z = [-C, C]$, 
        %then the property $f$ elicited by $\ell$ assigns to $\P \in \scP$ 
        then, for all $\P \in \TwoP$, $\ell$ elicits
        the median of the maximum mean-median deviation distribution in $\P$ \citep[Example 1.(v)]{embrechts2021bayes}.
        \item If $\ell(z, \theta) = \theta + \frac{e^{\gamma(y - \theta)}}{\gamma}$ for some $\gamma \in (0,\infty)$ and $\Z = [-C, C]$, 
        %then the property $f$ elicited by $\ell$ assigns to $\P \in \scP$ 
        then, for all $\P \in \TwoP$, $\ell$ elicits
        the maximal $\gamma$-entropic risk measure of the distributions in $\P$ \citep[Example 1.(ii)]{embrechts2021bayes}.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{example}

\section{Conclusion and open questions}
In this paper, we provide a list of necessary conditions for IP-properties to be elicitable. In particular, the level sets of the property have to be convex and closed under union. Hence, only such properties \emph{can} be learned in MDL. We further show that an IP-property elicited through maximin is equal to the standard property elicited on the maximum Bayes risk distribution. MDL \emph{learns} the property of the maximum Bayes risk distribution. Nevertheless, this work leaves a series of interesting open questions unanswered:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item For the standard setting, elicitability can be related to identifiability: A property is identifiable if it can be identified through a minimizer condition (the expected derivative of the loss function is equal to zero) \citep{steinwart2014elicitation}. What is the analogue of identifiability in the imprecise case? How can elicitability and identifiability be related for IP-properties?
    \item Which conditions on an IP-property guarantee the existence of a loss function which elicits it? In other words, what are sufficient conditions for elicitability? %(\cf Proposition~\ref{prop:Necessary Conditions for Elicitability})
    \item There can be multiple loss functions which elicit the same precise property. A standard example is the Bregman divergences, which all elicit the mean \citep{savage1971elicitation}. 
    But an IP $\P$ has multiple means---one for each probability $P \in \P$---and different Bregman divergences may elicit different means for the same IP $\P$. That is to say, the choice of Bregman divergence may change the probability $P \in \Pr$ whose mean is elicited. More generally, does there exist a relationship between families of loss functions which elicit the same precise property, but not the same IP-property (\cf the characterization of all scoring functions which elicit a real-valued property in \citep{steinwart2014elicitation})?
    \item Conditional value at risk (CVar) at level $\alpha$ and the $\alpha$-quantile are closely connected \citep{embrechts2021bayes}. If $\P$ is the IP-set corresponding to the conditional value at risk (CVar) at level $\alpha$, then what is elicited by the loss function which elicits---in the precise setting---the $\alpha$-quantile?
\end{enumerate}

