Fig.~\ref{fig:explosion2-in-sand} shows the evolution of sand barrier breakaway and shock wave propagation in the air surrounding the destroyed sand cylinder. A significant difference in the velocity between the expelling detonation products and fragments of the destroyed barrier is observed. The barrier during fracture takes away the part of the momentum from the detonation products, thus weakening the shock wave. At the same time, the barrier is fragmented into safe, low-velocity pieces.

