\begin{abstract}
We study  stationary fluctuations
of conserved slow modes in a two-lane model of hardcore particles which 
are expected to show universal behaviour.  
Specifically,  we focus on  the properties of fluctuations at a special  umbilic  point where the characteristic velocities coincide.    At large space and time scales, fluctuations are described by  a system of stochastic Burgers equations studied recently in
 \cite{2024Spohn}. Our data suggest coupling-dependent scaling functions and, even more surprisingly, coupling-dependent dynamical scaling exponents, distinct from  KPZ scaling exponent typical for surface growth processes.
\end{abstract}


