\subsection{Other values of the coupling }
To support our findings, we have investigated the space-time correlator $S_{\alpha\alpha}(x,t)$ at different values of the coupling parameter $\ga$.  Only for  $\ga=1$,  the point where the dynamics splits into two independent totally asymmetric processes, do we retrieve the well-known KPZ scaling of a stochastic scalar Burgers equation $z_1=z_2 \equiv 3/2$,  with $ f_{\alpha} = f_{\rm KPZ} $ the celebrated KPZ scaling function obtained in Ref.~\cite{Prae04}.  For all other 
value of the coupling parameter $\ga<1$ we find not only scaling functions distinct from $f_{\rm KPZ}$ but also different dynamical  exponents, see Fig.~\ref{FigZ1Z2vGa}. 
 We observe a clearly tendency of both $z_1,z_2 \rightarrow 3/2 $ as $\ga$ increases towards $\ga=1$, 
the point of decoupling into separate simple  exclusion processes. 
Also the shape of scaling functions $f_1,f_2$  become closer to KPZ as $\ga$ increases towards $1$, data not shown. 
The data for the regime $\ga < \ga_{\rm crit}$ which correspond to an isolated umbilic point ( saddle point topology) are less transparent. 
The critical value of the coupling $\ga = 1/4$, at which the saddle 
point appears,  corresponds to the maximal
$|z_1-z_2|$ difference.
The limit $\ga \rightarrow 0$  cannot be reached via Monte Carlo simulations since the effective MC dynamics close to $\ga =0$ at the umbilic point becomes too slow. Further studies of this regime are warranted.  

