				\item $S_j(P) \notin U$, for any $\text{val}(P)\leq j\leq \text{deg}(P)$.
			\end{enumerate}
		\end{problem}

		It is usually difficult to control the partial sums of a given polynomial. It is reasonable to think that it is even more difficult to control all the partial sums of polynomials with some approximation property. In Problem \ref{Pb2}, the most natural linear form $\mu$ to consider is the evaluation at a given point, let say $1$.

		In this section, we give some solutions to Problem \ref{Pb2}. First, we will formulate and prove some polynomial approximation results.

		We use two different methods; the first one involves the notion of a Dirichlet set and the second one uses the weighted polynomial approximations mentioned in Section \ref{wpa}. We first recall the definition of a Dirichlet set.

		\begin{definition}[Dirichlet set]A set $K\subset \T$ is called a Dirichlet set if there exists an increasing sequence of integers $(n_k)_k$ such that the sequence of monomials $(z^{n_k})_k$ converges to the constant function $1$, uniformly on $K$.
		\end{definition}

		Examples of such sets are finite sets. It is known that a Dirichlet set must have Lebesgue measure $0$ and be nowhere dense. There are infinite sets that are Dirichlet sets. We refer to \cite{Kahane1969,Korner1970,Korner1974}.

		Our first result is as follows.

		\begin{lemma}\label{appr_Dir}Let $K\subset \T$ be a Dirichlet set and let $\zeta \in K$. For any $\varepsilon>0$, $N\in \N$, $\vp\in C(K)$ and any compact set $L\subset \D$, there exists a polynomial $P=\sum_{k=N}^da_kz^k$ such that:
			\begin{enumerate}
				\item $\sup_{z\in K}|P(z)-\vp(z)|< \varepsilon$;
				\item $\sup_{z\in L}|P(z)|<\varepsilon$;
				\item $\Re(\sum_{k=N}^ja_k\zeta ^k) \geq \min\{0,\Re(\vp(\zeta))\}$, for any $N\leq j\leq d$.
			\end{enumerate}
		\end{lemma}

		\begin{proof}Up to a rotation, we may assume that $\zeta =1$. By Mergelyan's theorem (since a Dirichlet set cannot be dense in $\T$, it is contained in a compact set with connected complement), there exists a polynomial $Q=\sum_{k=0}^da_kz^k$ such that
			\[
			\sup_{z\in K}|Q(z)-(\vp(z)+\varepsilon/2)|< \varepsilon/4.
			\]
			Since $K$ is a Dirichlet set, there exists a sequence $(n_j)_j$ of integers such that $(z^{n_j})_j$ converge\overline{\D}s to $1$ uniformly on $K$. Choose an integer $J_0\geq N$ large enough in such a way that, for any $j_k\geq J_0$, $0\leq k \leq d$,
			\[
			\sup_{z\in K}\left|Q(z)-\sum_{k=0}^d a_kz^{k+n_{j_k}}\right| < \varepsilon/4\quad\text{and }\sup_{z\in L}\left|\sum_{k=N}^d a_kz^{k+n_{j_k}}\right| <\varepsilon.
			\]
			Now, select $j_k\geq J_0$, $0\leq k \leq d$, so that  for any $0\leq k,k'\leq d$ such that $\Re(a_k)\geq 0$ and $\Re(a_{k'})<0$, we have $k+n_{j_k} < k'+n_{j_{k'}}$. Then it is easily checked that the polynomial $P(z):=\sum_{k=0}^d a_kz^{k+n_{j_k}}$ does the job. Indeed, (1) and (2) are clear by the triangle inequality. For (3), it suffices to observe, first, that $\Re(P(1))>\Re(\vp(1))$ and, second, that if we write $P$ as $P(z)=\sum_{i=N}^{d'}b_iz^i$, then there exists $N\leq i_0\leq d'$ such that for any $N\leq i \leq i_0$, $\Re(b_i)\geq 0$, and for any $i_0 <i\leq d'$, $\Re(b_i)<0$.
		\end{proof}

		\medskip

		The previous lemma is interesting because it provides with polynomial approximation on a compact set, with constrained on the partial sums evaluated at a point of this compact set. However, Dirichlet sets are very specific and small, in a sense. It is seemingly difficult to extend such result to more standard instances of compact sets, such as intervals, arcs or discs. The second approach to Costakis-Peris problem, developed in the next subsection, will provide us with some more satisfying examples.

