	To visualize the orientation of the director field within the highly aligned regions, as depicted in Fig.~\ref{fig:figure_4}(b), we map each orientation on the unit sphere to a distinct color, ensuring that the antipodal points share the same color. This mapping, while not unique, is achieved by interpolating between three base colors---magenta, yellow, and cyan---based on the squared components of the director field $\boldsymbol{n} = (n_x, n_y, n_z)$. Specifically, the fractions $n_z^2$, $n_y^2$, and $n_x^2$ determine the contributions of magenta, yellow, and cyan, respectively, as illustrated in the legend in Fig.~\ref{fig:figure_4}(d). This color scheme reveals coherent structures within the director field, highlighting that each highly aligned region is also found to be spatially oriented in the same direction. These are plotted in Fig.~\ref{fig:figure_4}(d), along with the highly dense regions of heterochromatin, shown in dark gray (see legend). The snapshots correspond to the dynamics shown in {Videos~7, 8, and 9}.

