	%%  #6  %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
	\section{Discussion}
	%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

	Our continuum framework captures how euchromatin and heterochromatin, together with the surrounding solvent (the nucleoplasm), drive the formation of chromatin compartments, highlighting both the demixing of chromatin components and their ensuing morphological changes. We show that heterochromatin can spontaneously form droplets through a mechanically driven phase separation governed by frictional, viscous, and contractile forces. This demixing is primarily controlled by contractile stresses arising from heterochromatin cross-linking proteins, leading to the formation of nascent droplets that subsequently coalesce into larger ones. This provides a physical mechanism for chromatin phase separation, where heterochromatin-rich regions segregate from transcriptionally active euchromatin-rich domains.
	\\
	%
	\indent
	In an open or periodic system, such coarsening process typically results in a single large heterochromatin droplet. However, within a confined geometry such as a spherical nucleus, heterochromatin relocates to the nuclear periphery, wetting the boundary and forming a shell-like layer. This localization arises entirely from mechanical effects, {without requiring a preferential heterochromatin-lamin binding}. Previously, polymer-based models assuming a binding of heterochromatin to the nuclear lamins found a peripheral accumulation of heterochromatin \cite{Mirny2019, Spakowitz2020,Saintillan2022}. In contrast, our model imposes a no-slip boundary condition on both euchromatin and heterochromatin, and thus enforces that neither component can move relative to the nuclear envelope. Interestingly, despite this non-preferential treatment, only heterochromatin accumulates at the periphery. This is not due to specific energetic interactions with the nuclear envelope, but as a result of its contractile stresses and the spatial constraints imposed by the confinement. This is consistent with observations across various cell types, where heterochromatin is predominantly localized near the nuclear periphery \cite{Bizhanova2021}.
	\\
	%
	\indent
	Introducing euchromatin activity disrupts this peripheral localization, causing continuous deformations of the heterochromatin layer. These shape changes of the interfacial boundary manifest as protrusions and blebs that can detach and rejoin over time, reflecting a dynamic remodeling of the chromatin organization. Such an active remodeling suggests that molecular motors play a critical role in maintaining chromatin in a dynamic state, preventing static configurations, and enabling perpetual local rearrangements.
	\\
	%
	\indent
	Previous continuum models that described chromatin as a two-fluid system, accounted for chromatin and nucleoplasm as two separate phases \cite{Bruinsma2014, Eshghi2023, Grosberg2023}. Our current approach distinguishes euchromatin and heterochromatin as two distinct compressible fluids, which interact with an incompressible nucleoplasm via friction. This distinction allows us to capture the separate mechanical roles of heterochromatin condensation and euchromatin-driven extensile stresses. In contrast to free-energy-based models \cite{Mirny2019, Safran2021, Safran2023, Potoyan2024}, which describe phase separation in equilibrium or near-equilibrium settings, our mechanical approach is based on mass and momentum conservation laws. Thus, it allows for capturing both passive and active stresses as well as induced flows, which together dynamically drive chromatin density variations and shape nuclear organization. This provides a mechanical framework that links the genome organization inside the nucleus to a minimal set of parameters, while providing key physical insights.
	\\
	%
	\indent
	A major consequence of euchromatin activity in our model is the emergence of large-scale, correlated flows that influence chromatin organization, with spatial correlations dependent on the strength of active forces. Such coherent motions have been observed in live-cell experiments, where chromatin exhibits correlated displacements over micrometer scales \cite{Zidovska2013}. We find that at moderate activity levels, euchromatin and heterochromatin exhibit long-range coherent flows, suggesting collective motion across extended regions of the nucleus. 
	\\
	%
	\indent
	Finally, we propose a feedback mechanism between heterochromatin condensation and euchromatin-driven activity. As heterochromatin occupies more of the nuclear interior, euchromatin becomes confined to a smaller volume, increasing its local density and amplifying the active stresses it generates. These enhanced stresses, in turn, destabilize and reshape the heterochromatin layer, creating a dynamic interplay between chromatin organization and active mechanics.
	\\
	%
	\indent
	Taken together, our results suggest that large-scale nuclear organization emerges from the interplay among active processes, mechanical forces, and geometric constraints. Future studies could extend this framework by incorporating additional factors, such as spatially heterogeneous chromatin rheology, chromatin-binding proteins, and chromatin-lamina interactions, as well as by testing these predictions experimentally. Such efforts promise new insights into how physical and biochemical processes collectively shape genome organization in the nucleus.

