%driven by models such as AlphaFold2/3 and ESMFold, 
Recent advances in protein structure prediction have achieved near-atomic accuracy for well-folded proteins. However, current benchmarks inadequately assess model performance in biologically challenging contexts, especially those involving intrinsically disordered regions (IDRs), limiting their utility in applications like drug discovery, disease variant interpretation, and protein interface design. We introduce \model, a comprehensive benchmark for evaluating protein structure prediction models (PSPMs) under structural disorder and complex biological conditions. \model spans three key axes: (1) Data complexity—covering disordered regions, G protein-coupled receptors (GPCR)–ligand pairs, and multimeric complexes; (2) Task diversity—benchmarking twelve leading PSPMs across structure-based tasks with unified classification, regression, and interface metrics; and (3) Interpretability—via the \model Portal\footnotetext[1]{Code, Data and Portal can be accessed through: https://github.com/Susan571/DisProtBench.git}, offering precomputed 3D structures and visual error analyses. Our results reveal significant variability in model robustness under disorder, with low-confidence regions linked to functional prediction failures. Notably, global accuracy metrics often fail to predict task performance in disordered settings, emphasizing the need for function-aware evaluation. \model establishes a reproducible, extensible, and biologically grounded framework for assessing next-generation PSPMs in realistic biomedical scenarios.
%Recent advances in protein structure prediction have demonstrated unprecedented atomic-level accuracy for well-defined proteins. However, current benchmarks fail to rigorously evaluate those AI-predicted 3D protein structures in biologically challenging contexts, particularly those involving intrinsic disorder regions (IDR). These limitations hinder the deployment of prediction models in applications like drug discovery, disease variant interpretation, and protein interface design. We introduce \model, a comprehensive benchmark for evaluating protein structure prediction models (PSPMs) under intrinsic disorder and complex biological scenarios. It spans three axes: (1) Data complexity, with curated datasets capturing disordered regions, clinically relevant G protein-coupled receptors(GPCR)-ligand pairs, and multimeric protein complexes; (2) Task diversity, benchmarking twelve leading PSPMs across structure-based tasks such as protein–protein interaction and ligand-binding prediction using unified classification, regression, and interface metrics; and (3) Interpretability, via the \model Portal\footnotetext[1]{https://github.com/Susan571/DisProtBench.git}, which provides precomputed 3D structures for all proteins in the benchmark datasets, allowing users to inspect predicted protein structures without running models locally, alongside comparison of model outputs and error analysis across structural and functional dimensions. Our experimental results highlight substantial variability in model robustness under structural uncertainty, showing that low-confidence regions correlate strongly with disorder and functional prediction drop-offs. Notably, our results suggest that global accuracy metrics may not be insufficient predictors of task performance in disordered or flexible regions, underscoring the need for function-centric evaluation. \model provides a reproducible, extensible, community-driven infrastructure for assessing next-generation protein structure predictors in realistic, high-impact biomedical settings by aligning structural fidelity with biological relevance.
\end{abstract}

