According to \cite{Li2021} and \cite{Zhao2018}, SH is closely related to data diversity, as it reflects the extent to which the global model approximates the performance of a model trained on IID data. In this context, we aim for a DM that generalizes well on non-IID data by using techniques that maximize SH. However, in HFL, clients are assigned directly to edge servers for aggregation, which makes the SH score at \textit{client} level in \eqref{eq:stat_homo} insufficient. 
Therefore, we compute the SH score at \textit{edge server} level for the aggregation on the central server. 
In HFL, the \textit{accumulated distribution} for an edge server is the weighted sum of the label distributions from all clients assigned to that edge server. 
%This distribution is dynamically updated as clients participate in training rounds. 
This accumulated distribution must be \textit{refreshed} (re-initialized) dynamically at a specific frequency to maintain an accurate representation of the data of the participating clients. In HFL, aggregation occurs more frequently than in FL, so this refreshment is synchronized with the frequency of FL aggregation, which is denoted by $r_{g}$ in this work.
