%Given the short lifetime of the excited state, it is difficult to capture the transient structural and electronic dynamics preceding dissociation. Even in ND$_3$, where the dissociation proceeds more slowly, previous UED studies failed to resolve the transient dynamics~\cite{X}. Here, we observe a short-lived transient feature appearing at 70~fs in the early stage of the reaction, characterized not only by enhanced small-angle scattering but also by an isolated weak positive peak near 5~$\text{\AA}^{-1}$. This peak can be qualitatively attributed to charge pairs with an approximate bond length of ${2\pi}/{5~\mathrm{\AA}^{-1}}\approx1.26~\text{\AA}$, but due to the complexity of the reaction channels and products, it is not suitable to assign a specific structure to this feature. To gain insight into the observed feature, we present the \textit{ab initio} PD signals (100\% excitation) for a range of bond angles along the umbrella motion induced by photoexcitation~\cite{} in Fig.~\ref{fig:PD_exp}(c). We find that the positions of these peaks (as indicated by the arrows in Fig.~\ref{fig:PD_exp}(c)) can be well estimated using the relation $2\pi/R_\text{N-H}$ and the PD can reproduce the peak near 5~$\text{\AA}^{-1}$ when the N--H bond length reaches approximately 1.28~$\text{\AA}$. Accounting for the time resolution, we average the PDs calculated for structures with different pyramid angles and scale the result according to the experimental data. The resulting PD in Fig.~\ref{fig:PD_exp}(d) shows excellent quantitative agreement with the experimental measurement, indicating that the umbrella-mode transitional structure in the predissociation stage has been successfully captured. Furthermore, to better understand the influence of the valence electron motion and electron-correlation–induced inelastic scattering on the PD, we display the PD obtained from the IAM calculation in Fig.~\ref{fig:PD_exp}(d). The main differences are concentrated below 4~$\text{\AA}^{-1}$. However, the momentum-space signals (PD) alone provide limited intuitive insight into the structural evolution and electronic dynamics of the system. In the following, we demonstrate how the CPDF enables real-space analysis of the evolution of all charge pairs.

A distinctive benefit of UED is that the electrons' wavelength is considerably short, enabling the measurement of the scattering signal over a broad momentum transfer range. This, in turn, facilitates precise inversion to retrieve the information in real-space. However, conventional PDF analysis based on IAM requires the removal of low-angle inelastic scattering signals, which introduces substantial truncation errors in the low-$s$ region and compromises information pertaining to valence electrons. The aforementioned issues can be circumvented by employing CPDF which inverts the full scattering signal to recover charge-pair information in real-space. Moreover, given that electrons and hydrogen nuclei carry the same unit charge, the changes in the scattering signal induced by valence electron motion are comparable to those from hydrogen atoms. Consequently, the CPDF facilitates the concurrent imaging of both valence electrons and hydrogen dynamics.



Fig.~\ref{fig:CPDF_exp}(a) shows the inverted experimental $\Delta$CPDF. It should be noted that the interpretation of the positive and negative values in the $\Delta$CPDF is dependent on the product of the charges. For electron–electron (ee) and nucleus–nucleus (NN) pairs, a positive signal indicate the increase in pair density at a certain distance. By contrast, for electron–nucleus (eN) pairs, due to their opposite charges, a positive $\Delta$CPDF value indicates a decrease in pair density. The most distinct features of the measured $\Delta$CPDF are a bleached band near 1~$\text{\AA}$ and “U”-shaped enhanced bands surrounding it. Given that the dominant signal in NH$_3$ dissociation originates from the breaking of the N–H bond at approximately 1~$\text{\AA}$, the negative band that emerges at this distance at around 100 fs signifies the decrease in pair density for N–H NN pairs. 

To gain a more profound understanding of the features observed in the experimental $\Delta$CPDF, the theoretical $\Delta$CPDF is calculated from \textit{ab initio} simulations (Fig.~\ref{fig:CPDF_exp}(b)) and the theoretical $\Delta$PDF is derived from IAM calculations (Fig.~\ref{fig:CPDF_exp}(c)). The \textit{ab initio} $\Delta$CPDF in Fig.~\ref{fig:CPDF_exp}(b) also exhibited a “U”-shaped positive feature near 1~$\text{\AA}$, in good agreement with the measurement. In contrast, the IAM-derived $\Delta$PDF in Fig.~\ref{fig:CPDF_exp}(c) exhibited solely a broad negative signal below 2~$\text{\AA}$ and a broad positive signal above 2~$\text{\AA}$, a typical feature for dissociation. As illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:CPDF_exp}(b), a negative band between 2 and 4~$\text{\AA}$ is also evident, peaking around time zero and decaying as the time delay increases. It is evident that the ground-state structure of NH$_3$ contains virtually no charge-pairs beyond 2~$\text{\AA}$. Therefore, any signal change in this region, whether positive or negative, must originate from the formation of new charge-pairs, and only eN pairs can contribute negative signals as pair density increases. Because of the nuclei's comparatively slower movement relative to the electrons, the pronounced negative signal around time zero likely arises from the motion of the electrons. %Furthermore, a close comparison between Fig.~\ref{fig:CPDF_exp}(a) and Fig.~\ref{fig:CPDF_exp}(b) indicate that the positive and negative signals in the 2.5--4~$\text{\AA}$ region observed in experiment were missing in the \textit{ab initio} calculation. 

%In addition to this feature, we identify a surrounding “U”-shaped positive signal near 1~$\text{\AA}$, as well as a positive band in 2.5--3~$\text{\AA}$ and a negative band between 3--4~$\text{\AA}$. These patterns cannot be explained solely by changes in nuclear geometry, indicating a significant contribution from valence electrons to the $\Delta$CPDF. To better understand the features observed in the experimental $\Delta$CPDF, we show the CPDF calculated from \textit{ab initio} simulations and the PDF derived from IAM calculations in Figs.~\ref{fig:CPDF_exp}(b) and \ref{fig:CPDF_exp}(c), respectively. The \textit{ab initio} CPDF in Fig.~\ref{fig:CPDF_exp}(b) also exhibits a “U”-shaped feature near 1~$\text{\AA}$, consistent with the experimental observation. In contrast, the IAM-derived PDF in Fig.~\ref{fig:CPDF_exp}(c) shows only a negative signal associated with the decrease in NN pair density due to dissociation, and the “U”-shaped feature is absent. This further confirms that the “U”-shaped signal arises from valence electron motion. 

%This discrepancy can be attributed to an underestimation in the theoretical calculations of the contributions from newly formed ee or NN pairs in the 2.5--3~$\text{\AA}$ region. In the early stage of the reaction, the main contribution comes from the positive signals at the bottom of the “U”-shaped feature. Considering that the N--H bond length in the ground state is about 1~$\text{\AA}$, the charge-pair density in this region is expected to decrease upon dissociation. Thus, the presence of a positive signal at 1~$\text{\AA}$ highlights the significant role of eN pair dynamics in the early-time evolution. 





