All derived constraints are listed in Tab.~\ref{tab:SMEconstraints}.
The spin-dependent coefficients $\mathcal{T}_{p_{kjm}}^\text{ NR($qP$)}$ of momentum power ${k=0}$ are better constrained by \hydrogen\  maser measurements~\cite{Phillips2001,Humphrey2003,Kostelecky2015}, while those for $k=2$ and 4 are improved by 4 and 14 orders of magnitude.
This significant improvement can be entirely attributed to the use of \deuterium , taking advantage of the substantially larger proton momentum compared to \hydrogen , while it is still a similarly well-calculable system.  
The spin-independent coefficients $\mathcal{V}_{p_{kjm}}^\text{ NR}$ are constrained for the first time. \\

Since no significant SME effects have been observed, the zero-field value of the \deuterium\ hyperfine splitting $\nuD$ can be determined from the quasi-simultaneously recorded $\sigma_1$ and $\sigma_2$ transitions. The following formula is obtained by elimination of the magnetic field $x=\mu^\text{D}_- B_\text{stat}/(h \nuD)$ in the Eqs.~(\ref{eq:sigma12}): 

\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:zeroField}
    \nuD = \frac{1}{2} \sqrt{
        \nu_{\sigma_1}^2 \! + \! \nu_{\sigma_2}^2 
        +\sqrt{
                (\nu_{\sigma_1}^2 \! + \! \nu_{\sigma_2}^2)^2
            - 9 (\nu_{\sigma_1}^2 \! - \! \nu_{\sigma_2}^2)^2
        }
    }
\end{equation}

As the weighted average over all 213 resonance pairs, we obtain \SI{327384354.9}{\hertz} with a purely statistical uncertainty of \SI{1.7}{\hertz}.
Conservatively, we increase the uncertainty by a factor of 1.6 following the (modified) Birge ratio adjustment~\cite{Bodnar_2014} calculated for grouped data (see End Matter~§C for details).
The resulting  final total uncertainty of \SI{2.8}{\hertz} also encompasses systematics that could originate from the way $\nu^\text{c}$ is defined by the empirical fit. 
This is the best result for this quantity achieved in a beam experiment, improving over Ref.~\cite{Kusch1955} by an order of magnitude.
The deviation to more precise maser results~\cite{Wineland1972} amounts to \SI{2.3}{\hertz}. \\

%%% CONCLUSION %%% ####################

\textit{Conclusion---}In summary, we have provided improved and new constraints on \textit{CPT} and Lorentz symmetry violations governed by non-relativistic proton coefficients of the SME framework by searching for sidereal variations in combined frequencies of the $\sigma_1$ and $\sigma_2$ transition of the \deuterium\ ground-state hyperfine structure.
Despite inferior absolute precision in comparison to \hydrogen\  maser measurements, an improvement was possible due to a strong sensitivity enhancement mediated by the proton's momentum in the deuteron, thereby highlighting the potential of complementing \deuterium\ studies.
Moreover, the zero-field hyperfine splitting of \deuterium\ was obtained from the resonance pairs via Eq.~(\ref{eq:zeroField}).
The result agrees with the literature and, at a precision of \SI{2.8}{\hertz}, it represents the best value achieved in a \deuterium\ beam experiment to date.

With further campaigns, the \deuterium\ experiment could provide additional constraints through a boost analysis as discussed in~\cite{PhysRevD.109.055001}.
Other opportunities are opened by measurements at various static magnetic field values and especially by reversing the field direction to address those SME coefficients, which do not lead to sidereal variations as showcased for \hydrogen\ just recently~\cite{Nowak2024_PLB-HBeamSME}.
Precision and resolution enhancements are possible in the existing setup by operating at lower velocities~\cite{Killian2023,killian2024}, in an improved setup by introducing the Ramsey method~\cite{Ramsey1950,Ramsey1990experiments,Amit_CPT2019}, or in a new setup, e.g., a dedicated \deuterium\  maser~\cite{Wineland1972}.

Finally, the community at the AD/ELENA facility of CERN (Antiproton Decelerator / Extra Low ENergy Antiproton ring) is presently evaluating future opportunities offered by antideuterons. Beyond the more general possibility of comparative antimatter studies to decouple limits on \textit{CPT}-even and \textit{CPT}-odd coefficients, this system could give unique access to antineutron properties. \\

