\section{Related Work}
% The optimization and acceleration of stencil computations have been extensively studied across both CPU ~\cite{10.1145/3458817.3476154, yuansc17} and GPU architectures ~\cite{8451874, 6114468, 10.1145/2491956.2462176}. 

% On CPUs, vectorization remains a critical technique to improve the performance of stencil computations~\cite{10.1145/3458817.3476154, 10.1145/2464996.2467268, 10.1007/978-3-642-19861-8_13}. Data reuse strategies are instrumental in minimizing load/store operations and reducing register pressure by optimizing the execution order~\cite{8665800, 10.1145/2666356.2594342, zhaosc19}. Various tiling techniques have also been developed to improve data locality and cache reuse, including diamond tiling~\cite{10.1109/SC.2012.107, 10.1145/1375581.1375595}, time skewing~\cite{1592833, 10.1023/A:1015460304860}, rectangular tiling~\cite{1592745}, and tessellating tiling~\cite{yuansc17}.

% On GPUs, spatial tiling~\cite{7034720, zhaosc19, maruyama2014optimizing} and temporal tiling~\cite{Holewinski12ics, 10.1145/1542275.1542313, 10.1145/2830018.2830025, 10.1145/2400682.2400713, 7582549, 10.1145/2458523.2458526} techniques are commonly employed to leverage the parallelism and memory hierarchy inherent in GPU architectures. Additional methods, such as unrolling~\cite{10.1145/3469030}, prefetching~\cite{8820786}, and streaming~\cite{8451874}, further enhance performance. Frameworks like Brick~\cite{zhaosc19, 10.1145/3437801.3441598, 8639931} exploit data reuse opportunities within fine-grained blocks, achieving performance portability across both CPUs and GPUs. DRStencil~\cite{you2021drstencil}, on the other hand, utilizes fusion-partition optimization to accelerate stencil computations and integrates this into an effective code generation framework. While these efforts have focused on CUDA cores, some research also explores Tensor Cores for stencil computations. To our knowledge, only TCStencil~\cite{liu2022toward}, ConvStencil~\cite{10.1145/3627535.3638476} and LoraStencil~\cite{SC41406.2024.00059} exists. TCStencil applies Tensor Cores to stencil computations, which constrains its practicality. ConvStencil combines the similarities between convolution and stencil, forging a bridge between HPC and deep learning. LoraStencil introduces a low-rank adaptation approach to mitigate memory access redundancy on TCUs by decomposing stencil weight matrices into smaller rank-1 matrices. Additionally, libraries like cuDNN~\cite{cudnn, chetlur2014cudnn}, developed by NVIDIA, provide optimized implementations for functions such as convolution, while AMOS~\cite{zheng2022amos} maps various operations, including depth-wise convolutions equivalent to stencil operations, onto different hardware units, including Tensor Cores.

The optimization of stencil computations has been extensively studied on both CPUs~\cite{10.1145/3458817.3476154, yuansc17} and GPUs~\cite{8451874, 6114468, 10.1145/2491956.2462176}. 

On CPUs, vectorization~\cite{10.1145/3458817.3476154, 10.1145/2464996.2467268, 10.1007/978-3-642-19861-8_13, 10579118}, data reuse strategies~\cite{8665800, 10.1145/2666356.2594342, zhaosc19}, and tiling techniques, such as diamond~\cite{10.1109/SC.2012.107, 10.1145/1375581.1375595}, time skewing~\cite{1592833, 10.1023/A:1015460304860}, rectangular~\cite{1592745}, and tessellating tiling~\cite{yuansc17}, are key methods for improving performance. 

On GPUs, spatial tiling~\cite{7034720, zhaosc19, maruyama2014optimizing}, temporal tiling~\cite{Holewinski12ics, 10.1145/1542275.1542313, 10.1145/2830018.2830025, 10.1145/2400682.2400713, 7582549, 10.1145/2458523.2458526}, unrolling~\cite{10.1145/3469030}, prefetching~\cite{8820786}, and streaming~\cite{8451874} techniques effectively leverage GPU parallelism and memory hierarchies. Frameworks like Brick~\cite{zhaosc19, 10.1145/3437801.3441598, 8639931} exploit fine-grained data reuse, while DRStencil~\cite{you2021drstencil} applies fusion-partition optimization for stencil acceleration. Recent research also explores Tensor Cores, with TCStencil~\cite{liu2022toward} applying them directly, ConvStencil~\cite{10.1145/3627535.3638476} bridging convolution and stencil computations, FlashFFT~\cite{10.1145/3710848.3710897} restructures FFT-based stencil computations on TCU and LoraStencil~\cite{SC41406.2024.00059} reduces redundancy through low-rank decomposition of stencil. Furthermore, libraries like cuDNN~\cite{cudnn, chetlur2014cudnn} optimize stencil-related operations, and AMOS~\cite{zheng2022amos} maps depth-wise convolutions, equivalent to stencil operations, to hardware units, including Tensor Cores.


\section{Conclusion}

This paper presents SparStencil, a novel system leveraging sparse Tensor Cores for stencil computations via Permutation Invariant Transformation. It integrates Adaptive Layout Morphing, Structured Sparsity Conversion, and Automatic Kernel Generation to optimize stencil computations on sparse TCUs. Evaluations demonstrate its effectiveness, outperforming state-of-the-art approaches, with potential benefits for scientific and engineering applications.

