\title{FACT: the Features At Convergence Theorem for neural networks}

\begin{document}
\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
A central challenge in deep learning theory is to understand how neural networks learn and represent features. To this end, we prove the \textit{Features at Convergence Theorem} (FACT), which gives a self-consistency equation that neural network weights satisfy at convergence when trained with nonzero weight decay.
For each weight matrix $W$, this equation relates the ``feature matrix'' $W^\top W$ to the set of input vectors passed into the matrix during forward propagation and the loss gradients passed through it during backpropagation.
We validate this relation empirically, showing that neural features indeed satisfy the FACT at convergence. 
Furthermore, by modifying the ``Recursive Feature Machines'' of Radhakrishnan et al.  \cite{radhakrishnan2024mechanism} so that they obey the FACT, we arrive at a new learning algorithm, \textit{FACT-RFM}.
FACT-RFM achieves high performance on tabular data and captures various feature learning behaviors that occur in neural network training, including grokking in modular arithmetic and phase transitions in learning sparse parities.
\end{abstract}


\section{Introduction}

A central aim of deep learning theory is to understand how neural networks learn and represent features. A recently prevalent approach to this question has been to study neural network training dynamics – tracking weight evolution to understand how features emerge \cite{olsson2022context,edelman2024evolution,nichani2024transformers,cabannes2023scaling,cabannes2024learning,arous2021online,abbe2022merged,abbe2023sgd,kumar2023grokking}. While insightful, these analyses are technically challenging and are typically limited to synthetic  datasets. %

In this paper, we pursue an alternative approach: we seek conditions on network weights that are satisfied {\it at the conclusion of training}, to gain insight into how the trained network represents the learned function. Our primary theoretical result is the \textit{Features at Convergence Theorem} (FACT), which is a self-consistency formula that neural networks at convergence must satisfy. By focusing on the network state at convergence, we can circumvent many of the difficulties associated with analyzing training dynamics, and the resulting insights are directly applicable to real-world data beyond simplified synthetic settings. 

We consider the standard setting of training a model $f$ with a loss function $\ell(\cdot,\cdot)$ on data points $(x_1,y_1)\ldots,(x_n,y_n)$. We assume, and that is the only assumption we require, that this model has an internal weight matrix $W$ so that it is of the form $f(x) = g(Wh(x), x)$, where $f$ depends on $W$ only through $W h(x)$ and $h(x)$ is a vector dependent on the input (and other parameters of the model) but independent of $W$. This is a mild condition, since the weight $W$ can be the weight matrix in any internal layer of a neural network, and there may even be skip connections. The FACT states that, as long as the network is trained with nonzero weight decay, the weights at convergence must satisfy
\begin{align}\label{eq:inf-nfl}
W^{\top}W \propto \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n \nabla \ell(f(x_i); y_i) x_i^{\top}\,, \tag{FACT}
\end{align}
where $\nabla \ell(f(x_i); y_i)$ denotes the gradient with respect to the input of the layer $W$. %
Our main contribution is to demonstrate that this easily derived identity captures important aspects of feature learning in neural networks, since the left-hand side $W^{\top} W$ is the so-called Neural Features Matrix (NFM), which governs how features are filtered from one layer to the next. We structure the contributions of our paper as follows.






























\paragraph{\textbullet~ The FACT achieves alignment with neural features.} 
First, we derive \eqref{eq:fact} in Theorem~\ref{thm:fact}, with a simple derivation that takes only a few lines. We empirically validate that \eqref{eq:fact} is satisfied in trained deep fully-connected networks on real-world image datasets. As part of our analysis, we compare the FACT predictions to a leading empirically-driven alternative proposed in prior work: the \textit{Neural Feature Ansatz} (NFA) \cite{radhakrishnan2024mechanism}. Furthermore, we demonstrate theoretically and experimentally that in certain synthetic settings the NFA predictions can be uncorrelated to the ground truth, while FACT still holds. See Table~\ref{tab:leader-table} and Section~\ref{sec:separation}.
\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrr@{}} %
  \multicolumn{3}{c}{\textit{Correlation with $W^{\top} W$ after training}} \\
  \toprule %
          & MNIST     & CIFAR-10 \\
  \midrule %
  FACT (ours) & \textbf{0.999} & \textbf{0.896} \\
  NFA \cite{radhakrishnan2024mechanism} & 0.909 & 0.722 \\
  eNFA \cite{ziyin2025formationrepresentationsneuralnetworks} & 0.903 & 0.714 \\
  \bottomrule %
\end{tabular}
\caption{FACT is satisfied by learned neural features in ReLU networks trained on MNIST and CIFAR-10; see Section~\ref{sec:approx-real-world} for more results.}\label{tab:leader-table}
\end{table}















\paragraph{\textbullet~ The FACT enables high-performing learning on tabular data.} Having validated the FACT, we turn to using it in applications. We show that the FACT identity can be used to power an algorithm analogous to Recursive Feature Machines (RFM), a state-of-the art algorithm on tabular datasets \cite{radhakrishnan2024mechanism}. We obtain high performance on numerous tabular datasets and reproduce phenomena that had been previously observed with NFA-based RFM and that mirror how neural networks learn: 
(i) low-rank sparse parity learning, and (ii)~grokking of arithmetic. See Figure~\ref{fig:leader-parities} and Section~\ref{sec:wagop-rfm}.

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \begin{tabular}{c}
    \textit{\small Learning sparse parities} \\
    \includegraphics[width=0.5\linewidth]{figs/teaser_fig_sparse_parity1_redo.pdf}
    \end{tabular}

    \caption{When FACT is used to power the Recursive Feature Machines (RFM) algorithm on the task of learning sparse parities (the data are sampled uniformly from  $\{0,1\}^{50}$  and $y = x_7\oplus x_{15} \oplus x_{25} \oplus x_{29}$) it reproduces phase transitions found in learning with neural networks, and features learn the support of the sparse parity; details in Section~\ref{sec:factrfm-reconstructs-behaviors}.}\label{fig:leader-parities}
    \end{figure}





\paragraph{\textbullet~ The FACT gives insight into the NFA.} Finally, we observe that the  RFM algorithm of \cite{radhakrishnan2024mechanism} based on NFA can be viewed as implementing an approximate version of the FACT-RFM algorithm. Indeed, we empirically find that the features learned by both algorithms are highly correlated in most settings (apart from the synthetic adversarial settings that we construct in Section~\ref{sec:approx-two-layer} where they differ). Thus, FACT sheds light on the mechanism by which the NFA of \cite{radhakrishnan2024mechanism} mimics feature learning in neural networks. See Section~\ref{sec:nfa-explanation}.




























\subsection{Related literature}




The closest prior work is on the Neural Feature Ansatz (NFA) \cite{radhakrishnan2024mechanism}, which is a conjecture for the mechanism of feature learning in neural networks; see Section~\ref{sec:preliminaries} for a definition. The NFA was used in \cite{radhakrishnan2024mechanism} to power a kernel learning algorithm called RFM, which has been used to achieve state-of-the-art performance in monitoring activations of models \cite{beaglehole2025aggregate}, as well as for new low-rank matrix learning algorithms \cite{radhakrishnan2025linear}. 
Additionally RFM has been shown to recover intriguing phenomena related to neural networks and feature learning, including grokking modular arithmetic \cite{mallinar2024emergence} and neural collapse \cite{beaglehole2025collapse}.

However, the NFA does not have a theoretical derivation for why it should hold at convergence. By providing the closely-related FACT quantity and a theoretical derivation for it, our results thus connect the literature on the NFA to a separate literature analyzing the KKT conditions that must be satisfied by networks at stationarity. This literature -- along with results on KKT conditions that arise with exponentially-tailed losses at large training times 
\cite{soudry2018implicit,ji2019implicit,lyu2019gradient,ji2020directional} -- has been used to show implicit bias of deep linear architectures towards low-rank \cite{gunasekar2017implicit,arora2019implicit}, of diagonal networks towards sparsity \cite{woodworth2020kernel}, of convolutional networks towards Fourier-sparsity \cite{gunasekar2018implicit}, of fully-connected networks towards algebraic structure when learning modular arithmetic \cite{mohamadi2023grokking,morwani2023feature}, among other implications such as to linear regression with bagging \cite{stewart2023regression} and understanding adversarial examples \cite{frei2024double}.


Another alternative to the NFA, called the ``equivariant NFA'' (eNFA), was recently proposed in  \cite{ziyin2025formationrepresentationsneuralnetworks} based on an analysis of the dynamics of noisy SGD. This is distinct from the FACT and we also compare to it in Section~\ref{sec:separation}.

