In order to expose the symmetries underlying this specialisation of (\ref{ham}) set 
 \begin{align*}
e_1&=\sqrt{2}(b_1^\dagger b_2+b_2^\dagger b_3), & h_1&= 2(N_1-N_3) ,& f_1&= \sqrt{2}(b_2^\dagger b_1+b_3^\dagger b_2),  \\
e_2&=\frac{1}{2}b_2^\dagger b_2^\dagger -b_1^\dagger b_3^\dagger,  & h_2&= \frac{1}{2}(2N+3I)  , & f_2&= b_1b_3-\frac{1}{2}b_2b_2 .
\end{align*}
These operators realise the $o(4)$ Lie algebra by satisfying the commutation relations
\begin{align*}
[e_j,\,f_k]&=\delta_{jk}h_j,  &
[h_j,\,e_k]&=2\delta_{jk}e_k, &
[h_j,\,f_k]&=-2\delta_{jk}f_k.
\end{align*}
It is found that the Hamiltonian (\ref{ham}) subject to the constraints 
(\ref{int1}), (\ref{int2}),  (\ref{int4}) is expressible as 
\begin{align}
H &= H_1+H_2, \label{hamint}   \\
H_1&=\frac{U}{4}h_1^2      -\frac{\mu}{2} h_1    +\frac{{\mathcal E}}{\sqrt{2}}(e_1+f_1), \nonumber \\
H_2&=U\left(h_2-\frac{3}{2}I\right)^ 2 . \nonumber 
\end{align}
%
Under this realisation, the corresponding $o(4)$ Casimir invariants assume the form
\begin{align*}
C_1&=\frac{1}{2}h_1^2+e_1f_1+f_1e_1    \\
&= 2N^2 +2N -8N_1N_3-2N_2^2 +2N_2 + 4b_2^\dagger b_2^\dagger b_1b_3+4b_1^\dagger b_3^\dagger b_2 b_2    ,\\
C_2&=\frac{1}{2} h_2^2+e_2f_2+f_2e_2  \\
%&=\frac{1}{2}N^2 +\frac{1}{2}N-\frac{3}{8}I- 2N_1N_3  -\frac{1}{2} N_2^2 +\frac{1}{2}N_2 +b^\dagger_1 b_3^\dagger b_2b_2 + b_2^\dagger b_2^\dagger b_1 b_3  \\
&= \frac{1}{4}C_1 -\frac{3}{8}I.  
\end{align*}
This realisation of the Casimir invariants provides the third independent conserved operator required to claim integrability of ({\ref{hamint}).


\section{Bethe Ansatz solution}       

To derive the Bethe Ansatz solution of the model, differential operator methods are adapted from \cite{lh06}. 
Before proceeding to that task, it is first necessary to completely describe the $o(4)$ action on the module provided by the Fock space.

With respect to the action of $o_1(3)={\rm span}\{e_1,\,h_1,\,f_1\}$, the Fock space decomposes into a direct sum of irreducible modules with lowest weight vectors  $|0,0,m\rangle$ such that 
\begin{align*}
f_1|0,0,n\rangle  &=0, \\
h_1|0,0,n \rangle  &= -2n |0,0,n \rangle, \\
C_1|0,0,n \rangle  &= 2n(n+1) |0,0,n \rangle. 
\end{align*}
Such vectors are simultaneously lowest-weight vectors with respect to the action of $o_2(3)={\rm span}\{e_2,\,h_2,\,f_2\}$, satisfying
\begin{align*}
f_2|0,0,n\rangle  &=0, \\
h_2|0,0,n\rangle  &= \frac{1}{2}(2n+3) |0,0,n\rangle, \\
C_2|0,0,n\rangle  &= \frac{1}{2}\left(n(n+1)-\frac{3}{4}\right) |0,0,n\rangle. 
\end{align*}  
Now set, for $({\mathcal N}-n)/2 \in {\mathbb Z}_{\geq 0}$, the recursive definition 
\begin{align*}
|n, 0, n\}&=|0,0,n\rangle  , \\
|{\mathcal N}+2, 0, n\} &= \frac{-2}{{\mathcal N}+n+3}e_2|{\mathcal N}, 0, n\}.     
\end{align*}
The following hold
\begin{align*}
f_1  |{\mathcal N}, 0, n\}  &=0, \\
h_1  |{\mathcal N}, 0, n\} &= -2n |{\mathcal N}, 0, n\}   , \\
C_1  |{\mathcal N}, 0, n\} &= 2n(n+1) |{\mathcal N}, 0, n\}, \\
%f_2  |{\mathcal N}, 0, n\} &=?? |{\mathcal N}-2, 0, n\}, \\
h_2  |{\mathcal N}, 0, n\} &= \frac{1}{2}(2{\mathcal N}+3) |{\mathcal N}, 0, n\}, \\
C_2  |{\mathcal N}, 0, n\} &= \frac{1}{2}\left(n(n+1)-\frac{3}{4}\right) |{\mathcal N}, 0, n\}. 
\end{align*} 
Similarly recursively define, for $m=0,\dots, 2n-1$,
\begin{align*}
|{\mathcal N}, m+1, n\} &= \frac{1}{2n-m}e_1 |{\mathcal N}, m, n\}.
\end{align*}
Then the action of the $o(4)$ algebra on this set of states is given by (recall $({\mathcal N}-n)/2 \in {\mathbb Z}_{\geq 0}$ ) 
\begin{align*}
f_1  |{\mathcal N}, m, n\}  &=m |{\mathcal N}, m-1, n\},\\
h_1  |{\mathcal N}, m, n\} &= 2(m-n) |{\mathcal N}, m, n\}   , \\
e_1  |{\mathcal N}, m, n\} &=  (2n-m)|{\mathcal N}, m+1, n\}   , \\
C_1  |{\mathcal N}, m, n\} &= 2n(n+1) |{\mathcal N}, m, n\}, \\
f_2  |{\mathcal N}, m, n\} &= 
\frac{{\mathcal N}-n} {2} |{\mathcal N}-2, m, n\} ,  \\
h_2  |{\mathcal N}, m, n\} &= \frac{1}{2}(2{\mathcal N}+3) |{\mathcal N}, m, n\}, \\
e_2  |{\mathcal N}, m, n\} &= -\frac{{\mathcal N}+n+3}{2} |{\mathcal N}+2, m, n\} ,   \\
C_2  |{\mathcal N}, m, n\} &= \frac{1}{2}\left(n(n+1)-\frac{3}{4}\right) 
|{\mathcal N}, m, n\}. 
\end{align*} 
Since the eigenvalues with respect to $h_1,\,h_2,\,C_1$ uniquely identify $|{\mathcal N}, m, n\}$, it follows that the set of such vectors is linearly independent. That they span ${\mathcal F}$ follows by a counting argument.  Each vector $|{\mathcal N},0,n\}$ is a lowest-weight vector of weight $-2n$, so it generates an irreducible $o_1(3)$-model $V_n$ of dimension ${\rm dim}(V_n)=2n+1$. Now if ${\mathcal N}$ is even, then $n$ is even, and 
\begin{align*}
\sum_{n\in 2{\mathbb Z}_{\geq 0}}^{\mathcal N} {\rm dim}(V_n)=
\sum_{k=0}^{{\mathcal N}/2} {\rm dim}(V_{2k})=\sum_{k=0}^{{\mathcal N}/2} (4k+1)= 
 \frac{({\mathcal N}+1)({\mathcal N}+2)}{2}={\rm dim}({\mathcal F}_{\mathcal N}).
\end{align*}
Otherwise, if ${\mathcal N}$ is odd such that $p=n-1$ is even,
\begin{align*}
\sum_{p\in 2{\mathbb Z}_{\geq 0}}^{{\mathcal N}-1} {\rm dim}(V_{p+1})=
\sum_{k=0}^{({\mathcal N}-1) /2} {\rm dim}(V_{2k+1})=\sum_{k=0}^{({\mathcal N}-1)/2} (4k+3)= 
 \frac{({\mathcal N}+1)({\mathcal N}+2)}{2}={\rm dim}({\mathcal F}_{\mathcal N}).
\end{align*}
Having established that the vectors $| {\mathcal N}, m, n\}  $ provide a basis for ${\mathcal F}$,
define the ${\mathcal N}$-particle states 
\begin{align}
|\Psi(u_1,\dots,u_{2n};{\mathcal N})\rangle&=\prod_{j=1}^{2n}(e_1-u_jI) |{\mathcal N}, 0, n\} ,
\label{states}\\
|\Psi_k(u_1,\dots,u_{2n};{\mathcal N})\rangle&=\prod_{j\neq k}^{2n}(e_1-u_jI) |{\mathcal N}, 0, n\}.  
\nonumber
\end{align}
It is found that
\begin{align}
e_1|\Psi(u_1,\dots,u_{2n};{\mathcal N})\rangle 
&=-\sum_{k=1}^{2n} u_k |\Psi(u_1,\dots,u_{2n};{\mathcal N})\rangle  
-\sum_{k=1}^{2n} u^2_k |\Psi_k(u_1,\dots,u_{2n};{\mathcal N})\rangle , 
\label{eact}\\
h_1|\Psi(u_1,\dots,u_{2n};{\mathcal N})\rangle
&=2n  |\Psi(u_1,\dots,u_{2n};{\mathcal N})\rangle +2\sum_{k=1}^{2n} u_k |\Psi_k(u_1,\dots,u_{2n};{\mathcal N})\rangle  ,
\label{hact}\\  
h_1^2 |\Psi(u_1,\dots,u_{2n};{\mathcal N})\rangle&= 4n^2  |\Psi(u_1,\dots,u_{2n};{\mathcal N})\rangle+8\sum_{k=1}^{2n} \sum_{l\neq k}^{2n}  \frac{u^2_k}{u_k-u_l} |\Psi_{k}(u_1,\dots,u_{2n};{\mathcal N})\rangle  \nonumber \\
&\qquad -4(2n-1)\sum_{k=1}^{2n} u_k  |\Psi_{k}(u_1,\dots,u_{2n};{\mathcal N})\rangle  
   , \label{h2act} \\   
f_1 |\Psi(u_1,\dots,u_{2n};{\mathcal N})\rangle &=\sum_{k=1}^n |\Psi_k(u_1,\dots,u_{2n};{\mathcal N})\rangle .     
\label{fact}\end{align}
As the calculations leading to the above formulae are somewhat technical, the details have been placed in the Appendix.  

The above show that the action of (\ref{hamint}) on (\ref{states}) is evaluated as
\begin{align*}
H |\Psi(u_1,&\dots,u_n;{\mathcal N})\rangle= U{\mathcal N} |\Psi(u_1,\dots,u_{2n};{\mathcal N})\rangle \\
&+
\frac{U}{4}\left(4n^2  |\Psi(u_1,\dots,u_{2n};{\mathcal N})\rangle + 8\sum_{k=1}^{2n} \sum_{l\neq k}^{2n}   \frac{u^2_k}{u_k-u_l} |\Psi_{k}(u_1,\dots,u_{2n};{\mathcal N})\rangle 
\right.\nonumber \\
&\qquad\qquad \qquad -\left.  
4(2n-1)\sum_{k=1}^{2n} u_k  |\Psi_{k}(u_1,\dots,u_{2n};{\mathcal N})\rangle     
	\right)    \\
% 
& -\frac{\mu}{2} \left(2n  |\Psi(u_1,\dots,u_{2n};{\mathcal N})\rangle +2\sum_{k=1}^{2n} u_k |\Psi_k(u_1,\dots,u_{2n};{\mathcal N})\rangle 
\right)  \\
%
& +\frac{{\mathcal E}}{\sqrt{2}}\left( \sum_{k=1}^{2n} u_k |\Psi(u_1,\dots,u_{2n};{\mathcal N})\rangle  
+\sum_{k=1}^{2n} u^2_k |\Psi_k(u_1,\dots,u_{2n};{\mathcal N})\rangle \right. \\
&\qquad\qquad\qquad \left.  -\sum_{k=1}^n |\Psi_k(u_1,\dots,u_{2n};{\mathcal N})\rangle   \right). 
\end{align*}
The unwanted terms $ |\Psi_{k}(u_1,\dots,u_{2n};{\mathcal N})\rangle $ cancel when the Bethe Anatz equations 
\begin{align}
2U\sum_{l\neq k}^{2n}    \frac{u^2_k}{u_k-u_l} &= U(2n-1)u_k+\frac{\mathcal E}{\sqrt{2}} (1-u_k^2)-\mu u_k , &
&k=1,\dots,2n,
\label{bae} 
\end{align}
hold, rendering $|\Psi(u_1,\dots,u_{2n};{\mathcal N}\rangle$ an eigenstate with energy eigenvalue
\begin{align}
E= U({\mathcal N}^2+n^2)-2\mu n +\frac{\mathcal E}{\sqrt{2}} \sum_{k=1}^{2n} u_k.
\label{nrg}
\end{align}


\section{Conclusion}

This work reports the construction of an integrable 3-site, extended, Bose-Hubbard model, providing a counterpoint to the integrable model studied in
\cite{ccrsh21,cwcrfh24,tywfl20,wytlf18,wcfs22,wcrfh25,wyblf23}.
Two distinguishing features of the Hamiltonian 
(\ref{hamint}) are that all interactions are on-site or nearest neighbour, and that the Hamiltonian remains integrable in the presence of a tilting potential with coupling $\mu$. A by-product of the integrability is that the model admits a Bethe Ansatz solution. 

The Bethe Ansatz solution presented above through Eqs. (\ref{states}), (\ref{bae}), and (\ref{nrg}) is complete. All eigenstates of the system can be cast into the form (\ref{states}). Potentially, the solution may fail if there is degeneration of the set of Bethe roots $\{u_1,\dots,u_{2n}\}$, 
corresponding to a singularity in (\ref{bae}). However, this possibility may be excluded by adapting the methods described in \cite{lz09}. Mapping the spectrum of (\ref{hamint}) to the quasi-exactly solvable sector of a 
one-dimensional Schr\"odinger problem, and invoking the oscillation theorem, establishes that degeneration does not happen.  
Moreover, spurious solutions of (\ref{bae}) cannot occur. Spurious solutions of Bethe Ansatz equations arise when the evaluation of the Bethe state through the Bethe roots yields a null state. While this is a feature of some spin \cite{a92,av86,gd14,ks14,nw14}, fermionic \cite{lmz13}, and anyonic \cite{cdil10} systems, spurious solutions do not arise here. Since boson creation operators do not admit a non-trivial kernel, the general form 
(\ref{states}) cannot vanish for any choice of $\{u_1,\dots,u_{2n}\}$.

For future work, one avenue is to investigate the quantum dynamics of the system utilising the Bethe Ansatz solution and undertaking a comparison with the results of \cite{ccrsh21,cwcrfh24,tywfl20,wytlf18,wcfs22,wcrfh25,wyblf23}. A particular focus will be to characterise the dynamics in the so-called   
resonant tunnelling regime, where there are oscillations that are approximately harmonic. It would be of interest to derive analytic formulae for the amplitude and frequency of these oscillations, to understand their dependency on the tilting parameter $\mu$. 

Finally, an important consequence of the formulation above is that it facilitates extension to systems with more degrees of freedom. In particular, a 4-site system is accommodated through a realisation of $o(3)\oplus o(3) \oplus o(3)$, providing a counterpoint to the integrable model studied in \cite{bil23,gwylf22,gywtfl22} for interferometric applications. A study of this 4-site system, obtained by extending the methods developed above, will be communicated in a forthcoming publication.   

