\section{Noncommutative case} \label{sec:NC} 
%\textcolor{blue}{split the theoretical part from the computational part, and move the last one in a new section after odd}

We begin in~\S\ref{FuRingsNC} by presenting several constraints on the isomorphism classes of noncommutative complexified fusion rings, arising from Galois-theoretic considerations. In particular, we establish that rank~$6$ is the minimal possible rank for a noncommutative fusion ring. 

In~\S\ref{DrRingsNC}, we turn to Drinfeld rings and show—using Egyptian fraction techniques—that the group ring~$\mathbb{Z}S_3$ is the unique noncommutative integral Drinfeld ring of rank~$6$, and consequently the only integral Grothendieck ring of that rank. We further extend the classification to all noncommutative integral Drinfeld rings of rank up to~$8$, and all integral Grothendieck rings of rank up to~$7$.

Finally, in~\S\ref{GrRingsNC}, leveraging results involving Frobenius--Schur indicators, we prove that any noncommutative, odd-dimensional integral Grothendieck ring must have rank at least~$21$. At this minimal rank, the sole example is the pointed fusion ring corresponding to the group~$C_7 \rtimes C_3$. We conclude with a discussion about the rank~$23$.

