{Constrained quantization for discrete distributions}

\begin{abstract}
Constrained quantization for a Borel probability measure refers to the process of approximating a given probability distribution by a discrete probability measure supported on a finite number of points that lie within a specified set, known as the \emph{constraint}. 
This paper investigates constrained quantization for several types of discrete probability distributions under two different constraints. The study begins with an analysis of two discrete distributions, one uniform and one nonuniform,  defined over a finite support. For each distribution, the constrained optimal sets of representative points and the corresponding constrained quantization errors are computed under both constraints.
The analysis is then extended to an infinite discrete distribution supported on the reciprocals of the natural numbers. For this infinite case, and under two different constraints, the paper determines all possible constrained optimal sets of $n$-points and the associated $n$th constrained quantization errors for all $1 \leq n \leq 2000$. The problem of constrained quantization for $n > 2000$ in this infinite setting remains open.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
 Quantization refers to the transformation of a continuous analog signal into a digital signal with $ k $ discrete levels, or the reduction of a digital signal with $ n $ levels to one with $ k $ levels, where $ k < n $. This process is fundamental for representing, processing, storing, and transmitting analog information in digital systems, as well as for achieving data compression. It remains a central and actively studied topic in source coding and information theory, with widespread applications across engineering and technology—particularly in data compression and clustering. For foundational work, see \cite{DFG, GG, GL1, GN, P, Z1, Z2}.
More recently, the scope of quantization theory has expanded with the development of constrained and conditional quantization (see \cite{PR1, PR2, PR3}). This has led to a classification of the theory into two principal areas: constrained and unconstrained quantization. For a concise overview and practical entry point into both branches, see \cite{PR1}. For a deeper exploration of unconstrained quantization, refer to \cite{GL2}. The present paper focuses specifically on constrained quantization.


Let $ P $ be a Borel probability measure on $ \mathbb{R}^2 $ equipped with the Euclidean metric $ d $ induced by a norm $ \|\cdot\| $. Let $ S $ be a nonempty closed subset of $ \mathbb{R}^2 $, and let $ \ga \subseteq S $ be a locally finite subset of $ \mathbb{R}^2 $; that is, the intersection of $ \ga $ with any bounded subset of $ \mathbb{R}^2 $ is finite. This implies that $ \ga $ is countable and closed.
The distortion error for the measure $ P $, with respect to the set $ \ga \subseteq S $, is defined by
\[
V(P; \ga ) = \int \min_{a \in \ga } \|x - a\|^2 \, dP(x).
\]
Then, for $ n \in \mathbb{N} $, the \textit{$ n $th constrained quantization error} for $ P $ with respect to the set $ S $ is defined by
\begin{equation} \label{Vr}
V_n := V_n(P) = \inf \left\{ V(P; \ga ) : \ga \subseteq S,\ 1 \leq \operatorname{card}(\ga ) \leq n \right\},
\end{equation}
where $ \operatorname{card}(A) $ denotes the cardinality of a set $ A $.
Let $ U $ be the largest open subset of $ \mathbb{R}^2 $ such that $ P(U) = 0 $. Then, the set $ \mathbb{R}^2 \setminus U $ is called the \textit{support of $ P $}, denoted by $ \operatorname{supp}(P) $.
For a locally finite set $ \ga \subseteq \mathbb{R}^2 $ and a point $ a \in \ga $, define
\[
M(a | \ga ) = \left\{ x \in \mathbb{R}^2 : d(x, a) = \min_{b \in \ga } d(x, b) \right\}.
\]
The set $ M(a | \ga ) $ is called the \textit{Voronoi region} generated by $ a \in \ga $. The collection
\[
\left\{ M(a | \ga ) : a \in \ga \right\}
\]
is called the \textit{Voronoi diagram} (or \textit{Voronoi tessellation}) of $ \mathbb{R}^2 $ with respect to $ \ga $.
A set $ \ga $ for which the infimum in \eqref{Vr} is attained and $ \operatorname{card}(\ga ) \leq n $ is called a \textit{constrained optimal set of $ n $-points} for $ P $ with respect to the constraint $ S $.
If the support of $ P $ contains at least $ N $ elements, then for any $ n \leq N $, an optimal set of $ n $-points contains exactly $ n $ elements (see \cite{PR1}). If $ S = \mathbb{R}^2 $, i.e., there is no constraint, then the notion of constrained quantization error reduces to the classical definition of unconstrained quantization error, often simply referred to as the quantization error (see \cite{GL2}). In the unconstrained setting, if the support of $ P $ contains at least $ n $ elements, then each element in an optimal set of $ n $-points corresponds to the conditional expectation over its own Voronoi region. Due to this property, an optimal set of $ n $-points in unconstrained quantization is also referred to as an optimal set of $ n $-means.


\subsection{Delineation}  
In this paper, we study constrained quantization for both finite and infinite discrete distributions. Section~\ref{sec0} provides the necessary preliminaries. We begin by considering two specific geometric constraints: one defined by a semicircular arc with base on the line segment $[-3, 3]$, and the other formed by the union of two sides of an equilateral triangle with the same base. Under these constraints, we determine the constrained optimal sets of $n$-points and the corresponding $n$th constrained quantization errors for all possible $n \in \mathbb{N}$.
Section~\ref{sec1} is devoted to the case of a uniform discrete distribution supported on the set $\{-3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3\}$, and is divided into two subsections corresponding to the two constraints. Section~\ref{sec2} mirrors this structure but addresses a nonuniform discrete distribution supported on the same set.
In Section~\ref{sec3}, we consider an infinite discrete distribution supported on the set $\left\{\frac{1}{n} : n \in \mathbb{N} \right\}$. The first subsection deals with the semicircular arc with base $[0, 1]$ as the constraint, for which we compute the constrained optimal sets $n$-points and the corresponding $n$th constrained quantization errors for all possible $n \in \mathbb{N}$. In the second subsection, using the constraint formed by two sides of an equilateral triangle with base $[0, 1]$, we perform the same computations for $n$ ranging from $1$ to $2000$. An open problem is also mentioned at the end of this subsection. 


\section{Preliminaries} \label{sec0} 
For any two elements $(a, b)$ and $(c, d)$ in $\mathbb{R}^2$, the squared Euclidean distance is given by
\[
\rho((a, b), (c, d)) := (a - c)^2 + (b - d)^2.
\]
Two elements $p, q \in \alpha$ are called \emph{adjacent} if their Voronoi regions share a common boundary. Let $e$ be a point on the boundary between the Voronoi regions of $p$ and $q$. Since this boundary is the perpendicular bisector of the line segment connecting $p$ and $q$, it satisfies the \emph{canonical equation}
\[
\rho(p, e) - \rho(q, e) = 0.
\]
Notice that any real number $x \in \mathbb{R}$ can be identified with the element $(x, 0) \in \mathbb{R}^2$. Using this identification, we define a function
\[
\rho : \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}^2 \to [0, \infty) \te{ such that } \rho(x, (a, b)) = (x - a)^2 + b^2,
\]
which represents the squared Euclidean distance between $x \in \mathbb{R}$ and $(a, b) \in \mathbb{R}^2$.
 \begin{remark} \label{rem1} 
 Let $ \ga_n$ be an optimal set of $ n $-points for a Borel probability measure $ P $. If a point in the support of $ P $ lies on the common boundary of two Voronoi regions generated by elements of $ \ga_n$, then the point is assigned to one of the Voronoi regions.
 \end{remark} 

 \begin{remark} \label{rem2} 
For $n\in\D N$, let $\ga_n$ denote an optimal set of $n$-points for a Borel probability measure $P$ with respect to a constraint $S$ in $\D R^2$. Then, for $\ga=\{a_1,a_2,\ldots\}\subseteq S$, let us define the sets $A_{a_i|\alpha}$ for $a_i\in \ga$ as follows:
	\begin{equation}\label{Megha1000}
		\begin{aligned}
			A_{a_{i}|\alpha}=\left\{\begin{array} {ll}
				M(a_1|\ga) & \te{ if } i=1,\\
				M(a_i|\ga)\setminus \UU_{k<i} M(a_k|\ga) & \te{ if } i\geq 2,
			\end{array}\right.
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation}
where $ M(a_i|\ga ) $ is the \textit{Voronoi region} generated by $ a_i \in \ga $.  
	The set $\set{A_{a_{i}|\alpha} : a_i\in \ga}$ is called the \tit{Voronoi partition} of $\D R^k$ with respect to the set $\ga$ (and $S$).  Then, for some positive integer $n$, an optimal set $\ga_n$  does not exist, by that it is meant that there is no $\ga\subseteq S$ containing at least $n$ elements such that 
	\[P(A_{a|\alpha})>0 \te{ for each } a \in \ga,\]  
and vice versa. 			
 \end{remark} 


 \begin{remark} \label{rem3} 
In view of Remark~\ref{rem2}, for some positive integer $n$ if a constrained optimal set $\ga_n$ does not exist, then $\ga_{k}$  does not exist for any positive integer $k\geq n$. 
 \end{remark} 
\begin{note} \label{note1} 
Let $P$ be a finite discrete distribution with support $\set{s_1, s_2, \cdots, s_N}$ for some positive integer $N\geq 2$ associated with a probability vector $(p_1, p_2, \cdots, p_N)$, i.e., the probability mass function $f$ for $P$ is given by 
\[f(s_j)=P(X : X=s_j)=\left\{\begin{array}{cc}
 p_j,  & \te{ where } j\in \set{1, 2, 3, \cdots, N},   \\
 0 & \te{otherwise}.
 \end{array}
\right.\]
Let $a, b\in \D R$ be such that $a\leq s_1<s_2<\cdots<s_N\leq b$. Let us now consider an equilateral triangle $ABC$, whose base $AB$ is the segment $[a, b]$, i.e., the coordinates of $A$ and $B$ are, respectively, given by $(a, 0)$ and $(b, 0)$. Let $S:=S_1\uu S_2$, where $S_1$ and $S_2$, respectively, represent the two sides $AC$ and $BC$. Let the equations of the lines $AC$ and $BC$ are, respectively, given by $y=m_1x+c_1$ and $y=m_2x+c_2$. Let $\ga_n$ be a constrained optimal set of $n$-points for $P$ with respect to the constraint $S$ for some positive integer $n$. Let $\te{card}(\ga_n\ii S_1)=\ell$ and $\te{card}((\ga_n\ii S_2)\setminus (\ga_n\ii S_1))=m$. Then, we have $\ell+m=n$. Then, there exists a set of $n$ distinct real number $a_j$ for $1\leq j\leq n$ with $a_1<a_2<\cdots <a_n$ such that 
\begin{align*} 
\ga_n\ii S_1=\set{(a_j,  m_1 a_j+c_1) : 1\leq j\leq \ell} \te{ and } \ga_n\ii S_2=\set{(a_j,  m_2 a_j+c_2) : 1\leq j\leq m}
\end{align*}
If an optimal set $\ga_n$ exists, then by Remark~\ref{rem2}, the Voronoi region of each element in $\ga_n$ must contain at least an element from the support of $P$.
Let 
\begin{align*}\te{card}(M((a_j, m_1 a_j+c_1)|\ga_n)) &=n_j \te{ for } 1\leq j\leq \ell, \te{ and } \\
\te{card}(M((a_j, m_2 a_j+c_2)|\ga_n) \setminus  M(&(a_j, m_1 a_j+c_1) |\ga_n)) =n_{\ell+j} \te{ for } 1\leq j\leq  m.
\end{align*} 
 Hence, we can assume that with each $\ga_n$ there exists a vector $(n_1, n_2, \cdots, n_{\ell}, n_{\ell+1}, n_{\ell+2}, \cdots, n_{\ell+m})$ associated with $\ga_n$ such that 
\[n_1+n_2+\cdots +n_{\ell+m}=N.\] 
The vector $(n_1, n_2, \cdots, n_{\ell}, n_{\ell+1}, n_{\ell+2}, \cdots, n_{\ell+m})$ associated with an optimal set of $n$-points will be referred to as a \tit{canonical vector}. 
If $V_n$ is the corresponding $n$th constrained quantization error, then 
\begin{equation} \label{eq890} 
 \begin{aligned} V_n& :=V_{n_1, n_2, \cdots, n_{\ell+m}}(P; \ga)=\sum_{j=1}^{n_1}p_j \rho(s_j, (a_1, m_1a_1+c_1))+\sum_{j=n_1+1}^{n_1+n_2} p_j \rho(s_j, (a_2, m_1a_2+c_1))\\
& + \cdots+\sum_{j=n_1+n_2+\cdots+n_{\ell-1}+1}^{n_1+n_2+\cdots+n_{\ell-1}+n_{\ell}}p_j \rho(s_j, (a_{\ell}, m_1a_{\ell}+c_1))\\
&  +\sum_{j=n_1+n_2+\cdots+n_{\ell-1}+n_{\ell}+1}^{n_1+n_2+\cdots+n_{\ell-1}+n_{\ell+1}}p_j \rho(s_j, (a_{\ell+1}, m_2a_{\ell+1}+c_2)) + \cdots\\
&+\sum_{j=n_1+n_2+\cdots+n_{\ell-1}+n_{\ell}+\cdots+n_{\ell+m-1}+1}^{n_1+n_2+\cdots+n_{\ell-1}+n_{\ell+1}+\cdots+n_{\ell+m-1}+n_{\ell+m}}p_j \rho(s_j, (a_{\ell+m}, m_2a_{\ell+m}+c_2)).  
\end{aligned} 
\end{equation} 
\qed
\end{note} 


In the following sections we give the main results of the paper. 

\section{Constrained quantization for a finite uniform discrete distribution} \label{sec1} 
Let $P$ be a finite uniform distribution with support $\set{-3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3}$. Then, the probability mass function $f$ for $P$ is given by
 \[f(j)=P(X : X=j)=\left\{\begin{array}{cc}
 \frac 17,  & \te{ where } j\in \set{-3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3},   \\
 0 & \te{otherwise}.
 \end{array}
 \right.\]
 Comparing with the list $\set{s_1, s_2, \cdots, s_N}$ as defined in Note~\ref{note1}, we have 
 \[s_j=-4+j \te{ for } j\in \set{1, 2, 3, 4, 5,  6, 7}.\]
In the following two subsection we calculate the constrained optimal set of $n$-points for the probability distribution $P$ for all possible values of $n\in \D N$ with respect to two different constraints. 

 \subsection{Constrained quantization when the constraint is a semicircular arc} \label{sub11}
  In this case let us take the constraint $S$ as the upper semicircular arc of the circle $x^2+y^2=9$, i.e.,  
 \[S:=\set{(x, y) : x^2+y^2=9 \te{ and } y\geq 0}.\]
 Notice that $S$ can also be represented by 
 \[S=\set{(3\cos \gq, 3\sin \gq): 0\leq \gq\leq \pi}.\]
We know that an optimal set of one-point always exists. For any $n\geq 2$, since the boundary of the Voronoi regions of any two optimal elements, in this case, passes through the center of the circle, from the geometry, we see that among $n$ Voronoi regions, only two Voronoi regions contain elements from the support of $P$, i.e., only two Voronoi regions have positive probability. Hence, in view of Remark~\ref{rem2} and Remark~\ref{rem3}, the optimal sets of $n$-points exist only for $n=1$ and $n=2$, and they do not exist for any $n\geq 3$. 

We now calculate the optimal sets of one-point and two-points in the following propositions.

\begin{prop1}
	Any element on the circle forms an optimal set of one-point with constrained quantization error $V_1=13$.
\end{prop1}

\begin{proof} Let $(3\cos \gq, 3\sin \gq)$ be an element on the circle. Then, the distortion error for $P$ with respect to the element is given by
	\begin{align*}
		V(P; \set{(3\cos \gq, 3\sin \gq)})=&\sum_{j=-3}^3\frac 17\rho(j, (3\cos \gq, 3\sin \gq))=13 
		\end{align*}
	which does not depend on $\gq$. Hence, any element on the circle forms an optimal set of one-point with constrained quantization error $V_1=13$.
\end{proof}

\begin{prop1} \label{prop110}
	The set $\set{(-3, 0), (3, 0)}$ forms an optimal set of two-points with constrained  quantization error $V_2=\frac {19}{7}$.
\end{prop1}
\begin{proof} Let $\set{(3\cos \gq_1, 3\sin \gq_1), (3\cos \gq_2, 3\sin \gq_2)}$ be an optimal set of two-points, where $0\leq \gq_1<\gq_2\leq \pi$. 
Since the boundary of any two elements on the circle passes through the center of the circle, in an optimal set of two-points, one Voronoi region will contain the left half, and the other Voronoi region will contain the right half of the support of $P$. Notice that the support element $0$ falls on the boundary of the two Voronoi regions. In that case, as mentioned in Remark~\ref{rem1}, we can assume that  $0$ belongs to the Voronoi region of $(3\cos \gq_2, 3\sin \gq_2)$. Then, the distortion error is given by 
	\begin{align*}
		&V(P; \set{(3\cos \gq_1, 3\sin \gq_1), (3\cos \gq_2, 3\sin \gq_2)})\\
&= \sum_{j=-3}^{0}\frac 17\rho(j, (3\cos \gq_2, 3\sin \gq_2))+\sum_{j=1}^{3}\frac 17\rho(j, (3\cos \gq_1, 3\sin \gq_1))\\
&=\frac{1}{7} \left(-36 \cos \gq_1+36 \cos \gq_2+91\right)
		\end{align*}
which is minimum if $\gq_1=0$ and $\gq_2=\pi$, and the minimum value is $\frac {19}{7}$. Thus, the set $\set{(-3, 0), (3, 0)}$ forms an optimal set of two-points with constrained  quantization error $V_2=\frac {19}{7}$.
\end{proof}

