Choose $(n_1, n_2, n_3, n_4)=(1,2,2,2)$. Then, the set $\set{(-3,0),  (-\frac{21}{8},\frac{3 \sqrt{3}}{8}), (\frac{19}{8},\frac{5 \sqrt{3}}{8}), (\frac{23}{8},\frac{\sqrt{3}}{8})}$ forms an optimal set of four-points with constrained quantization error $V_4= \frac{117}{56}$. By \eqref{eq90}, it follows that there are four different optimal sets of four-points. 
 \end{proof}  


\begin{prop1}  
The set $\set{(-3,0),  (-\frac{11}{4},\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}),  (-\frac{5}{2},\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}),(\frac{19}{8},\frac{5 \sqrt{3}}{8}), (\frac{23}{8},\frac{\sqrt{3}}{8})}$ forms an optimal set of five-points with constrained quantization error $V_5= \frac{29}{14}$, and there are seven different optimal sets of five-points
